The main problem with provided JavaScript code snippet is that the initial value for variable "max" is set to be at the index -1 of array "l". In JavaScript the index of an array can't be negative, it begins from 0. Hence, l[-1] is undefined and the wrong initialization happened for the variable max. We need to set it to the first element of the array.

Fixed code: 

```javascript
const maxElement = (l) => {
   let max = l[0];  // initializing max with 1st element of array
   for(let i = 0; i < l.length; i++) {
      if(l[i] > max) {
         max = l[i];
     } 
   }
   return max;
}
```

Time Complexity: O(n), where n is the length of the given list.
