While there’s no direct support for Boo in ASP.NET MVC out of the box in Visual Studio, you can still compile the Boo code and use the output dll in your MVC project. Please note Boo is not as popular as C# and VB.NET for ASP.NET MVC and finding help on issues might be difficult. Here’s a quick setup on how you can do this. 

First, ensure you have the Boo language installed correctly. If you are using .NET framework you can use nuget to install Boo. 

#### Steps to setup:

1) Create your ASP.NET MVC application with C#.

2) Install Boo language compiler if it is not installed. If you are using .NET framework, open the nuget package manager console and use the command `install-package boo` to install.

3) To use any class written in Boo, you'll first have to compile the class into a dll. Create your classes with Boo code and compile it. Use Boo compiler (booc) to compile the Boo code into a dll. Here's an example:

`booc -target:library -out:BooClassLibrary.dll /r:System.Web.Mvc.dll BooClass.cs`

This will create a `BooClassLibrary.dll`.

4) Add this BooClassLibrary.dll to your ASP.NET MVC project by right-clicking the References and choosing Add Reference.

5) Now you can use the types declared in your Boo code within your C# controllers. C# doesn't know or care what language the types were originally written in, as long as they've been compiled to .NET Intermediate Language (IL), it can use them. Here's an example:

```C#
public class HelloWorldController : Controller
{
    public ActionResult Index()
    {
        var booClass = new BooNamespace.BooClass();
        ViewBag.Message = booClass.GetHelloMessage();
        return View();
    }
}
```

In this example, 'BooNamespace' and 'BooClass' are the namespace and class defined in your Boo code.

Unfortunately, there's no way to use Boo for controllers or views themselves as the ASP.NET MVC framework relies on C# or VB.NET for that. 

Please note that ASP.NET MVC can support other languages but they need a framework that compiles those languages into IL code. 

Please note, this is just a workaround to use Boo in ASP.NET MVC. The support for it is not straightforward as such with languages such as C# and VB.NET. Also, debugging might get complex and tracking issues may be difficult.

