```javascript
function isPrime(num) {
    if (num <= 1) {
        return false;
    }
    else if (num == 2) {
        return true;
    } else {
        for(var i = 2; i < num; i++) {
            if(num % i === 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true; 
    }
}

console.log(isPrime(7));  // returns true
console.log(isPrime(10)); // returns false
```
In this function, we first check if the input number is less than or equal to 1, in which case it is not a prime number, so we return false. If the input number is 2, we treat it as a special case because 2 is the only even prime number, so we return true. For any other number, we run a loop from 2 to that number and check if it can be divided evenly by any number in that range, in which case it is not a prime number, so we return false. If the number isn't divided evenly by any number in the range, it is a prime number, so we return true.

Note: Although the above solution works fine, it can be optimized if we loop from 2 to the square root of num, instead of looping from 2 to num. This is because a larger factor of the number must be a multiple of a smaller factor that has already been checked. The time complexity of the above algorithm is O(n), but when improved it can be reduced to O(sqrt(n)). Here is the optimized function:

```javascript
function isPrime(num) {
    if (num <= 1) {
        return false;
    }
    else if (num == 2) {
        return true;
    } else {
        for(var i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
            if(num % i === 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true; 
    }
}
```
Both functions will yield the same result, the second one will just do it faster for large numbers.

