The standard library does not directly provide any out-of-the-box method to perform case-insensitive string comparison without transforming the string. So we need to write our own custom function or rely on third-party libraries.

There are several methods:

1. Use `std::toupper` or `std::tolower` and `std::equal` for ASCII comparison:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <cctype>

bool iequals(const std::string& a, const std::string& b)
{
    return std::equal(a.begin(), a.end(), b.begin(),
        [](char a, char b) {
            return std::toupper(a) == std::toupper(b);
        });
}
```

This method is limited to the ASCII characters in the current locale. Also, it is slightly less efficient as it checks and converts every character in both strings.

2. Using Boost library:

If your project can make use of the Boost library, there are functions for case-insensitive comparison in the Boost.Algorithm library:

```cpp
#include <boost/algorithm/string.hpp>

std::string s1 = "hello";
std::string s2 = "HELLO";
if(boost::iequals(s1, s2))
    std::cout << "s1 and s2 are equal";
```

This method supports Unicode strings and is case-insensitive. However, Boost is not a standard library and it may not be available in some environments.

3. For Windows, use `stricmp` or `_stricmp`:

```cpp
#include <cstring>

bool iequals(const char* a, const char* b)
{
    return stricmp(a, b) == 0;
}
```

The stricmp() function is a Microsoft extension and not standard C++, so it isn't portable. This method does not support Unicode either.

4. For Linux use `strcasecmp`:

```cpp
#include <cstring>

bool iequals(const char* a, const char* b)
{
    return strcasecmp(a, b) == 0;
}
```

This is similar to stricmp(), but strcasecmp() function is a POSIX function, not standard C++, so it's not portable. This does not support Unicode either.

In conclusion, if your project needs to consider Unicode and needs to be portable, the Boost library would be your best choice. If you only need to handle ASCII, the first method is simple and just enough. If you work only in Windows or Linux environments, the stricmp/strcasecmp-based methods may be the most effective solution.

