Java code implementation for bubble sort is shown below:

```java
public class BubbleSortExample {  
    static void bubbleSort(int[] arr) {  
        int n = arr.length;  
        int temp = 0;  
         for(int i=0; i < n; i++){  
                 for(int j=1; j < (n-i); j++){  
                          if(arr[j-1] > arr[j]){  
                                 //swap elements  
                                 temp = arr[j-1];  
                                 arr[j-1] = arr[j];  
                                 arr[j] = temp;  
                         }                          
                 }  
         }  
    }  
    public static void main(String[] args) {  
                int arr[] ={5, 2, 8, 10, 4, 9};  

                System.out.println("Array Before Bubble Sort");  
                for(int i=0; i < arr.length; i++){  
                        System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");  
                }  
                System.out.println();  

                bubbleSort(arr);

                System.out.println("Array After Bubble Sort");  
                for(int i=0; i < arr.length; i++){  
                        System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");  
                }   
        }  
}  
```

Explanation of the Code:

The `bubbleSort()` function loops through each element of the "arr" array. The `i` loop iterates, starting from the first element to the last. The embedded `j` loop also iterates through the array, but it stops one less each time it loops because the `i` loop is performing the sorting.

Inside the `j` loop, it checks if the current element `arr[j-1]` is larger than the next element `arr[j]`. The 'if' statement executes a block of code, if the condition is true. In this case, when the current element's value is greater than the value of the next element, these values switch places: the larger value, `arr[j-1]` is saved in a temporary variable `temp`, and then `arr[j-1]` takes the lower value `arr[j]`, finally `arr[j]` gets the saved higher value from `temp`.

This process repeats for all `j`. Because of the nature of bubble sort, the largest element 'bubbles' up to the correct position. These steps are repeated until the array is sorted, resulting in the list of numbers ordered from smallest to largest.

The `main()` function initializes the array `arr[] ={5, 2, 8, 10, 4, 9};` and then prints the array before and after the bubble sort.

Factorials can produce very large results and thus may not fit in conventional datatypes. To compute factorials and the number of trailing zeros of very large numbers efficiently, we can take advantage of a mathematical observation that the number of trailing zeroes in a factorial is determined by the number of `5x2` pairs present in the number. As the number `2` occurs more frequently than `5`, it suffices to count the number of `5s`.

Here's a solution in PHP that performs a count of `5s` for finding the factorial and the number of trailing zeroes:

```php
<?php

function findTrailingZeroes($n) {
    $count = 0;

    // Keep dividing n by powers of 5 and update count
    for ($i = 5; $n / $i >= 1; $i *= 5) {
        $count += floor($n / $i);
    }

    return $count;
}

function factorial($n) {
    $factorial = 1;

    // Calculate factorial of a number
    for ($i = 1; $i <= $n; $i++) {
