Calculating median without sorting requires to find the k-th smallest number, where k is half the length of the list. There is an algorithm called QuickSelect based on QuickSort which can be used to solve this problem.

Here is the solution below:

```python
def partition(l, low, high):
    pivot = l[high]
    i = low - 1 
    for j in range(low, high):
        if l[j] <= pivot:
            i += 1
            l[i], l[j] = l[j], l[i]

    l[i+1], l[high] = l[high], l[i+1]
    return i + 1

def quickselect(l, k):
    if len(l) == 1:
        return l[0]
    pivot_index = partition(l, 0, len(l) - 1)
    if pivot_index == k:
        return l[pivot_index]
    elif pivot_index > k:
        return quickselect(l[:pivot_index], k)
    else:
        return quickselect(l[pivot_index + 1:], k - pivot_index - 1)

def median(l):
    n = len(l)
    mid1, mid2 = quickselect(l, n // 2), None
    if n % 2 == 0:
        mid2 = quickselect(l, n // 2 - 1)
        return (mid1 + mid2) / 2
    else:
        return mid1

print(median([3, 1, 2, 4, 5]))  # 3
print(median([-10, 4, 6, 1000, 10, 20]))  # 15.0
```
Warning: QuickSelect has an average time complexity of O(n), but it can degrade to O(n^2) time complexity in the worst-case scenario when the selected pivot is the smallest or the largest number every time. To avoid this, you can implement a version of QuickSelect that chooses a random pivot, this would ensure that the average case time complexity is maintained in any run.

