If you don't want to use the in-built Collection.sort() method and want to implement sorting a list of custom objects using Comparators, you might want to consider writing your own version of a sorting algorithm that uses comparator. 

Given below is a simple example of Bubble sort algorithm, which then uses Comparator to compare objects:

```java
public class MyObject {
    private int id;

    public MyObject(int id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    public int getId() {
        return this.id;
    }

    // Comparator
    public static Comparator<MyObject> MyObjectIdComparator
            = new Comparator<MyObject>() {

        public int compare(MyObject myObject1,MyObject myObject2) {

            int id1 = myObject1.getId();
            int id2 = myObject2.getId();

            // For descending order.
            return id2 - id1;
        }
    };

    // Sorting algorithm
    public static void sort(List<MyObject> list, Comparator<? super MyObject> comparator) {
        for (int i = 0; i < list.size() - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < list.size() - 1 - i; j++) {
                if (comparator.compare(list.get(j), list.get(j + 1)) > 0) {
                    MyObject temp = list.get(j);
                    list.set(j, list.get(j + 1));
                    list.set(j + 1, temp);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<MyObject> myObjects = new ArrayList<>();
        myObjects.add(new MyObject(1));
        myObjects.add(new MyObject(3));
        myObjects.add(new MyObject(2));

        sort(myObjects, MyObjectIdComparator);

        for (MyObject myObject : myObjects) {
            System.out.println(myObject.getId());
        }
    }
}
```

The `sort` method in `MyObject` class basically implement the Bubble Sort algorithm. The algorithm works in multiple passes. In each pass, it compares adjacent elements and swap them if they are in incorrect orders. This process continues to the end of the list. In each pass, the largest element bubbles up to its correct location. Hence the name, Bubble Sort. 

Note that the Bubble Sort algorithm provided above is not efficient, there are other more efficient sorting algorithms such as Quicksort, Mergesort, Heapsort etc. that allow you to compare elements using comparator. The idea to use comparator in these sorting algorithms remains the same. However, these algorithms are more complex to implement. 

The `MyObjectIdComparator` Comparator descended sorted by the `id` attribute.

