A Windows service is simply an application that runs in the background and doesn't interact directly with the user interface. You can create a service by writing a program that adheres to a particular pattern prescribed by the Service Control Manager (SCM).

Here's a basic outline of how to write a Windows service in C:

1. Include the necessary headers:
```c
#include  <windows.h>
```

2. Provide a service entry point:
```c
VOID WINAPI service_main(DWORD dwArgc, LPTSTR *lpszArgv);
```
This function is the main entry point for your service. It is called by the SCM.

3. Provide a control handler function:
```c
VOID WINAPI service_ctrl(DWORD dwControl);
```
This function handles commands from the SCM, such as stop, pause, continue, and so on.

4. In your main function, call StartServiceCtrlDispatcher with a SERVICE_TABLE_ENTRY that references your service_main:
```c
int main(void) {
    SERVICE_TABLE_ENTRY serviceTable[] = {
        { "MyService", service_main },
        { NULL, NULL },
    };
​
    StartServiceCtrlDispatcher(serviceTable);
    return 0;
}
```
5. In your service_main function, register your service_ctrl with RegisterServiceCtrlHandler, and inform the SCM that your service is running with SetServiceStatus:
```c
VOID WINAPI service_main(DWORD dwArgc, LPTSTR *lpszArgv) {
    SERVICE_STATUS_HANDLE hServiceStatusHandle;
    SERVICE_STATUS serviceStatus;
​
    hServiceStatusHandle = RegisterServiceCtrlHandler("MyService", service_ctrl);

    if (hServiceStatusHandle)
    {
        memset(&serviceStatus, 0, sizeof(serviceStatus));
        serviceStatus.dwServiceType = SERVICE_WIN32_OWN_PROCESS;
        serviceStatus.dwCurrentState = SERVICE_RUNNING;
        serviceStatus.dwControlsAccepted = SERVICE_ACCEPT_STOP;
        SetServiceStatus(hServiceStatusHandle, &serviceStatus);
   }
}
```
6. In your service_ctrl function, handle the stop command, and inform the SCM that your service has stopped with SetServiceStatus:
```c
VOID WINAPI service_ctrl(DWORD dwControl) {
    SERVICE_STATUS serviceStatus;

    if(dwControl == SERVICE_CONTROL_STOP) 
    {
      serviceStatus.dwCurrentState = SERVICE_STOPPED;
      SetServiceStatus(hServiceStatusHandle, &serviceStatus);
    }
}
```
The whole point of a service is to run code in the background. You usually perform your actual work in a separate thread started from your service_main function, and your service_ctrl function stops that thread when the SCM orders your service to stop.

Note: To install a service, you can use the `sc` command prompt utility on Windows or you can use an API function named `CreateService`.

