In order to organize the array in ascending order, one viable solution can be to use the "Sort" function available in the standard C++ library. Time complexity of this sort function in most implementations is generally O(nlogn). To determine whether or not the sorting algorithm is stable, it would require analytical reasoning.

Stable sorting maintains the relative order of records with equal keys. In other words, if two elements in the unsorted list are equal, and one appears before the other, then their order will be maintained in the sorted list.

std::sort is not a stable sort, instead std::stable_sort can be used which has the time complexity of O(nlog^2n).

This is how you can accomplish above with C++:

```c++
#include<algorithm>
#include<iostream>
#include<vector>

// Function to sort array
std::vector<int> sort_arr(std::vector<int> unsorted_arr)
{
    std::sort(unsorted_arr.begin(), unsorted_arr.end());
    return unsorted_arr;
}

// Function to check if sort is stable or not
bool is_stable(std::vector<int> unsorted_arr, std::vector<int> sorted_arr)
{
    std::map<int, int> positions;
    for(int i = 0; i < unsorted_arr.size(); i++)
    {
        if(positions.find(unsorted_arr[i]) != positions.end())
        {
            if(positions[unsorted_arr[i]] > i)
                return false;
        }
        positions[unsorted_arr[i]] = i;
    }

    for(int i = 0; i < sorted_arr.size()-1; i++)
    {
        if(sorted_arr[i] == sorted_arr[i+1])
            if(positions[sorted_arr[i]] > positions[sorted_arr[i+1]])
                return false;
    }
    return true;
}

int main()
{
    std::vector<int> unsorted_arr = {45, -21, 12, 56, 7, -47, 34};
    std::vector<int> sorted_arr = sort_arr(unsorted_arr);
    std::cout<<"Sorted array: ";
    for (int i=0; i<sorted_arr.size(); i++)
       std::cout << sorted_arr[i] << " ";
    std::cout<<"\nSort is stable: "<<std::boolalpha<<is_stable(unsorted_arr, sorted_arr)<<"\n";
    return 0;
}
```
Remember, the function is_stable will return false for std::sort as it's not a stable sort. The logic inside is_stable function tries to check if the relative order of equal elements is preserved or not.

The question basically asks us to build a Regular Expression which can handle short form and long form hexadecimal color codes, and can also correlate short form to their long form. 

Short form hexadecimal color codes are of the form `#abc` and their long form is `#aabbcc`, where 'a', 'b' and 'c' are single hexadecimal digits (0-9, a-f or A-F). 

We would need a function to convert a short form hex color code to a long form hex color code, and vice-versa.

Here's the Python code that handles these:

```python
import re

# Regular expressions for short and long notation
short_form = re.compile(r'^#([a-fA-F0-9]{3}){1}$')
full_form = re.compile(r'^#([a-fA-F0-9]{6}){1}$')

def short_to_full(code):
    """ Convert short form hex code to long form. """
    return "#" + "".join([c+c for c in code[1:]])

def full_to_short(code):
    """ Convert long form hex code to short form. """
    return "#" + "".join([code[i] for i in range(1, len(code), 2)])

# Testing
tests = ["#abc", "#AaBbCc", "#ABC", "#000", "#000000", "#fff", 
         "#FFF", "#111111", "#abcd", "abc", "#1234567", "#ghj"]

for test in tests:
    if short_form.match(test):
        print(f"{test} is valid in short form notation, its long form is {short_to_full(test)}")
    elif full_form.match(test):
        full = test.lower()
        if full_to_short(full) == short_to_full(full_to_short(full)):
            print(f"{test} is valid in long form notation, its short form is {full_to_short(test)}")
        else:
            print(f"{test} is invalid")
    else:
        print(f"{test} is invalid")
```

This script first checks if the given hexadecimal color code matches the short form regex, if it does, it will convert it to its long form. If it doesn't, then it checks if it matches the full form regex. If it does, it converts it to the short form. If it doesn't match either, it prints that the code is invalid. 

Please note that this script doesn't handle the cases where input color codes have characters other than hexadecimal.

