Creating custom attributes in C# is fairly straightforward, but there's a crucial point you need to understand - attributes by themselves don't DO anything. They're essentially metadata that you attach to some piece of code (assembly, class, method, property, etc), which can then be retrieved via reflection at runtime.

So for attributes to achieve anything, some other part of the code needs to be responsible for checking for the presence of the attribute and acting on it. The compiler or the runtime won't do this for you, except for a few very special cases (like the `[Obsolete]` attribute, which the compiler treats specially).

Notably, creating an attribute that breaks the build at compile time when used is not possible, because attributes don't affect the compilation process in this way.

That said, here's an example of how to create an attribute:

```csharp
[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Method, AllowMultiple = false)->]
public class BreakBuildAttribute : Attribute
{
  public BreakBuildAttribute(string message)
  {
    this.Message = message;
  }

  public string Message { get; private set; }
}
```

