Defining the problem correctly, every node will have an additional attribute, size, which represents the number of descendants including the node itself. The size of every node can be maintained by updating these values during standard Red-Black tree operations like addition or deletion, which will not affect the time complexity of these operations.

Considering this, we can address the problem using both iterative and recursive techniques as follows:

Iterative method:
```
Node findKthLargest(Node root, int k)
{
    int leftSize = root.left != null ? root.left.size : 0;

    while (root != null)
    {
        if (leftSize + 1 == k) 
            return root;
        else if (k <= leftSize)
            root = root.left;
        else 
        {
            k = k - (leftSize + 1);
            root = root.right;
        }
        leftSize = root && root.left ? root.left.size : 0;
    }
    return null;
}
```
Recursive method:
```
Node findKthLargestRecursive(Node root, int k)
{
    if (root == null)
        return null;
    int leftSize = (root.left != null) ? root.left.size : 0;
    if (k == leftSize + 1) 
        return root;
    if (k <= leftSize) 
        return findKthLargestRecursive(root.left, k); // If k is less than the number of nodes in the left-subtree then, search in left subtree.
    else 
        return findKthLargestRecursive(root.right, k-leftSize-1); // Else, search in right subtree.
}
```
With some additional storage overhead, these techniques allow for obtaining the kth node operation in an efficient O(log n) time complexity manner.

It is essential to mention that the solution assumes that all elements in the Red-Black tree are different. It would require modifications to deal with equal elements or for finding the kth smallest element.

For handling unconventional inputs, the functions should be modified to handle cases where k is negative, or k is greater than the total nodes in the tree.
The root node passed to the function should not be null (in case of an empty tree).

