        graph = collections.defaultdict(list)
        def dfs(node, par = None):
            if node:
                graph[node].append(par)
                graph[par].append(node)
                dfs(node.left, node)
                dfs(node.right, node)

        dfs(root)
        stack = [node for node,_ in graph.items() if node and node.val == k]
        seen = set(stack)

        while stack:
            node = stack.pop()
            if node:
                if len(graph[node]) <= 1:
                    return node.val
                for nei in graph[node]:
                    if nei not in seen:
                        seen.add(nei)
                        stack.append(nei)
        return -1
```

This solution may return incorrect results as DFS does not guarantee that the first leaf node found is the closest one. Indeed, DFS could potentially traverse the whole height of the tree or a longer path before encountering a leaf. Therefore, the BFS approach should be used to guarantee that the closest leaf node is found first.

