The provided code sorts an array using Bubble Sort. This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n^2), which can be expensive for larger datasets.

For optimization, we can use a more efficient sorting algorithm such as Merge Sort, Heap Sort or Quick Sort. Below is an implementation of Quick Sort:

```Java
public class QuickSort {
    static int partition(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        int pivot = arr[high];
        int i = (low - 1);

        for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < pivot) {
                i++;

                // swap arr[i] and arr[j]
                int temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[j];
                arr[j] = temp;
            }
        }

        // swap arr[i+1] and arr[high] (or pivot)
        int temp = arr[i+1];
        arr[i+1] = arr[high];
        arr[high] = temp;

        return i+1;
    }

    static void quickSort(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            int pi = partition(arr, low, high);

            quickSort(arr, low, pi - 1);
            quickSort(arr, pi + 1, high);
        }
    }

    public static void sortArray(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;

        quickSort(arr, 0, n-1);
    }
}
```
Please note that the best case time complexity for Quick Sort is O(n log n) and the worst case (rare) can be O(n^2) if the pivot element is not chosen wisely. In most cases, it performs better than Bubble Sort. However, the best choice of sorting algorithm could depend on several factors like the size of the dataset, time complexity constraints etc.

