Next, you need to tell MVC how to route requests to your nested controller. This is done in your `RouteConfig.cs` file:

```csharp
public static void RegisterRoutes(RouteCollection routes)
{
    routes.IgnoreRoute("{resource}.axd/{*pathInfo}");

    routes.MapRoute(
        name: "Default",
        url: "{controller}/{action}/{id}",
        defaults: new { controller = "Home", action = "Index", id = UrlParameter.Optional },
        namespaces: new[] { "YourAppName.Controllers" }
    );

    // a route for supplies controller
    routes.MapRoute(
        name: "Supplies",
        url: "Warehouse/{controller}/{action}/{id}",
        defaults: new { action = "Index", id = UrlParameter.Optional },
        namespaces: new[] { "YourAppName.Controllers.Warehouse.Supplies" }
    );
}
```

The key here is the "namespaces" property in the route definition, where you define the full namespace of your nested controller(s), making sure to match the directory and namespace structure.

Note: The order of routes matters. ASP.NET MVC uses the first route that matches the incoming request, so make sure to order your routes from the most specific to the most general.

