Yes, it is possible to create a single .exe file that encapsulates your entire application, including all dependent libraries. This is done by embedding the required .dll files in the main executable and loading them on demand at runtime.

Here's a general approach:

1. Store your dependent assemblies (.dll files) as embedded resources in your .NET application project. This can be done in the "Add Existing Item" section of Visual Studio. 

2. Some changes will be needed in the application startup code (Program.cs file, for instance). You need to add an AssemblyResolve event to current AppDomain when your application starts. This event will be raised every time your application needs a .dll that hasn’t been loaded yet.

3. In the AssemblyResolve event handler, you can determine if the assembly is one of your dependencies (by comparing the name), and if so, you can load it from the embedded resources.

Here's some pseudo C# code as an example:
```csharp
 AppDomain.CurrentDomain.AssemblyResolve += (sender, args) =>
{
    String resourceName = new AssemblyName(args.Name).Name + ".dll";
    var assembly = Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly();
    using (var stream = assembly.GetManifestResourceStream(resourceName))
    {
        Byte[] assemblyData = new Byte[stream.Length];
        stream.Read(assemblyData, 0, assemblyData.Length);
        return Assembly.Load(assemblyData);
    }  
};
```
This should allow you to deploy your application as a single .exe file. 

Keep in mind, however, that this can make it harder to update individual components of the application later, as you’ll need to replace the entire binary. It also doesn't work with native (C++) libraries, since they are not loaded as .NET Assemblies.

Additionally, this approach does not always comply with the licenses of third-party libraries, you should make sure you are not violating any terms.

