To find the greatest common divisor of two given numbers we are going to use the Euclidean algorithm. To cover the scenario in which we have more than two numbers we're going to find the gcd between the first two numbers and then find the gcd between the result and the next number and so on, until we go through each of the numbers in the array.

The implementation in java will be:

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class GCD {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {60, 24, 18};

        System.out.println("GCD: " + findGCD(numbers));
    }

    //Implement the Euclidean algorithm to find gcd of two numbers
    public static int findGCD(int a, int b) {
        if (b == 0)
            return a;
        return findGCD(b, a % b);
    }

    //Find gcd of array of numbers
    public static int findGCD(int[] numbers) {
        int result = numbers[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < numbers.length; i++) {
            result = findGCD(result, numbers[i]);

            if(result == 1) {
               return 1; 
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```
In the findGCD method that deals with arrays of numbers, the gcd is updated for each index of the numbers in the array. We also include a quick exit strategy for the case when gcd becomes 1 (as gcd cannot be less than 1, thereby saving computation for the remaining elements). 

The time complexity of this solution is O(n) in the worst-case, where n is the number of elements in the array, which makes it an efficient solution.

Below is an Objective-C function that can be used to construct a ternary tree using depth-first search (DFS). This DFS approach is implemented using recursion.

```objc
#import <Foundation/Foundation.h>

@interface Node : NSObject
@property (nonatomic, strong) Node *left;
@property (nonatomic, strong) Node *middle;
@property (nonatomic, strong) Node *right;
@property (nonatomic, assign) int value;
-(instancetype)initWithValue:(int)value;
@end

@implementation Node
-(instancetype)initWithValue:(int)value
{
    self = [super init];
    if (self) {
        self.value = value;
    }
    return self;
}
@end

@interface TernaryTree : NSObject
@property (nonatomic, strong) Node *root;
-(void)insertValue:(int)value;
@end

@implementation TernaryTree

-(void)insertValue:(int)value {
    self.root = [self insertNode:self.root value:value];
}

-(Node *)insertNode:(Node *)node value:(int)value {
    if (node == nil) {
        node = [[Node alloc] initWithValue:value];
    } else if (value < node.value) {
        node.left = [self insertNode:node.left value:value];
    } else if (value > node.value) {
        node.right = [self insertNode:node.right value:value];
    } else {
        if (node.middle == nil) {
            node.middle = [[Node alloc] initWithValue:value];
        } else {
            node.middle = [self insertNode:node.middle value:value];
        }
    }
    return node;
}
@end
```
This code defines a class `Node` for each node in the ternary tree. Each node has a left, middle, and right child, and it holds a value.

Another class `TernaryTree` is defined for the ternary tree itself. This class has a root node and a function to insert a value into the tree. The insertion process traverses the tree in a depth-first manner: if the value being inserted is less than the value of the current node, the function delves into the left subtree, and if it's greater, it delves into the right subtree. If the value is equal to the value of the current node, it delves into the middle subtree. 

To use this code, you can create an object of the `TernaryTree` class and then call the "insertValue" function for each of the given numbers in the order you want. Please notice that the structure of the created ternary tree strongly depends on the order of the numbers.

