Here is a Java method implementation to find the largest prime factor of a number:

```java
public int largestPrimeFactorSieve(int n) {
    int largest = -1;

    // Divide n by 2 until n becomes odd
    while (n % 2 == 0) {
        largest = 2;
        n /= 2;
    }

    // n is odd at this point so find odd factor of n
    for (int i = 3; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i += 2) {
        while (n % i == 0) {
            largest = i;
            n /= i;
        }
    }

    // This condition is to handle the case when n is a prime number
    // greater than 2
    if (n > 2)
        largest = n;

    return largest;
}
```
This method is a typical implementation of the Sieve of Eratosthenes to find prime numbers, but it also keeps track of the largest prime number that divides `n` evenly. The sieve works by successively dividing `n` by each prime number starting from 2, then continues with the next odd number (to skip even numbers), up until the square root of `n`. Each time `n` is divided evenly, the divisor is stored as the largest prime number. If after this process `n` is still greater than 2, then `n` itself is a prime number and it will be the largest prime divisor. 

For instance, in the example `largestPrimeFactorSieve(13195)`, the method will divide 13195 by 2, 3, 5, 7, ... After the loop, `n` becomes 29 which is a prime number, so 29 is returned as the largest prime factor.

