Here is a simple algorithm in Java for generating random passwords of length 10:

```java
import java.security.SecureRandom;

public class Main{
    static final String CHARACTERS = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789~!@#$%^&*()_+=-";

    public static void main(String args[]) {
        System.out.println(generatePassword(10));
    }

    public static String generatePassword(int length) {
        SecureRandom rnd = new SecureRandom();
        StringBuilder pass = new StringBuilder(length);

        for(int i = 0; i < length; i++) {
            pass.append(CHARACTERS.charAt(rnd.nextInt(CHARACTERS.length())));
        }

        return pass.toString();
    }
}
```

This algorithm uses the `SecureRandom` class to generate a new random number each iteration. This number is then used to get a random character from `CHARACTERS` string which includes uppercase and lowercase letters, digits and some special characters. The random character is appended to `pass` string until the length of `pass` is equal to the input length.

Here are two functions in MATLAB for generating the first N Fibonacci series numbers, and computing their average.

```MATLAB
function fib = fibonacci(N)
    % Function to generate the first N Fibonacci series numbers
    fib = zeros(1, N);
    fib(1) = 0;
    if N > 1
        fib(2) = 1;
    end
    for ii = 3:N
        fib(ii) = fib(ii-1) + fib(ii-2);
    end
end

function avg = fib_average(N)
    % Function to compute the average of the N Fibonacci numbers
    fib_nums = fibonacci(N);
    avg = sum(fib_nums) / N;
end
```
In the function "fibonacci", it initially creates an array of N numbers with all zero. Then it assigns the first two Fibonacci series numbers 0 and 1 (if N > 1). Then it calculates the rest of the Fibonacci series numbers by adding the last two numbers in the series.

In the function "fib_average", it first calls the "fibonacci" function to get the first N Fibonacci series numbers and then calculates the average of these N numbers.

These functions use a dynamic programming technique for calculating Fibonacci series which prevents calculating the same sub-problem multiple times and this gives a time complexity of O(N). Also, they use an array to store the first N Fibonacci numbers which provide space complexity of O(N).

