The issue you're experiencing comes from your misuse of getenv. The function getenv takes a string as a parameter, usually the name of an environmental variable you want the value of.

You're currently passing it a full envp string (for example "USER=root"), which is not a valid environmental variable name. Instead it should be used like this: getenv("USER").

Here's how you would extract the variable name from an envp entry and then get its value with getenv:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char **argv, char **envp) {
    char *aux;
    //Split the env string into name and value
    char* name = strtok(envp[0], "=");
    char* value = strtok(NULL, "=");

    if (name == NULL || value == NULL) {
        printf("Invalid env string");
        return 0;
    }

    printf("Env name: %s\n", name);
    printf("Env value: %s\n", value);

    aux = getenv(name);
    if (aux != NULL) {
        printf("Value from getenv: %s\n", aux);
    } else {
        printf("NULL\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Note: strtok modifies its string argument, if you need to preserve envp[0], you should make a copy before tokenizing it. Also, strtok is not thread safe, if thread safety is a concern for you consider using strtok_r instead.

