Creating a steganography algorithm adhering to such specific conditions requires a comprehensive understanding of Java, XML and steganography techniques, particularly the Least Significant Bit (LSB) technique.

Here's a streamlined approach to build this system:

```java

import java.awt.image.BufferedImage;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import javax.imageio.ImageIO;

public class LSBSteganography {
	private static final String STEG_IMAGE_FILE = "steg.png";

	private static int[] toBin(int i) {
		int[] result = new int[8];
		for (int j = 0; j < 8; j++) {
			result[j] = i%2;
			i = i/2;
		}
		return result;
	}

	private static int toDec(int[] bin) {
		int result = 0;
		int base = 1;
		for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
			result += base*bin[i];
			base = base*2;
		}
		return result;
	}

	public static File hideMessage(File originalImage, String message, String type) throws IOException {
		BufferedImage img = ImageIO.read(originalImage);

		String xml_start = "<msg>";
		String xml_end = "</msg>";
		message = xml_start+message+xml_end;

		byte[] binaryMessage = message.getBytes();

		int msgPointer = 0, bitPointer = 0;
		boolean finish = false;

		for (int x = 0; x < img.getWidth(); x++) {
			for (int y = 0; y < img.getHeight(); y++) {
				if (finish) break;

				int pixel = img.getRGB(x, y);
				int alpha = (pixel >> 24) & 0xff;
				int red = (pixel >> 16) & 0xff;
				int green = (pixel >> 8) & 0xff;
				int blue = pixel & 0xff;

				if (msgPointer < binaryMessage.length) {					
					int[] bin = toBin((int)binaryMessage[msgPointer]);

					red = (red - red%2) + bin[bitPointer++];

					if (bitPointer >= 8) {
						bitPointer = 0;
						msgPointer++;
					}
				} else {
					finish = true;  
				}

				pixel = (alpha<<24) | (red<<16) | (green<<8) | blue;
				img.setRGB(x, y,pixel);

			}
		}	

		File steg_image = new File(STEG_IMAGE_FILE);
		ImageIO.write(img, type, steg_image);
		return steg_image;

	}

	public static String retrieveMessage(File steg_image) throws IOException {
		BufferedImage img = ImageIO.read(steg_image);

		int[] bin = new int[8];
		int msgPointer = 0, bitPointer = 0;
		String message = "";
		boolean start = false;

		for (int x = 0; x < img.getWidth(); x++) {
			for (int y = 0; y < img.getHeight(); y++) {

				int pixel = img.getRGB(x, y);
				int red = (pixel >> 16) & 0xff;

				bin[bitPointer++] = red%2;

				if (bitPointer >= 8) {					
					bitPointer = 0;
					byte b = (byte)toDec(bin);
					String c = new String(new byte[]{b});

					if (c.equals("<")) start = true;
					if (start) message += c;
					if (c.equals(">")) {
						if (message.contains("</msg>")) return message.substring(5, message.length()-6);
					} 
				}

			}
		}	

		return message;

	}

}```

The Java program adopts the LSB steganography technique and embeds an XML message into a still image. It utilizes `BufferedImage` and `ImageIO` to read and write image files and manipulate color channels. A bytewise version of the message is inserted into the least significant bit of the red color channel, building an XML data structure. 

`hideMessage()` generates the steganographic image while `retrieveMessage()` fetches the hidden message from the output image of `hideMessage()`. Whenever an upgrade is required, simple modification in the associated function is enough. This design models are aimed at providing high adaptability to future updates, modifications, threat detection, and technology advances.

