Here's a way to solve this problem using dynamic programming. First, we create a 2D table (dp[][]) where each cell of the table represents whether the substring of the input string, starting at index i and ending at index j, is palindromic or not. After filling up the table, we can find all the palindromic sub-sequences by checking each cell of the table.

```python
def all_palindrome_subsequence(input_string):
    n = len(input_string)
    rev = input_string[::-1]

    dp = [[0 for x in range(n + 1)] 
             for y in range(n + 1)]

    # mark all characters as a sub-sequence
    for i in range(n + 1):
        for j in range(n + 1):
            if (i == 0 or j == 0):
                dp[i][j] = 0
            elif (input_string[i - 1] == 
                  rev[j - 1]):
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
            else:
                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j],
                               dp[i][j - 1])

    # recover all sub-sequences
    index = dp[n][n]
    lps = ['0']*index
    i = n
    j = n

    while (i > 0 and j > 0):
        if (input_string[i - 1] == rev[j - 1]):
            lps[index - 1] = (input_string[i - 1])
            i -= 1
            j -= 1
            index -= 1
        elif (dp[i - 1][j] > dp[i][j - 1]):
            i -= 1
        else:
            j -= 1

    palindromes = set()

    # find palindromic sub-sequences
    for string in lps:
        for i in range(len(string)):
            for j in range(i + 2, len(string) + 1):
                if string[i:j] == string[i:j][::-1]:
                    palindromes.add(string[i:j])

    return palindromes

input_string = "abacb"
print(all_palindrome_subsequence(input_string))
```

In the code above, "all_palindrome_subsequence" is the function that takes a string as its parameter, and returns all unique palindromic sub-sequences present in that string, excluding single-letter sub-sequences. "lps" is the string array that contains the longest palindromic sub-sequence (which will be used to extract all palindromic sub-sequences), "dp[][]" is the table that gets filled based on whether sub-strings of the input string are palindromic or not, and the "palindromes" set contains all the unique palindromic sub-sequences. At the end, the function returns this set.

