Here is a simple program in Java to determine if two strings are anagrams of each other. 

As a reminder, two strings are anagrams if they contain the exact same characters in the same quantities. For example, "listen" and "silent" are anagrams because they both contain one 'l', one 'i', one 's', one 'e', one 'n', and one 't'. 

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    static boolean areAnagram(String str1, String str2) {
        // Check if both strings lengths are not same return false
        if (str1.length() != str2.length())
            return false;

        // Convert both strings into characters
        char [] charStr1 = str1.toCharArray();
        char [] charStr2 = str2.toCharArray();

        // Sort both strings
        Arrays.sort(charStr1);
        Arrays.sort(charStr2);

        // Compare sorted strings
        for (int i = 0; i < charStr1.length; i++)
            if (charStr1[i] != charStr2[i])
                return false;

        // If all characters match, then return true
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String args[]) {
        String str1 = "listen";
        String str2 = "silent";

        if (areAnagram(str1, str2))
            System.out.println("The two strings are anagram of each other");
        else
            System.out.println("The two strings are not anagram of each other");
    }
}
``` 

In this program, the function `areAnagram` checks if two input strings are anagrams. This is done by first verifying that the strings are the same length. If they aren't, they can't be anagrams, so the function returns false. If the strings are the same length, they are each converted to a character array and sorted. The sorted arrays are compared character by character, and if any characters do not match, the function returns false. If all characters match, the function returns true, and the strings are anagrams. The `main` method calls `areAnagram` to check if "listen" and "silent" are anagrams and prints the result.

