Sentry does not directly allow you to change the environment dynamically at runtime. Internally, Sentry indeed calls the `SentryFlutter.init()` when your app starts and uses that information across your app.

However, you may use tags to log details dynamically. You could have a tag for your environments and update that at runtime. This is a common way to handle runtime changes. Here is an example:

```dart
import 'package:sentry_flutter/sentry_flutter.dart';

String currentEnvironment = "production"; // change this value dynamically

Future<void> reportError(dynamic error, dynamic stackTrace) async { 
  await SentryFlutter.captureException(
    error,
    stackTrace: stackTrace,
    withScope: (scope) {
      scope.setTag("environment", currentEnvironment);
    },
 );
}

void main() async {
  await SentryFlutter.init(
    (options) {
      options.dsn = 'https://xxxxx.sentry.io';

      // Add default tag for errors captured outside `reportError`
      options.addTag("environment", currentEnvironment);
    },
    appRunner: () => runApp(MyApp()),
  );
}
```

This way, all your sentry issues will have an environment tag that you could filter on the Sentry dashboard. Changing the value of `currentEnvironment` will reflect on the new errors you log to Sentry.

