In the given problem, we have to check if we can right shift the array to make it non-decreasing and also return the minimum number of shifts if possible and -1 if not possible. 

The way to approach this problem is to find the breaking point, i.e., if the array was already rotated, then there should be one point in the array where the next number is lesser than the current number. Once we find this breaking point, we can determine the number of shifts as array length - index of this breaking point.

Here is the refactored code for this problem.

```javascript
const sortArrayCheck = (arr) => {

    let len = arr.length;
    if (len === 0) return true;

    let breakPoint = -1;

    for (let i = 0; i < len - 1; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
            if (breakPoint != -1) return [-1];
            breakPoint = i;
        }
    }

    // if break point not found, array is already sorted
    if (breakPoint == -1) return [true, 0];

    if (arr[len - 1] <= arr[0]) return [true, len - (breakPoint + 1)];

    return [-1];
}
```

In the above code, the function `sortArrayCheck` first checks if the array length is 0 and returns true if it is. We then loop through the array looking for the point where a number is larger than the next one (the break point). If we find more than one such point, we return -1 because we can't sort the array with right shifts. 

If only one break point is found or no such point is found, we check if the last number in the array is less than or equal to the first one. If it is, then we return the number of shifts required as array length minus the break point index. Otherwise, we return -1.

