The issue is that React `useState` update function (`setState` here) is not immediately updating the actual state value. It will be updated on the next re-render.

If you want to log the updated state value immediately, there are different ways to achieve this:

1. By using a so-called "effect":

You can use the useEffect hook to 'listen' for changes to state, which can then allow you to console.log and even do much more when the state updates. See this:

```javascript
function Test() {
  const [state, setState] = React.useState(0);

  const handler = () => {
    setState(1);
  }

  React.useEffect(()=>{
    console.log(state);
  }, [state])

  return (
    <div>
      <div>My state: {state}</div>
      <button onClick={handler}>Click</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

`useEffect` will run after every render. By passing `[state]` as the second argument, we are telling React to skip applying an effect if certain values (in this case `state`) have not changed between re-renders.

2. By using a callback function inside setState function:

There is ability to pass a callback function into the `setState` method. But it's not like the `this.setState` in class components, where you get the previous state as a parameter. In functional components `setState` does not accept callback function as a second argument. But you can use it as first argument:

```javascript
function Test() {
  const [state, setState] = React.useState(0);

  const handler = () => {
    setState(prevState => {
      const newState = prevState + 1;
      console.log(newState);
      return newState;
    });
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <div>My state: {state}</div>
      <button onClick={handler}>Click</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

React `useState` updater function `setState` accepts a callback function. This function will have access to the previous state value.
In this case, within `setState`, you increment the previous state and then log it. Since you are logging the state before `return` thus you will log when the state actually changes.

