Transactions in MongoDB are session dependent, meaning the same session object must be used for the complete cycle - start, perform operations, commit / abort.

You're apparently doing things right from a traditional standpoint, but there is a catch with Mongoose (an ODM for MongoDB).

If you're using Mongoose, Mongoose buffers all the commands until it gets a connection. This implies, if you're creating MongoDB transactions with Mongoose models the way you are doing, you should note that everything inside a transaction should be an async/await method call with `{ session }` option passed everywhere. But if there's anything that doesn't follow this rule, the sequence of operation execution gets jumbled because Mongoose opens up a new session instead of utilizing the one provided.

In your example, the issue could be the `IssueModel.create([req.body], { session: session })` and `IssueHistoryModel.create([req.body], { session: session })` commands are getting queued up to run when a connection becomes available, but the error is thrown immediately and hence doesn't roll back the operations because it's not technically within the same session. 

Here's a fixed version of your code:

```js
const session = await dbConn.startSession();
session.startTransaction();
let issue;

try {
    issue = await IssueModel.create([req.body], { session: session });
    await IssueHistoryModel.create([req.body], { session: session });
    await session.commitTransaction();      // Commit the Transaction
    session.endSession();                   // End the session
} catch(error) {
    await session.abortTransaction();       // Rollback the Transaction in case of an error
    session.endSession();
    console.log('Something went wrong!', error);
    throw error;
}
```

Now, whenever an error is encountered within the `try` body, it will jump to `catch` block, abort the transaction and close the session. This will make sure all operations within the transaction are atomic and will rollback in case of an error. Hope this helps.

Also, remember to use MongoDB v4.0+ and Mongoose 5.2.0+ for the above snippet to work as they introduced support for transactions.

