C language doesn't have a built-in Unicode support, you need to use a library or manually parse the Unicode.

But a quick solution, if it fits your needs, would be:

1. Change your file encoding to UTF-8 without BOM (Byte Order Mark). You can do this with many text editors like Notepad++ or Sublime Text. This will save your accented characters as multi-byte UTF-8 characters.

2. Use `fgetc` function instead of `fgets`. `fgetc` returns a single character from file. If you have a multi-byte UTF-8 character, it will still return it byte by byte. You need to store those bytes and process them as a single character. Here is how to do it:

```c
FILE * file = fopen("file.txt", "r");
char c;
while ((c = fgetc(file)) != EOF) {
    if (c & 0x80) { // This is a multi-byte UTF-8 character
      int count = 0;

      // count the number of bytes the character has
      for (int i = 0; i < 8; i ++) {
         if ((c >> (7 - i)) & 1) count ++;
         else break;
      }

      // store all the bytes of the character in a string
      char utf8Char[5] = {0};
      utf8Char[0] = c;
      for(int i = 1; i < count; i++) {
         if((c = fgetc(file)) == EOF) {
            // handle EOF here
            // for example set c to 0
            c = 0;
         } else {
            utf8Char[i] = c;
         }
      }

      // utf8Char now contains a single UTF-8 character
      // you can use it in your code
      // for example print it
      printf("%s", utf8Char);
    } else {
      printf("%c", c);
    }
}
fclose(file);
```
This code reads the file byte by byte and when it encounters a multi-byte UTF-8 character, it reads all its bytes and stores them in a string. Now you can use this string in your code as a single character.

Keep in mind that this works only for UTF-8 files. If your file uses another Unicode encoding, you'll need a Unicode library which has its own functions for reading and handling files.

