You can use dynamic programming to solve this problem. The idea is to create a table that stores the length of the longest palindromic subsequence for every possible substring. Then, you can also create another table to store the count of such longest subsequences. Here is a Python solution:

```python
def longest_palindromic_subsequence(s):
    n = len(s)
    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]
    count = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]

    for i in range(n-1, -1, -1):
        dp[i][i] = 1
        count[i][i] = 1
        for j in range(i+1, n):
            if s[i] == s[j]:
                dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i+1][j-1]
                count[i][j] = count[i+1][j-1]
                if dp[i][j] == 2:
                    count[i][j] += 1
            else:
                if dp[i+1][j] > dp[i][j-1]:
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i+1][j]
                    count[i][j] = count[i+1][j]
                elif dp[i+1][j] < dp[i][j-1]:
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-1]
                    count[i][j] = count[i][j-1]
                else:
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i+1][j]
                    count[i][j] = count[i+1][j] + count[i][j-1]
                    if dp[i+1][j-1] > 0:
                        count[i][j] -= count[i+1][j-1]

    return dp[0][n-1], count[0][n-1]

print(longest_palindromic_subsequence("racecarprogramming"))
```

Here, `dp[i][j]` stores the length of the longest palindromic subsequence of the substring `s[i..j]` and `count[i][j]` stores the count of such longest subsequences. The final answer will be `dp[0][n-1]` and `count[0][n-1]`, which represent the length and count of the longest palindromic subsequence of the whole string.

In Perl, you can use the 'splice' function to remove and replace a subset of an array. To sort the subset, you can use a custom sort function. Here is how you can rewrite the Python code in Perl:

```perl
sub custom_sort {
  my($l, $n, $m, $s) = @_;
  my @subset = @{$l}[$n..$m];
  @subset = sort { $a <=> $b } @subset;
  @subset = map { $_ => 2 } @subset;
  @subset = reverse @subset if $s eq 'desc';
  splice @{$l}, $n, $m-$n+1, @subset;
  return $l;
}
```

Please note that Perl uses zero-based indexing like Python, where index '0' refers to the first element in the array. In the provided Python function, the range for slicing the list is inclusive of the end point 'm'. In Perl, when using ranges with array slicing or the 'splice' function, the range is inclusive of both end points. So, the code '$n..$m' includes both the 'n-th' and 'm-th' elements in the array.

Also note that in Perl, it's customary to pass arrays by reference (i.e., a pointer to the array) to avoid unnecessary copying of array data. If the array is large, copying it can waste memory and slow down your program, passing by reference is more efficient. So in above Perl function we have '$l' received as a reference to an array.

Here's a basic implementation of a GraphQL API for a note taking application using Apollo Server and Express:

Here, we will be using an in-memory storage (Array) for this example. In a production-level application, a proper database like MongoDB, PostgreSQL etc. should be used.

First, install the necessary dependencies: 

```bash
npm install express apollo-server-express graphql
```

Now, let's consider the project main file (`server.js`):

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const { ApolloServer, gql } = require('apollo-server-express');

// An in-memory collection of our notes
let notes = [];

// Defining the schema for our GraphQL API
const typeDefs = gql`
  type Note {
    id: Int!
    title: String!
    description: String!
  }

  type Query {
    getNotes: [Note!]
  }

  type Mutation {
    addNote(title: String!, description: String!): Note!
    editNote(id: Int!, title: String!, description: String!): Note
    removeNote(id: Int!): Boolean
  }
`;

// The resolvers for the schema
const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    getNotes: () => notes,
  },
  Mutation: {
    addNote: (_, { title, description }) => {
      const newNote = { id: notes.length + 1, title, description };
      notes.push(newNote);
      return newNote;
    },
    editNote: (_, { id, title, description }) => {
      const noteIndex = notes.findIndex(note => note.id === id);

      if(noteIndex === -1) throw new Error('Note not found');

      notes[noteIndex] = { id, title, description };
      return notes[noteIndex];
    },
    removeNote: (_, { id }) => {
      const noteIndex = notes.findIndex(note => note.id === id);

      if(noteIndex === -1) throw new Error('Note not found');

      notes.splice(noteIndex, 1); 
      return true;
    },
  },
  // No need for resolvers for Note since the property names match exactly the names in our schema
};

// Initiating our Apollo Server
const server = new ApolloServer({ typeDefs, resolvers });

// Attaching our Apollo Server to an instance of Express
const app = express();
server.applyMiddleware({ app, path: '/graphql' });

// Starting the server
app.listen({ port: 4000 }, () =>
  console.log(`Server ready at http://localhost:4000${server.graphqlPath}`)
);
```
You can now start your app with the command `node server.js` and navigate to `http://localhost:4000/graphql` in a web browser to test out the GraphQL Playground. 

