Implementing multithreading in this program will not yield a performance improvement due to the nature of the problem. Threads need to access data sequentially (last character first), which makes parallelization useless. But since this is an academic exercise here is how you might implement it:

```c++
#include<iostream>
#include<string>
#include<thread>
#include<vector>
#include<mutex>

std::mutex mu;

void reverse_string(const std::string& str, int start, int end){
    for (int i = end; i >= start; i--) {
        mu.lock();
        std::cout << str[i];
        mu.unlock();
    }
}

int main(){
    std::string input_string;
    std::cout<<"Enter a string: ";
    std::cin>>input_string;

    unsigned int num_threads = std::thread::hardware_concurrency();
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;

    // Decide how many characters each thread will process
    int string_size = input_string.size();
    int chars_per_thread = string_size / num_threads;

    for (int i = 0; i < num_threads; i++) {
        int start = string_size - (i * chars_per_thread + chars_per_thread);
        int end = start + chars_per_thread;

        // The last thread picks up any remaining characters
        if(i == num_threads - 1 && end < string_size){
            end = string_size;
        }

        threads.push_back(std::thread(reverse_string, input_string, start, end - 1));
    }

    // Wait for all threads to finish
    for (auto& thread : threads) {
        thread.join();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This program creates a number of threads equal to the number of processor cores on the machine it's running on. It then divides the string into parts and assigns each part to a thread. Because the characters must be printed in reverse order, the threads are created in reverse order. As each thread completes, it prints the section of the string assigned to it in reverse order.

The mutex lock is used to serialize the output to the console. Although each thread processes a unique section of the string, multiple threads might try to write to the console at the same time. This could result in race conditions where the output from one thread interrupts the output from another. The mutex lock prevents this, ensuring that each thread's output is printed as a block.

