This can be implemented with the help of a Queue in Java. 

Specifically, a Queue of Iterators can be used, where each Iterator represents an input List. 

Given that the input is 'v1' and 'v2', firstly create Iterators for these input Lists and add them to the Queue.

To get the next element, poll the first Iterator from the Queue, get its next element, and if this Iterator still has more elements, add it back to the Queue.

The iteration finishes when the Queue becomes empty, meaning that there are no more elements in all Iterators.

Here is the Java code that can achieve this:

```java
class ZigzagIterator {
    Queue<Iterator<Integer>> queue; // queue of iterators

    public ZigzagIterator(List<Integer> v1, List<Integer> v2) {
        queue = new LinkedList<>();
        if (!v1.isEmpty()) queue.offer(v1.iterator());
        if (!v2.isEmpty()) queue.offer(v2.iterator());
    }

    public int next() {
        if (!hasNext()) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException();
        }
        Iterator<Integer> curr = queue.poll();
        int res = curr.next();
        if (curr.hasNext()) queue.offer(curr);
        return res;
    }

    public boolean hasNext() {
        return !queue.isEmpty();
    }
}
```

For the extension question where we are given k arrays, the above solution is general and can adapt to k inputs naturally. 

For each input, we create an Iterator and add it to the Queue. The rest of the process remains the same. 

The only difference is that instead of only two inputs 'v1' and 'v2', we now have a list of inputs, and we need to iterate this list to add all non-empty iterators to our queue. 

The overall time complexity for next() and hasNext() is still O(1) in average assuming the length of each list is similar, and the space complexity is O(k), where k is the number of the given arrays.

