You can also add a database connect exception to circuit breaker. CircuitBreaker can be used in conjuction with MicroProfile's FaultTolerance. Here's a basic example:

```java
@Path("/api")
@ApplicationScoped
public class MyResource {

  @Inject
  @CircuitBreaker(requestVolumeThreshold = 4, failureRatio=0.5, delay = 1000)
  MyDatabaseService myDatabaseService;

  @GET
  @Path("/data")
  @Produces(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
  public Response getData() {
    try {
      return Response.ok(myDatabaseService.getData()).build();
    } catch(Exception e) {
      throw new ServiceUnavailableException("Database connection issue");
    }
  }
}
```

In this example, if the `myDatabaseService` fails to successfully execute `getData()` method more than `failureRatio * requestVolumeThreshold` times, then for `delay` ms the breaker will be open and all other calls will fail immediately. After delay ms, next calls will determine if the breaker can be closed again.

