It is normal and okay to call `createSubscription` during the initialization of your Cloud Run service. The key here is not to call `createSubscription` for every request the service handles, as the subscription is shared across all instances of the service, not per instance or request. Hence, it needs to be created just once.

It is suggested to add a try/catch block to handle the error when the subscription already exists. It might seem odd, but it actually is an idempotent way to ensure that the subscription exists. 

Here's a sample snippet:

```
try {  
  await pubsub.topic(topicName).createSubscription(subscriptionName);  
} catch (error) {
  if (error.code === 6) {
    console.log(`Subscription ${subscriptionName} already exists.`);  
  } else {
    console.error(`Error creating subscription ${subscriptionName}:`, error);
    throw error;
  }
}
```

In this code:

- If the subscription already exists, you'll get a gRPC status code 6 (`ALREADY_EXISTS`) and you can ignore this error.
- If the code fails with some other error, something might be wrong, hence you log the error message and rethrow the error.

You're not doing anything wrong by implementing it this way. It might seem unusual because there's a possibility of an error being thrown then ignored, but it's actually a specifically allowed pattern when you consider that this is an idempotent operation (running the operation multiple times has the same effect as running it once) and it helps to ensure the subscription's existence.

