To calculate the power of two of a given number using bitwise operation, you can shift the number "1" to the left by the given number of times. In java, you achieve this using the "<<" operator. Here is how you could write the function:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int number = 4;          // Input Number
        int powerOfTwo = 1 << number; // Bitwise left shift
        System.out.println("2 to the power " + number + " is : " + powerOfTwo);
    }
}
```

The `<<` operator is a bitwise operator to shift the bits of a number to the left. So, when you do `1 << 4` it means you're shifting the bits of the number `1` four places to the left. In binary `1` is `0001` (considering only 4 bits), so shifting `1` four places to the left would get you `10000`, which is 16 in decimal. Hence, `2 ^ 4 = 16`.

Note that this method only work as "2 power of" not "any number power of", and also fails to calculate negative power or power of 0.

