This solution creates a new Node class for the linked list and a mergeLists function for handling the merging process.

We use recursion in the mergeLists method to combine the two lists. At each recursive step, we compare the values of the current nodes in both lists and add the smaller one to the merged list. If we reach the end of one list before the other, we simply append the remaining nodes from the other list to our result.

```java
public class Node {
    int val; 
    Node next;
    Node() {}
    Node(int val) { this.val = val; } 
    Node(int val, Node next) { this.val = val; this.next = next; } 
}

public Node mergeLists(Node l1, Node l2) {
    if(l1 == null) {
        return l2;
    }
    if(l2 == null) {
        return l1;
    }

    /* If the value of the first linked list is smaller than the second one, 
       we link the next node to the remaining merged list. If the value of the first 
       linked list is larger, the first and second lists are swapped. */
    if(l1.val <= l2.val) {
        l1.next = mergeLists(l1.next, l2);
        return l1;
    } else {
        l2.next = mergeLists(l1, l2.next);
        return l2;
    }
}
```

Once we have the merging function, we can easily create our linked lists and call the function.

```java
