One way to resolve this problem is by not converting the binary number to a decimal. That's because the last digit of any binary number determines whether it is even or odd. If the last digit is 0, it's even. If it's 1, it's odd. We can get the last character from the string and check its value directly. This allows us to handle any number of bits without worrying about the number size limit.

Here is the JavaScript function that checks if a binary string represents an odd number:

```javascript
function isBinaryOdd(binary) {
    // Validate input
    if (typeof binary !== 'string' || !/^[01]+$/.test(binary)) {
        throw new Error('Input must be a string of 0s and 1s only.');
    }
    // Check last digit
    return binary[binary.length - 1] === '1';
}
```

