The issue seems to be that the `default` option in the `@Column` decorator in TypeORM is a constant value not a function that is re-evaluated for each insertion. In other words, the default value for your public_id column is computed once when your app starts and doesn't change after that. 

One workaround is to manually set the `public_id` each time you create a new instance of your entity. Here’s an example using constructor:

```typescript
import { nanoid } from 'nanoid'

@Entity()
export class YourEntity {

    // other columns...

    @Column({ unique: true })
    public_id: string;

    constructor() {
        this.public_id = nanoid();
    }
}
```

Another way is to use lifecycle hooks. `BeforeInsert` is a decorator that designates a method to be run before an entity gets inserted into the database.

```typescript
import { nanoid } from 'nanoid'

@Entity()
export class YourEntity {

    // other columns...

    @Column({ unique: true })
    public_id: string;

    @BeforeInsert()
    generatePublicId() {
        this.public_id = nanoid();
    }
}
```

This method will run for every new instance of the entity that gets saved, ensuring you have a new nanoid generated each time.

