In C++, you can call a base class constructor from a derived class using an initialization list. The syntax is as follows:

```cpp
DerivedClass::DerivedClass() : BaseClass(argument_list) {
    // Derived class constructor body
}
```

The argument list (argument_list) in the example above is supposed to be a list of arguments that you want to pass to the base class constructor.

Note the following rules about calling the base class constructor:

1. The base class constructor call must be placed after the colon (:) and before the body of the derived class constructor.

2. If you do not explicitly call the base class constructor, the default constructor of the base class is automatically called.

3. If you provide a call to a base class constructor, it must be done before initialization of any non-static member variables in the derived class. This is because in C++, the order of initialization is strictly defined: base classes are initialized first, followed by non-static member variables in the order that they appear in the class definition.

4. If the base class does not have a default constructor (a constructor with no arguments), you must explicitly call one of the base class constructors in the derived class constructor initialization list.

5. If a class is derived from multiple base classes, the constructors of the base classes are called in the order they were declared in the class definition.

Here is an example:

```cpp
class Base {
public:
    Base(int val) : val_(val) { }

private:
    int val_;
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
    Derived(int val) : Base(val), derivedVal_(val + 1) { }

private:
    int derivedVal_;
};
```

Here, the `Derived` class constructor explicitly calls the `Base` class constructor and passes it an argument. We also initialize `derivedVal_` member variable in the constructor initialization list.

