This problem can be solved recursively. The basic idea is to trim the branches recursively. Every time you encounter a node, you first check if the node is within the boundary. If it is not, you should cut this branch and move to the node's child.

The algorithm works as follows:
1. When node.val > high, we know that the trimmed binary tree must occur to the left of the node. Similarly, when node.val < low, the trimmed binary tree occurs to its right. Otherwise, we will trim both sides of the trees.

Here is a Python solution:

```python
class Solution:
    def trimBST(self, root, low, high):
        if root is None:
            return None
        elif root.val > high:
            return self.trimBST(root.left, low, high)
        elif root.val < low:
            return self.trimBST(root.right, low, high)
        else:
            root.left = self.trimBST(root.left, low, high)
            root.right = self.trimBST(root.right, low, high)
            return root
```

This solution works by traversing the BST using the Depth First Search (DFS). When the current node's value is out of the range `[low, high]`, we return the trimmed subtree that our algorithm will traverse next. 

Therefore, when `root.val > high`, we return `trimBST(root.left, low, high)` that all the nodes in the left subtree are less than the node.val and when `root.val < low` we return `trimBST(root.right, low, high)`, thus all nodes in the right subtree are greater than the node.val. 

Otherwise, we trim both the left and right subtree. The time complexity of this algorithm is `O(N)`, where `N` is the total number of nodes in the tree. Since we visit each node at most once. The space complexity is also `O(N)`. Even though we don't explicitly use any additional data structures, we have to take into account the recursion stack, which in worst case scenario could be `N` levels deep (unbalanced tree), before hitting the base case `root is None`.

