To solve this task you can make use of the Vue.js framework and its mixin functionality. Mixins are a flexible way to distribute reusable functionalities for Vue components. A mixin object can contain any component options. When a component uses a mixin, all options in the mixin will be "mixed" into the component's own options.

Here is an example code that should fulfill your requirements:

```javascript
var pageMixin = {
  data: function() {
    return {
      currentPage: null
    }
  },
  created: function() {
    this.currentPage = this.getPageFromURL();
  },
  methods: {
    getPageFromURL: function() {
      // Obtain the current URL
      var url = new URL(window.location.href);

      // Obtain the "page" query parameter
      var pageQueryParam = url.searchParams.get("page");

      return pageQueryParam;
    }
  },
  watch: {
    '$route': function(newRoute) {
      this.currentPage = this.getPageFromURL();
    }
  }
}

// Now, you can use this mixin in a component
var MyComponent = {
  mixins: [pageMixin],
  // more code here...
}

```

In this code, a mixin is created with the name "pageMixin". When a component is using this mixin, the "currentPage" data attribute of the component is set to the "page" query parameter from the URL when the component is created and every time the route changes.

Note: The "created" entry with the function is a Vue.js lifecycle hook, which applies when a component instance has just been created.

The "getPageFromURL" method obtains the current URL using "window.location.href", then create a new URL object and extract "page" query parameter's value.

The "watch" property is defined to listen for changes to the Vue Router '$route' object, so we can then set the currentPage again when the URL route changes.

Solving this problem via programming would involve using tree-based data structures, particularly a Trie (also known as Prefix Trees). The optimization of both computational efficiency and space utilization are some of the key concerns of databases and Trie is a data structure that takes care of both in terms of insertion, deletion, and search operations.

A Trie is a tree-like data structure whose nodes store the set of keys of a dataset. Strings can be retrieved from the dataset by traversing down a branch path of the tree. It is an efficient data structure as it provides a better performance, while allowing for faster retrieval times. The time complexity for creating (inserting), searching, and deleting a Trie is O(L), L being the length of the word. This makes it a good choice for situations where you have to perform multiple searches like in the case of a database.

Explanation through Programming (Python):

```python
class TrieNode:
    def __init__(self):
        self.children = [None]*26
        self.isEndOfWord = False

class Trie:
    def __init__(self):
        self.root = self.getNode()

    def getNode(self):
        return TrieNode()

    def _charToIndex(self,ch):
        return ord(ch)-ord('a')

    def insert(self,root,Key):
        pCrawl = root
        length = len(Key)
        for level in range(length):
            index = self._charToIndex(Key[level])
            if pCrawl.children[index] == None:
                pCrawl.children[index] = self.getNode()
            pCrawl = pCrawl.children[index]
        pCrawl.isEndOfWord = True

    def search(self, root, Key):
        pCrawl = root
        length = len(Key)
        for level in range(length):
            index = self._charToIndex(Key[level])
            if pCrawl.children[index] == None:
                return False
            pCrawl = pCrawl.children[index]
        return pCrawl != None and pCrawl.isEndOfWord
```

To summarize, Tries tend to be faster for larger datasets. They are superior for tasks that have to deal with finding words with a prefix, determining if a string is a prefix of any words in the dataset, and figuring out the longest prefix of a word. Additionally, the Trie is more space-efficient when it deals with a situation where many keys share the same prefix. All these advantages make Trie data structure an optimal solution to handle a structured database involving lots of searching and insertion of strings, thus influencing the system performance as well as scalability.

