Here is the implementation in Java:

```java
public class BinarySearch {
    static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target, boolean searchFirst) {
        int low = 0;
        int high = arr.length - 1;
        int result = -1;

        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2; 

            if (target == arr[mid]) {
                result = mid;
                // If we find a match, we move in the 'opposite' direction of the desired end.
                if (searchFirst) { 
                    high = mid - 1; 
                } else { 
                    low = mid + 1;
                }
            } else if (target < arr[mid]) {
                high = mid - 1;
            } else {
                low = mid + 1;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {2, 4, 10, 10, 10, 18, 20};
        System.out.println(binarySearch(numbers, 10, true)); // Output: 2 
        System.out.println(binarySearch(numbers, 10, false)); // Output: 4
    }
}
```

This implementation does a regular binary search but has an extra parameter `searchFirst`. When `searchFirst` is true, if we find the target value, we continue to search in the left half of the mid element to see if the target appears before the current found position. If `searchFirst` is false and we find the target value, we continue to search in the right half of the mid element to see if the target appears after.

Binary search runs with a time complexity of O(log n) because at each step, we're reducing the potential 'search space' by half. The space complexity of this solution is O(1) because only a constant amount of extra space is used (for the variables `low`, `high`, `mid` and `result`), regardless of the input size.

It's crucial to understand that binary search immensely benefits from the sorted nature of the input array. This allows us to make educated decisions about where our desired number might be, exploiting the definitional properties of ordered sets. The introduction of duplicated numbers necessitated small adaptations to the traditional binary search algorithm.

