Here's a possible approach you can consider:

1. Use Azure Blob SAS (Shared Access Signatures): SAS provides secure, delegated access to your Azure Storage resources without exposing your connection strings or requiring your keys. With SAS, you can grant limited permissions to your Azure Blob Storage objects for a specified amount of time and with a specific set of permissions.

2. Create a middle-tier service/API: Rather than accessing blob storage directly from the client-side, create a back-end server (in .NET, as you're using) that interacts with Blob storage on behalf of the client. The server manages the access to the Azure Storage and retrieves the SAS token. 

