There are several methods to solve this problem but one of the optimized methods is the Sliding Window concept. The Sliding Window concept is a window that can move to the right one by one, and only when a repeated character is found.

To solve the problem, we can use the `unordered_map` in C++. The key of`unordered_map `is the character, and the value is the last position of the character.

Here's the C++ solution:
```cpp
#include <unordered_map>
using namespace std;

pair<int, int> longestSubstringWithoutRepeatingCharacters(string s) {
    if (s.empty()) return {0, 0};
    unordered_map<char, int> map;
    int start = 0, length = 0, uniqueChars = 0;
    for(int i = 0; i < s.size(); i++){
        if(map.find(s[i]) == map.end()){
            map[s[i]] = i;
            if(i - start + 1 > length){
                length = i - start + 1;
                uniqueChars = map.size();
            }
        }
        else{
            start = map[s[i]] + 1;
            map.clear();
            map[s[i]] = i;
        }
    }
    return {length, uniqueChars};
}

int main() {
    string s = "pwwkew";
    pair<int, int> result = longestSubstringWithoutRepeatingCharacters(s);
    cout << "The length of the longest substring without repeating characters is " << result.first << '\n';
    cout << "The number of unique characters in that substring is " << result.second << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

In this code, we initialize the start position of the window and the length of the longest non-repetitive substring to be 0. We then iterate through the string. If a character has not been repeated, we store in the map the character and its position, and update the length (and unique char count) if needed. If a character has been repeated, we need to adjust the start position of the window to the next position of the previous same character, clear the map and add the current character to it.

The sliding window technique is a very useful concept in solving problems related to contiguous subarray/substring within an array/string.

