Here is a simple C code that can be used to calculate the power of a number without using pow() function:

```C
#include <stdio.h>

double power(int base, int exponent){
    int i;
    double result = 1.0;

    if(exponent > 0){
        for(i = 1; i <= exponent; i++){
            result = result * base;
        }
    }

    else if(exponent < 0){
        for(i = 1; i <= -exponent; i++){
            result = result / base;
        }
    }

    // if exponent is 0, result will remain 1
    return result; 
}

int main(){
    int base, exponent;
    printf("Enter base number: ");
    scanf("%d", &base);
    printf("Enter an exponent: ");
    scanf("%d", &exponent);
    printf("%d^%d = %.2lf", base,exponent, power(base, exponent));
    return 0;
}
```

This C code uses a `for` loop to multiply or divide the base number by itself for the number of times equal to the exponent's absolute value.

When the exponent is a positive integer, the base number is multiplied by itself in the number of times equal to the power. The multiplication result is stored in the variable `result`. The loop iterates until `i` is greater than the positive `exponent`.

When the exponent is a negative integer, to handle negative powers, the base number is divided by itself the number of times equal to the power. The division result is stored in the variable `result`. The loop iterates until `i` is greater than the absolute value of the negative `exponent`.

When the exponent is 0, the power of any number is always 1, hence `result` remains 1.

Finally, it will print the result of the calculation. The result is a double value, so it's using '%.2lf' to limit the output to two decimal places. 

Note: The user must enter an integer for the base number and the exponent.

