The `semtimedop()` is a Linux system call that can perform operations on a set of semaphores (a synchronization mechanism that protects shared resources from simultaneous access).

Here's how to initialize a `timespec` argument:

```c
struct timespec timeout;

timeout.tv_sec = 5;      // 5 seconds
timeout.tv_nsec = 0;     // 0 nanoseconds
```

In the above example, we've set the timeout to be 5 seconds. The `timespec` structure has two fields: `tv_sec` for seconds and `tv_nsec` for nanoseconds.

Here’s how we can use the `semtimedop()` function in C:

```c
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/ipc.h>
#include <sys/sem.h>
#include <time.h>

#define SEMID 123456   // It should be semaphore id semget had previously returned.

int main() {
    // Initialize sembuf
    struct sembuf operations[2];

    operations[0].sem_num = 0;
    operations[0].sem_op = 0;
    operations[0].sem_flg = 0;

    operations[1].sem_num = 0;
    operations[1].sem_op = 1;
    operations[1].sem_flg = 0;

    // Initialize timespec
    struct timespec timeout;
    timeout.tv_sec = 5;      // 5 seconds
    timeout.tv_nsec = 0;     // 0 nanoseconds

    if (semtimedop(SEMID, operations, 2, &timeout) == -1) {
        perror("Semtimedop failed");
        return 1;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In the above example, it waits until the zeroth semaphore of the semaphore set that `SEMID` refers to becomes 0. If it is already 0, it immediately proceeds to the next operation, which increments the semaphore. If it is not 0, it waits up to the specified timeout (5 seconds). If the semaphore does not become 0 before the timeout has elapsed, `semtimedop()` returns -1. The operations are performed atomically (i.e., either all operations are done or none are).

Remember that you need to include the header files `sys/types.h`, `sys/ipc.h`, `sys/sem.h` and `time.h` to use `semtimedop()` and `timespec`. The `semtimedop()` function is part of the System V semaphores API. Please be mindful of the permissions needed to access semaphore sets in a multiprocess/multithreaded environment.

