import { interpolate } from 'flubber';

var fromPath = document.querySelector('path');
var toPath = document.querySelector('#targetPath');

var interpolator = interpolate(fromPath.getAttribute('d'), toPath.getAttribute('d'), { maxSegmentLength: 1 });

function update(t) {
  fromPath.setAttribute('d', interpolator(t));
  if (t < 1.0)
    requestAnimationFrame(update);
}

requestAnimationFrame(update);
```

This code will use `flubber` library to create an `interpolator` function between your original path and the target path (the circle). `update` function will then be called on each animation frame till animation reaches its end (when `t` becomes 1).

Note the `maxSegmentLength` option for flubber's `interpolate` function. It's often necessary to coerce your input paths to have sufficiently similar structure that they can be interpolated, `flubber` can do this automatically by resampling them to have a uniform number of approximately equally-spaced points. 

Please be aware that complex SVG morphing (like this one, where original SVG has multiple paths and final shape is a single circle) could also require manual pre-processing, and some paths can't be morphed to certain shapes. This example is simplified and should serve as a starting point. Consider using software dedicated to creating complex SVG animations like Adobe After Effects with Bodymovin plugin, if you don't like coding that much.

