Here is a Java function that calculates the standard deviation and also validates the input and finds outliers. The function accepts an array of floating-point numbers as the argument. It first checks if the array is empty or null. It then calculates the mean of the numbers. The standard deviation is then found out. An outlier in this case is defined as a number that is more than two standard deviations away from the mean. 

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        float[] data = {3.3f, 5.6f, 7.8f, 5.4f, 9.9f};
        calculateSDAndFindOutliers(data);
    }

    public static void calculateSDAndFindOutliers(float[] data) {

        // validate input
        if (data == null || data.length == 0) {
            System.out.println("Empty dataset provided");
            return;
        }

        // calculate mean
        float sum = 0.0f;
        for(float num : data) sum += num;

        float mean = sum/data.length;

        //calculate squared difference from the mean
        sum = 0.0f;
        for(float num: data) sum += Math.pow(num - mean, 2);

        // calculate SD
        float standardDeviation = (float) Math.sqrt(sum / data.length);
        System.out.println("Standard Deviation = " + standardDeviation);

        // check for outliers
        float outlierLowerBound = mean - 2 * standardDeviation;
        float outlierUpperBound = mean + 2 * standardDeviation;

        ArrayList<Float> outliers = new ArrayList<Float>();
        for(float num: data) {
            if (num < outlierLowerBound || num > outlierUpperBound) {
                outliers.add(num);
            }
        }

        if (outliers.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("No outliers in data.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Outliers: " + Arrays.toString(outliers.toArray()));
        }
    }
}
```
This function will first print the calculated standard deviation and then either a message indicating that there are no outliers or a list of detected outliers.

