In Java, the Fibonacci sequence can be computed with recursion and memoization by storing values that have already been computed in a table. Here is a program that achieves this:

```java
public class Fibonacci {
    private long[] memo;

    public Fibonacci() {
        this.memo = new long[26];  // Create the memoization table
        this.memo[0] = 0;          // Base case 1
        this.memo[1] = 1;          // Base case 2
    }

    public long fib(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
                return n;
            } else if (memo[n] != 0) {             // If the value is in the table
                return memo[n];                     // Return it
            } else {                                // Otherwise
                long result = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);   // Compute it
                memo[n] = result;                  // Store it in table
                return result;                     // Return it
            }
        }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Fibonacci fib = new Fibonacci();
        for (int i = 0; i <= 25; i++) {
            System.out.println(fib.fib(i));
        }
    }

}
```

This program defines a `Fibonacci` class with a `fib` method that computes the Fibonacci sequence. A table named `memo` stores computed values to eliminate repeated work. This much speeds up the computation by avoiding recalculating the same Fibonacci numbers many times, but instead just fetching them from the `memo` table when needed. This technique of saving computations is called "memoization".

The `main` function in the program creates an instance of the `Fibonacci` class, then prints out the Fibonacci sequence up to 25. Note that because the `memo` table is only pre-initialized for the first two Fibonacci numbers, we can't print out higher Fibonacci numbers without first resizing the `memo` table.

Here is a Java program that prints the first n lines of a modified Pascal's triangle. We'll use dynamic programming to optimize and speed up the execution time. The method `gcd` will find the greatest common divisor (gcd) between two numbers. Also, the program will handle the case where n < 0 by printing an error message in such cases.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class ModifiedPascalTriangle {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Enter the number of lines: ");
        int n = scanner.nextInt();
        if (n < 0){ 
            System.out.println("Error: Number of lines cannot be negative!");
            return;
        } 
        printModifiedPascalsTriangle(n);
    }

    private static int gcd(int a, int b) {
        if (b == 0) return a;
        return gcd(b, a % b);
    }

    private static void printModifiedPascalsTriangle(int n) {
        int[][] arr = new int[n][];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            arr[i] = new int[i + 1];
            arr[i][0] = arr[i][i] = 1;
            System.out.print("1 ");
            for (int j = 1; j < i; j++) {
                arr[i][j] = gcd(arr[i - 1][j - 1], arr[i - 1][j]);
                System.out.print(arr[i][j] + " ");
            }
            if (i != 0)
                System.out.print("1");
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

When run, the program will ask for the number of lines to print. It will then print that many lines of the triangle or an error message if the entered number is less than 0. The numbers in the triangle are calculated by taking the gcd of the two numbers directly above them in the previous row. The first and last number on each row are always 1. The computation is done row by row, storing the result of the previous row, this reuses previously computed numbers, a common trait of a dynamic programming approach.

Remember to always close the scanner when done, here we did not as we don't need to interact with the user after this but if you continue to use the scanner again, remember to close it.

_NOTE_: As per the original Pascal's Triangle, the first row (n=1) would only have one element i.e., '1'.

