Here is a thread-safe LinkedList implementation in Java using `synchronized` blocks for add, remove, and search operations. Exceptions are also handled properly by checking conditions before operations.

```Java
public class LinkedList {
    private Node head;

    private class Node {
        int value;
        Node next;

        Node(int value) {
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    // Add element to the list
    public synchronized void add(int value) {
        Node newNode = new Node(value);

        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
        } else {
            Node current = head;
            while (current.next != null) {
                current = current.next;
            }
            current.next = newNode;
        }
    }

    // Remove element from the list
    public synchronized void remove(int value) throws Exception {
        if (head == null) {
            throw new Exception("List is empty, nothing to remove");
        }

        if (head.value == value) {
            head = head.next;
            return;
        }

        Node current = head;
        while (current.next != null) {
            if (current.next.value == value) {
                current.next = current.next.next;
                return;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }

        throw new Exception("Value not found in list. Nothing removed.");       
    }

    // Search for element in the list
    public synchronized boolean search(int value) {
        Node current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.value == value) {
                return true;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }
        return false;      
    }

    // Print all elements in the list
    public synchronized void listAllElements() {
        Node current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.println(current.value);
            current = current.next;
        }
    }
}
```

In this class, the `Node` inner class has a `value` and a `next` reference to another Node. When you add elements, new Nodes are created and chained in the `next` reference, creating the linked list. When you remove elements, the previous node points to the node succeeding the one being removed.

