Here is a simple yet complex example of a thread-safe C++ class implementing the asked features:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>

class ComplexClass {
private:
    int *data;
    std::string info;
    std::mutex mut;
public:
    // Default Constructor
    ComplexClass(const std::string &info = "", int dataSize = 0) : info(info) {
        this->data = new int[dataSize]();
    }

    // Copy Constructor 
    ComplexClass(const ComplexClass &obj) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(obj.mut);
        this->info = obj.info;
        this->data = new int[sizeof(obj.data)]();
        std::copy(obj.data, obj.data + sizeof(obj.data), this->data);
    }

    // Get method for 'info' with thread-safety
    std::string getInfo() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mut);
        return info;
    }

    // Set method for 'info' with thread-safety
    void setInfo(const std::string &newInfo) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mut);
        this->info = newInfo;
    }

    // Destructor
    ~ComplexClass() {
        delete[] data;
    }
};
```

In this class, a mutex (`std::mutex`) is used to ensure thread safety for the 'info' member. The `std::lock_guard` automatically locks the mutex when control enters the scope and releases the lock when control leaves the scope, making it exception safe.

In the copy constructor, a deep copy of 'data' is performed using the `std::copy` function after locking the source object's mutex for thread safety.

The destructor cleans up the dynamic 'data' array to prevent memory leaks.

