This issue has to do with how the C# compiler processes the `using` directives. When a type alias is defined, the compiler needs to resolve the right-hand side of the directive (the actual type) only when it comes across a usage of the alias (the left-hand side).

So in the example:

```
using IntPair = Tuple<int,int>;
using IntPairList = List<IntPair>; 
```

The compiler tries to resolve the type alias `IntPair` when it's trying to define `IntPairList`. At this point, `IntPair` is not yet known to the compiler because its usage hasn't been encountered yet. This results in a compile error.

This problem, however, doesn't exist with nested scopes, e.g. inside classes, where the type resolution occurs in top-down order. So this will compile:

```csharp
class Bar
{
    using IntPair = Tuple<int,int>;
    using IntPairList = List<IntPair>; // This now works because IntPair is defined in the same or outer scope.
}
```

But in general, type aliases should be carefully used to avoid confusion or potential resolution problems like this one. They are best suited for long or complex type names that are hard to read and/or write. For simpler types, using the actual type name is more clear and less error-prone.

