Here is a Python solution for the problem:

```python
def lowestCommonAncestor(self, p: 'Node', q: 'Node') -> 'Node':
    node1, node2 = p, q
    while node1 != node2:
        node1 = node1.parent if node1 != p else q
        node2 = node2.parent if node2 != q else p
    return node1

def isValidBST(self, root: 'Node', low=float('-inf'), high=float('inf')) -> bool:
    if not root:
        return True
    if not low < root.val < high:
        return False

    return (self.isValidBST(root.left, low, root.val)
            and self.isValidBST(root.right, root.val, high))
```
The first function, `lowestCommonAncestor`, is where we find the Lowest Common Ancestor of two nodes in a binary tree. By starting from both `p` and `q` and moving upward, when `p` and `q` meet, it is the lowest common ancestor of both. When either pointer moves up to the root, it is moved to the other's original position. The purpose of this is to make sure they'll meet at some point, even if they are not in the same depth.

The second function, `isValidBST`, is to validate if a binary tree is a valid Binary Search Tree (BST). In a valid BST, for any node, all nodes in its left subtree are less than it, and all nodes in its right subtree are larger than it. A recursive method is used here to check all nodes in the tree. If any node doesn't meet the requirement, `False` is returned immediately.

The time complexity of `lowestCommonAncestor` is O(H), where H is the height of the tree. The space complexity is O(1), because we don't need any additional space.

The time complexity of `isValidBST` is O(N), where N is the number of nodes in the tree. The space complexity is O(H), where H is the height of the tree, because we have to hold up to H recursive calls in the call stack.

