&lt;p&gt;
Imagine a directed graph constructed from an orthogonal lattice comprising $H\times W$ nodes. The edges are the horizontal and vertical connections between adjacent nodes. $W$ vertical directed lines are drawn, with all the edges on these lines adopting that direction. Similarly, $H$ horizontal directed lines are drawn, with all the edges on these lines adopting that direction.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Two nodes, $A$ and $B$ in a directed graph, are &lt;strong&gt;strongly connected&lt;/strong&gt; if there is a path, along the directed edges, from $A$ to $B$ and vice versa. It's important to note that every node is strongly connected to itself.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
A &lt;strong&gt;strongly connected component&lt;/strong&gt; in a directed graph is a non-empty set $M$ of nodes that fulfills the following two conditions:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;All nodes in $M$ are strongly connected to each other.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$M$ is maximal, meaning that no node in $M$ is strongly connected to any node outside of $M$.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
There are $2^H\times 2^W$ methods of drawing the directed lines. Each method results in a directed graph $\mathcal{G}$. We denote $S(\mathcal{G})$ as the number of strongly connected components in $\mathcal{G}$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
The illustration below depicts a directed graph with $H=3$ and $W=4$ that is composed of four distinct strongly connected components (highlighted by the different colours).
&lt;/p&gt;
