Here, we will create a Java solution based on Runnable interface. We utilize synchronized block to synchronize across multiple threads.

```java
public class MultiplePrinter implements Runnable {

    private final int number;
    private static int current = 1;
    private static final Object lock = new Object();

    public MultiplePrinter(int number) {
        this.number = number;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        while (current <= 10) {
            synchronized (lock) {
                System.out.println(number * current);
                current++;
                try {
                    // Let others threads run.
                    if (current <= 10) {
                        lock.wait();
                    }
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }

                lock.notifyAll();
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int valueToTest = 5;  // replace it with any value you want.
        for (int i = 0; i < Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors(); i++) {
            new Thread(new MultiplePrinter(valueToTest)).start();
        }
    }
}
```
In this code, we have a Runnable MultiplePrinter that multiplies a given number with a shared counter (current). So, if number is 5, it starts outputting 5, 10, 15, .... We use synchronized block to order the output in ascending order. notifyAll() is called to wake all the thread sleeping on lock and wait() is called to put the current thread in a waiting state until other thread calls notify(). Threads are created and started equal to number of available processors to show the multithreading scenario.

Some points on this:
- If current is more than 10, we don't call wait() to avoid the situation where all threads are waiting and none thread is left to call notify().
- As all threads are running the same Runnable, they all run in a loop until `current > 10`.
- It will generate 10 multiples of the number in ascending order since each thread increments the current counter exactly once per loop and it is in a synchronized block. So, no two threads can increment current at the same time.
- This will work with at least 1 available processor. More processors will better exhibit concurrent execution. 

This solution also handles InterruptedException which might occur during waiting state of thread.

