The erroneous CSS rule applies the font-style italic to all `div` elements and all paragraphs that are the first child of their parent. If the intent was to target only paragraphs under div or that are the first child of their parent, then the rule is not correctly defined. A better CSS rule would be:

```css
div > p, :first-child > p {
   font-style : italic;
}
```
In this rule, `div > p` applies the style to all paragraph (`p`) elements that are direct children of `div` elements. `:first-child > p` applies the style to all paragraphs that are the first child of any parent element.

Please note that using `:first-child` itself without specifying the type of the parent can have undesired effects if such a parent has a first child that is not a paragraph. Better specificity could be achieved by knowing what type of parent elements we are dealing with e.g. if they are `div` as well.

So, If we want to limit the first child style only to those paragraphs under a `div`, this can be the correct form:

```css
div > p, div > :first-child {
   font-style : italic;
}
```

_This above form of CSS only selects the first child of a div if it's a paragraph, also if a paragraph is a direct child of a div._

It's crucial to understand that CSS selectors have their specificity, CSS specificity assigns scores to a selector to dictate which styles are applied to an element. The CSS rule with the highest specificity score will be applied if there are multiple conflicting rules.

