Below is a JavaScript function that can be used to check if all elements in a given 3D array (matrix) are in descending order across each layer, row, and column.

```JavaScript
function checkDescending(matrix) {

    for(let k = 0; k < matrix.length; k++) {
        let layer = matrix[k];

        for(let i = 0; i < layer.length; i++) {
            let row = layer[i];

            for(let j = 1; j < row.length; j++) {
                if (row[j-1] < row[j]) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        for(let j = 0; j < layer[0].length; j++) {
            for(let i = 0; i < layer.length - 1; i++) {
                if (layer[i][j] < layer[i+1][j]) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    for(let i = 0; i < matrix[0].length; i++) {
        for(let j = 0; j < matrix[0][0].length; j++) {
            for(let k = 0; k < matrix.length - 1; k++) {
                if (matrix[k][i][j] < matrix[k+1][i][j]) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    return true;
}
```
This function works by iterating through the layers, rows, and columns of the matrix and comparing each element with its next element. 

If it finds that any element is smaller than the one right ahead of it in the same row, or the one right below it in the same column, or the one right in the front of it in the same column across different layer, it returns false, meaning the elements are not in descending order. 

If it gets through all the comparisons and hasn't found any where the next element is greater, it returns true, meaning all elements are in descending order.

