def findKth(nums1, nums2, k):
    if len(nums1) > len(nums2):
        nums1, nums2 = nums2, nums1
    if not nums1:
        return nums2[k - 1] if k <= len(nums2) else None
    if k == 1:
        return min(nums1[0], nums2[0])
    i, j = min(k // 2, len(nums1)), min(k // 2, len(nums2))
    if nums1[i - 1] > nums2[j - 1]:
        return findKth(nums1, nums2[j:], k - j)
    else:
        return findKth(nums1[i:], nums2, k - i)
```

This function uses a binary search approach to efficiently find the kth element in sorted arrays of potentially different sizes. We adjust the midpoint of our search in `nums1` and `nums2` based on the value of `k` and the relative size of `nums1[i - 1]` and `nums2[j - 1]`.

In the worst case scenario, our binary search will decrement by one in each recursive call when one array is significantly larger than the other (`k // 2` is equal to `len(nums1)`), which results in the time complexity of `O(log(min(n,m)))`. 

Please note that array's indices start from 0, so if there is no kth element (k is more than the sum of the lengths of the two arrays), `None` is returned. This is the approach to handle edge cases where `k` is larger than the combined size of the two arrays. 

