The following Java method checks if the given string is "happy" according to the given conditions:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {
    public boolean isHappy(String s) {
        if (s.length() < 3) {
            return false; // less than three letters
        }

        String firstSequence = s.substring(0, 3);

        if (!areAllCharsUnique(firstSequence)) {
            return false; // first three letters are not distinct
        }

        if (s.length() % 3 != 0) {
            return false; // length not multiple of 3, cannot be happy
        }

        for (int i = 3; i < s.length(); i += 3) {
            String sequence = s.substring(i, i + 3);
            if (!areAllCharsUnique(sequence) || !sequence.equals(firstSequence)) {
                return false; // a sequence has same letter or different sequence
            }
        }

        return true; // passed all tests, string is happy
    }

    public static boolean areAllCharsUnique(String str) {
        Set<Character> set = new HashSet<>();
        for (char c : str.toCharArray()) {
            if (!set.add(c)) {
                return false; // repeated character, not unique
            }
        }
        return true; // all characters are unique
    }
}
```
This method first checks if the string is long enough (at least three letters) and that the first three letters are distinct. Then it looks at each subsequent group of three letters. If any such group consists of letters that are not distinct or do not make up the same sequence as the first three letters, it returns false because the string is not "happy".

