As you are asking to solve Fib(N) mod M for values as large as 10^18, the naive approach of calculating Fibonacci directly (fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)) will not work even if we use memoization/dynamic programming due to space constraints. Matrix exponentiation technique also might not work due to the number's size. 

A efficient approach would be to use "Pisano Period" properties for the modulo of Fibonacci numbers. The remainder sequence when Fib(n) is divided by M repeats after a specific period (called Pisano period) for all M.

```
import java.util.*;

class Main {
    private static long getFibonacciHugeFast(long n, long m) {
        ArrayList<Long> fmod = new ArrayList<Long>(Arrays.asList(0L, 1L));
        //calculate pisano period
        for (int i = 2; ; i++) {
            fmod.add((fmod.get(i - 1) + fmod.get(i - 2)) % m);
            if (fmod.get(i) == 1 && fmod.get(i - 1) == 0) {
                break;
            }
        }

        //get remainder of n with period length to reduce the calculation
        int remainder = (int) (n % (fmod.size() - 2));

        return fmod.get(remainder);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        long n = scanner.nextLong();
        long m = scanner.nextLong();
        System.out.println(getFibonacciHugeFast(n, m));
        scanner.close();
    }
}
```

You can run this program and input two long number N and M(one for Fibonacci sequence, other is mod number), it will calculate Fibonacci(n) mod M.

This is a Java program based on Pisano Period. The time complexity for this will be O(m*m) for m < 10^4. This is because we need to find modulo m for each of the Fibonacci numbers until the pattern repeats. The repeating pattern length will always be <= m*m. Space complexity is also O(m*m) because we store the series until the pattern repeats.

For m < 10^4 and n up to 10^18, this program will work efficiently.

