Here's a simple Java program to reverse a string manually without using any built-in methods or libraries. This program uses character arrays and swaps characters in those arrays using a simple iteration. It handles all edge cases such as null, empty strings, and special ASCII characters.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String text = "Hello, World!";  // test string
        String reversedString = reverseString(text);

        System.out.println("Reversed String is: " + reversedString);
    }

    public static String reverseString(String str) {
        // Handling null and empty strings
        if (str == null || str.isEmpty()) {
            return str;
        }

        char[] chars = str.toCharArray(); // Convert string to character array
        int left = 0;
        int right = chars.length - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            // Swap the chars
            char temp = chars[left];
            chars[left] = chars[right];
            chars[right] = temp;

            // Move towards center
            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return new String(chars);
    }
}
```
This code will compute the reversed string in linear O(N) complexity where N is the length of the string. The optimization for memory usage comes from the fact that we're resorting to swapping characters in-place within the same array, compared to an approach that would build a new one entirely. This makes it an efficient solution to solve this problem. 

With this approach, you can handle a string of any length (bounded by the maximum length of a string that Java/C# can hold), handle all special characters, spaces, punctuation, and also null or empty strings.

