&lt;p&gt;In this problem, we employ randomness to approximate this product. That is, we choose a random, uniformly distributed, $m$-tuple of positive integers $(Y_1,Y_2,Y_3,\cdots,Y_m)$ such that $1=Y_0 \lt Y_1 \lt Y_2 \lt \cdots \lt Y_m \leq n$ and calculate a modified product $T^*$ as follows.
$$T^* = \prod_{i=1}^m g(Y_i)^{(Y_i-Y_{i-1})}$$&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We now define the error of this approximation to be $\Delta=T-T^*$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let $\mathbb{E}(\Delta|g(k),n,m)$ be the expected value of the error given the function $g(k)$, the number of terms $n$ in the product and the length of random sample $m$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, $\mathbb{E}(\Delta|k,100,50) = 2525/1326 \approx 1.904223$ and $\mathbb{E}(\Delta|\mu(k),10^4,10^2)\approx 5842.849907$, where $\mu(k)$ is Möbius function.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Find $\mathbb{E}(\Delta|\mu(k),12345678,12345)$ rounded to six places after the decimal point.&lt;/p&gt;

