Here is a JavaScript function that implements the Quick Sort algorithm, which has an average-case time complexity of O(n log n), one of the best among sorting algorithms. The function checks if the array contains any negative numbers and if so reverses the order of the sorted array before returning it.

```javascript
function quickSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1) {

    // Define the pivot and the split 
    const pivot = arr[Math.floor((right + left) / 2)];
    let i = left;
    let j = right;

    while (i <= j) {
        while (arr[i] < pivot) 
            i++;
        while (arr[j] > pivot) 
            j--;
        if (i <= j) 
        {
            [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]]; // ES6 array destructuring for swapping items
            i++;
            j--;
        }
    }

    // Recursively sort elements on the left of pivot and on the right
    if (left < j) 
        quickSort(arr, left, j);
    if (i < right) 
        quickSort(arr, i, right);

    return arr;
}

function sortArray(arr) {
  let sortedArr = quickSort(arr);

  // if array contains negative number, reverse the order
  if (sortedArr.some(num => num < 0)) {
    sortedArr.reverse();
  }

  return sortedArr;
}

let array = [5, 4, -3, 2, 1];
console.log(sortArray(array));
```

This will output: `[5, 4, 2, 1, -3]`

Note: QuickSort is not always efficient on small lists, but for simplicity reasons it's a good solution for this kind of problem. Merging Sort or Heap Sort could also be other choices with better worst-case scenario but with an extra space overhead. 

The negative values check here done after the sort doesn't impact that much to overall performance given that checking if an array with n elements contains at least one negative value takes linear time, O(n), given modern JS engines optimizations. 

Remember, always tailor the sorting algorithm to the specific characteristics of your problem to get optimal performance.

This task requires a sound understanding of C++ multithreading and synchronization. Here is a simple C++ program that uses threads to read characters from standard input and output them in the sequence they've been read. It uses a lock_guard for thread synchronization.

```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <condition_variable>

std::mutex mtx;
std::condition_variable cv;
std::queue<char> queue;
bool done = false;

void read() {
    char c;
    while (std::cin >> c) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lg(mtx);
        queue.push(c);
        cv.notify_one();
    }
    done = true;
    cv.notify_one();
}

void print() {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> ul(mtx);
    while (true) {
        cv.wait(ul, [] { return !queue.empty() || done; });
        while (!queue.empty()) {
            std::cout << queue.front() << " ";
            queue.pop();
        }
        if (queue.empty() && done) {
            break;
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    std::thread th1(read);
    std::thread th2(print);
    th1.join();
    th2.join();
    return 0;
}
```

This program creates two threads. The first thread (th1) reads a character from standard input and pushes it into a queue. The second thread (th2) waits until it receives a signal that either the queue is not empty or input reading is done. If the queue is not empty, it prints all characters from it. If the queue is empty and input reading is done then it breaks the loop and finishes its job.

Please note, a multithreaded console reading should be used carefully, as console input is buffered, and multiple threads reading from console at the same time could lead to inconsistent behavior.

