To engineer a Promise construct within JavaScript for managing AJAX procedures, you need to first understand both Promise and AJAX.

A Promise object in JavaScript represents the eventual completion (or failure) of an asynchronous operation and its resulting value. AJAX (Asynchronous JavaScript and XML), on the other hand, is a technology that enables web pages to be updated asynchronously by exchanging data with a web server behind the scenes. This means that it is possible to update parts of a webpage, without reloading the whole page.

Here is an example of how you could set up a Promise construct with an AJAX call:

```javascript
function getData(url) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        let xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
        xhr.open('GET', url);
        xhr.onreadystatechange = function() {
            if (xhr.readyState === 4) { // completed
                if (xhr.status === 200) { // ok
                    resolve(JSON.parse(xhr.response));
                } else { // error
                    reject('Error: ' + xhr.status);
                }
            }
        }
        xhr.send();
    });
}

let url = 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts'; // example url
getData(url)
    .then(data => console.log(data)) 
    .catch(error => console.log(error));
```
In the code above, we've created a `getData` function that takes in an URL for the AJAX call. This function returns a Promise that either resolves with the AJAX call response, or rejects with an error message. This allows the caller to handle both the completion and failure of the AJAX call, which is not possible with just a traditional callback-based AJAX call.

The key advantage of using Promises is that they make handling async tasks more composable and manageable, especially when dealing with multiple async operations. We can chain promises, use Promise.all() to handle multiple promises at once, and overall write more readable and less error-prone code.

