In most SQL systems, you can't conditionally rollback a transaction. Usually, you would check the condition before performing the operation. However, you can perform the delete operation inside a transaction, then rollback the transaction if needed. Please note that the query below will work only on DBMS like MySQL, SQL server, PostgreSQL, etc. that support transaction.

```sql
START TRANSACTION;

-- Get the population of Frankfurt before deletion
SELECT Population INTO @population FROM CITY WHERE city = 'Frankfurt';

-- Delete the city
DELETE FROM CITY WHERE city = 'Frankfurt';

-- Check the population and rollback if necessary
IF @population > 700000 THEN
    ROLLBACK;
ELSE
    COMMIT;
END IF;
```
Note: This is a pseudo-code. The actual SQL code might differ depending on the SQL dialect you're using (MySQL, Oracle, SQL Server, etc.) This code assumes that the column 'Population' exists in the table.

This script will start a transaction, perform the delete, and then either commit the transaction or rollback based on the condition.

If you are using a database system that does not support these transaction commands, you would normally perform the check in your application code before executing the delete statement. 

For instance, you would first execute a SELECT statement to get the population, check that in your application code, and then conditionally execute the DELETE statement based on the population size. This may not be ideal in some cases due to the delay between reading the population and deleting the row that might cause inconsistencies in a highly concurrent environment.  

Keep in mind that all the steps should run in a single transaction to prevent other queries from intervening between the operations.

