Sorting a multidimensional array can be a little tricky because sorting is usually applied to a one-dimensional (1D) array. However, one way to deal with multidimensional arrays is to view them as 1D arrays by simply focusing on one row or one column at a time. Below is the program that utilizes bubble sort and insertion sort.

```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

void BubbleSort(int arr[], int size) {
   for(int i = 0; i<size; i++) {
      bool swapped = false;
      for(int j = 0; j<size-i-1; j++) {
         if(arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {          
            swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);
            swapped = true;
         }
      }
      if(!swapped)
         break;
   }
}

void InsertionSort(int arr[], int size) {
   for (int i = 1; i < size; i++) {
      int key = arr[i];
      int j = i - 1;
      while (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {
         arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
         j = j - 1;
      }
      arr[j + 1] = key;
   }
}

int main() {
   int arr[3][3] = { {9, -3, 5}, {6, 8, -10}, {1, 3, 2} };
   int  row = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);  
   int  col = sizeof(arr[0])/sizeof(arr[0][0]);

   for(int i=0; i<row; i++) {
      // For even rows, sort using bubble sort
      if(i % 2 == 0) 
         BubbleSort(arr[i], col);
      // For odd rows, sort using insertion sort
      else
         InsertionSort(arr[i], col);
   }

   cout<<"Sorted multidimensional array is: "<<endl;
   for (int i=0; i<row; i++) {
      for(int j=0; j<col; j++)
         cout<<arr[i][j]<<" ";
      cout<<endl;
   }

   return 0;
}
```
In the above code, we are considering each row of the multidimensional array as separate 1D array and sorting them. For even rows, we are using Bubble Sort, and for odd rows, we are using Insertion Sort. If you have a 3D array or more, you can use similar concept to the above by considering each 2D array and sort accordingly.

