You can solve this problem by using a depth-first search (DFS) approach. Specifically, you start by creating a variable to store the cumulative sum. Then, you iterate through each element in the input array. If an element is a number, you add it to the cumulative sum. If an element is an array, you perform a recursive call to calculate its cumulative sum and add it to the overall cumulative sum. Consider the following JavaScript function that does just this:

```javascript
function cumulativeSum(array) {
    // Error handling for non-array input
    if (!Array.isArray(array)) {
        throw new Error("Input must be an array");
    }

    let sum = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
        if (Array.isArray(array[i])) {
            // Recursive call for inner arrays
            sum += cumulativeSum(array[i]);
        } else if (typeof array[i] === 'number') {
            // Checking for integers
            sum += array[i];
        } else {
            throw new Error("Array should contain only numbers or other arrays");
        }
    }

    return sum;
}
```

This function works for all 2D arrays (even if they're empty), and it performs error handling for situations where the array contains non-integer and non-array values. However, it also works with arrays that are more deeply embedded, not just 2D arrays. This is because of the recursive call in the for loop, which ensures that the function will also traverse any inner arrays within inner arrays, and so on.

