        if(b[i] != '\0')
            return false;
    return true;
}
```

Mistakes:
1. There's a logical issue in the nested loop where a matching character in the second string replaces the character. The inner-loop should break immediately after the replacement process to avoid replacing other similar characters.
2. The proposed solution will not work correctly if the first string conveys repeated characters. The algorithm would wrongly identify these as anagrams, even if the second string doesn't carry the same count of repeated characters.

Corrected C++ Code:

```cpp
bool isAnagram (string a, string b) {
    if(a.length() != b.length())
        return false;
    for(int i=0; i<a.length(); i++)
    {
        bool found = false;
        for(int j=0; j<b.length(); j++)
        {
            if(a[i] == b[j])
            {
                b[j] = '\0';
                found = true;
                break;
            }
        }
        if(!found)
            return false;
    }

    for(int i=0; i<b.length(); i++)
        if(b[i] != '\0')
            return false;

    return true;
}
```

