Below is a simple Java implementation of a Stack using a Linked List as requested:

```java
public class StackLL {

    // Inner class Node
    private static class Node {
        int data;
        Node next;
    }

    private Node top;
    private int size;

    // Constructor
    public StackLL() {
        top = null;
        size = 0;
    }

    // Check if the stack is empty
    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return (top == null);
    }

    // Return the size of the Stack
    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    // Push a value onto the Stack
    public void push(int data) {
        Node oldTop = top;
        top = new Node();
        top.data = data;
        top.next = oldTop;
        size++;
    }

    // Pop a value off the Stack
    // Throw EmptyStackException if the stack is empty
    public int pop() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            throw new java.util.EmptyStackException();
        }
        int data = top.data;
        top = top.next;
        size--;
        return data;
    }

    // Peek at the top of the Stack
    // Throw EmptyStackException if the stack is empty
    public int peek() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            throw new java.util.EmptyStackException();
        }
        return top.data;
    }
}
```

In this implementation, top always points to the most recently added node in the stack (the "top" of the stack), and size always tracks the current number of nodes in the stack. 

The `push()` method creates a new Node and makes it the new top of the stack. 

The `pop()` method first checks if the stack is empty and throws an exception if it is, and otherwise deletes the top Node and returns its data.

Finally, the `peek()` method only returns the data of the top Node without deleting it, also checking first if the stack is empty.

