Here is a walkthrough of the process in C++:

1. Include the required headers:

```cpp
#include <windows.h>
#include <iostream>
```

2. Declare the function to extract the resource:

```cpp
BOOL extractResource(const HINSTANCE hInstance, WORD resourceID, LPCTSTR filePath);
```

3. Define the function that extracts the resource:

 ```cpp
 BOOL extractResource(const HINSTANCE hInstance, WORD resourceID, LPCTSTR filePath) {
   HRSRC hResource = FindResource(hInstance, MAKEINTRESOURCE(resourceID), RT_RCDATA);
   if (!hResource)
      return FALSE;

   DWORD dwSize = SizeofResource(hInstance, hResource);
   if (dwSize == 0)
      return FALSE;

   const BYTE* pData = (const BYTE*)LoadResource(hInstance, hResource);
   if (!pData)
      return FALSE;

   // Create and write to the file
   HANDLE hFile = CreateFile(filePath, GENERIC_WRITE, 0, NULL, CREATE_ALWAYS, FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL, NULL);
   if (!hFile || hFile == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE)
      return FALSE;

   DWORD dwWritten = 0;
   BOOL result = WriteFile(hFile, pData, dwSize, &dwWritten, NULL);
   CloseHandle(hFile);

   return result;
}
```
4. Implement the `WinMain` function:

```cpp
int APIENTRY WinMain(HINSTANCE hInstance, HINSTANCE hPrevInstance, LPSTR lpCmdLine, int nCmdShow) {
   // Path to extract the resource
   TCHAR szFilePath[MAX_PATH];
   GetTempPath(MAX_PATH, szFilePath);
   lstrcat(szFilePath, TEXT("YourEmbeddedTool.exe"));

   // Extract the resource
   if (extractResource(hInstance, 101, szFilePath)) {
      // Execute the command line tool
      ShellExecute(NULL, NULL, szFilePath, NULL, NULL, SW_HIDE);
      // Potential pause to ensure process is finished before deletion
      Sleep(5000);
      // Delete the file
      DeleteFile(szFilePath);
   }

   return 0;
}
```

5. Add the command-line tool as a binary resource in the project. The resource type should be RCDATA and the resource ID should be 101 (as shown in the example).

The code assumes that you have access to a Windows API function `ShellExecute` that can run an executable file. The extracted executable file is written to the temporary directory and it is always deleted after execution. If you want the file to persist, just delete the line of code that does the deletion.

Note that this solution requires Read/Write permission in the directory where the executable is extracted. Also note that some antivirus programs might flag your executable since you are running an embedded program, so it is suggested to digitally sign your executable. 

