Here is a simple implementation of LRU cache using LinkedHashMap in Java:

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;
    private final Map<K, V> cache;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new LinkedHashMap<>(capacity, 0.75f, true) {
            protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry eldest) {
                return size() > LRUCache.this.capacity;
            }
        };
    }    

    public V get(K key) {
        return cache.getOrDefault(key, null);
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        cache.put(key, value);
    }

    public void remove(K key) {
         cache.remove(key);
    }
}
```

Here `capacity` is the maximum number of entries the cache can hold. Entries are added to the cache via the `put` method. When `get` is called, it returns the value associated with the key and moves the accessed entry to the end of the cache. Entries that haven't been accessed for the longest time are at the beginning of the LinkedHashMap, and they are the first to be removed when the cache is full. 

If you need to delete an entry before it naturally falls out of the cache, you can use the `remove` method.

In case the cache is full i.e., the number of entries equals the capacity, the `removeEldestEntry` method will be triggered every time we try entering a new value. This however does not remove the eldest entry until the size goes beyond its set capacity.

Please note that in the real world scenario, this cache would need to be more complex, depending on the use case. For example, it would likely need to be thread-safe if it were to be used in a multithreaded application.

