In general, it's not possible to distinguish how a process was stopped by another process - either by issuing a termination request (like via Task Manager), or by code exceptions, because all the external process sees is that the process has exited.

The only way to get this information is by catching the exit inside the process itself and log the reason for it's termination (exceptions etc). You can work on the ExitCode, but it is needed to set it manually on the exit.

Bear in mind that even a crash can also be seen as "exiting naturally". If the application crashes due to an unhandled exception, Windows will still set an exit code of `-532462766`(0xE0434F4D). But a process could also set that same exit code!

So your best option to distinguish an exit code from a crash is to make use of exit codes that are unlikely to be used by any other processes - stick to negative numbers or use high numbers from the entire range of UInt32. If you control both the starting and the stopping of the process, you could set a unique code for when the process is killed, and then check for this exit code.

Here is an example in the context of a console application that you control:

```c#
class Program {
    static int Main(string[] args)
    {
        AppDomain.CurrentDomain.ProcessExit += CurrentDomain_ProcessExit;
        try
        {
            throw new Exception();
            return 0; // Normal exit
        }
        catch
        {
            return 1; // Exception exit
        }
    }

    static void CurrentDomain_ProcessExit(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        // Process was killed
        Environment.ExitCode = 2;
    }
}
```

In the code above, an exit code of 2 would signify that the process was killed, 1 means that there was an exception, and 0 is a normal exit. 

This won't help if you're observing a process that you don't have control over, as there's no way to know what convention if any they're using for exit codes.

