Here is a simple implementation of a Doubly Linked List with a HashMap to optimize search operations. Hash Map stores the nodes of Linked List according to their values. 

While HashMap provides constant time complexity for searching operation, keep in mind that it also uses extra memory to store the same data, so it may not be efficient if memory footprint is a concern and the linked list is big.

```cpp
#include<unordered_map>
using namespace std;

//Node structure
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node* prev;
    Node(int val) : data(val), next(nullptr), prev(nullptr){}
};

//Doubly Linked List Class
class DoublyLinkedList {
    private:
        Node* head;
        Node* tail;
        unordered_map<int, Node*> nodeMap;
    public:
        DoublyLinkedList() : head(nullptr), tail(nullptr){}

        //Insert function
        void insert(int val) {
            Node* newNode = new Node(val);
            nodeMap[val] = newNode;
            if(head == nullptr){
                head = tail = newNode;
            }
            else {
                tail->next = newNode;
                newNode->prev = tail;
                tail = newNode;
            }
        }

        //Search function
        Node* search(int val) {
            if(nodeMap.find(val) != nodeMap.end())
                return nodeMap[val];
            else
                return nullptr;
        }

        //Delete function
        bool erase(int val) {
            Node* node = search(val);
            if(node == nullptr)
                return false;
            if(node->prev) 
                node->prev->next = node->next;
            if(node->next) 
                node->next->prev = node->prev;
            if(node == head) 
                head = node->next;
            if(node == tail) 
                tail = node->prev;
            nodeMap.erase(val);
            delete node;
            return true;
        }
};
```

This design assures O(1) for search function, while insert and erase function have also O(1) (assuming that hash function of unordered_map takes a constant time and do not cause rehashing). 

Please note that in reality there could be collision in hash map, which would result in more than O(1) operations. In addition, if number of erase operations is big, map could become sparse and cause use of extra memory. 

Also, while we assumed that the hash function operations are O(1), the cost of a good hash function often is more than O(1) operations. Hence, always take into consideration the trade-off between time and space complexity based on use case requirements.

Closures, in JavaScript, are functions that have access to the parent scope, even after the parent function has closed. They give you access to an outer function’s scope from an inner function. 

To break it down: 
- In JavaScript, variables are defined in their respective functions and they only exist within the function they're defined in, which is its scope. 
- Variables defined within a function are not accessible outside of that function, unless the function returns the value of that variable, or if a nested (inner) function accesses it.

This is where closures come in.

Closures make it possible for a function to have "private" variables. The function can access these variables privately, without exposing them to the rest of the code, which can prevent clashes with other parts of the script that might use the same variable names. 

That is, a closure allows a function to access all the variables, as well as other functions, that are in scope when the function is created. The function keeps this access even when called outside of that scope, preserving the environment it was created in.

Let's look at a quick example:

```javascript
function outerFunction() {
    let count = 0;
    return function innerFunction() {
        count++;
        return count;
    };
}

let instanceOne = outerFunction();

console.log(instanceOne()); // Outputs: 1
console.log(instanceOne()); // Outputs: 2

let instanceTwo = outerFunction();

console.log(instanceTwo()); // Outputs: 1
console.log(instanceOne()); // Outputs: 3
```

outerFunction() is a closure that includes the innerFunction and the "hidden" count variable that the innerFunction() has access to. 

Every time outerFunction is called, a new closure is created. So `instanceOne` and `instanceTwo` are different instances and do not share the `count`.

So, effectively, a JavaScript closure is a function (let's call it the inner function) that has access to its own scope, its outer function's scope and the global scope, along with access to the outer function's parameters and variables, even after the outer function has returned.

