This problem can be solved by using a Trie data structure, often used to store strings for efficient retrieval. The key point is to build the Trie in reverse order to facilitate suffix look-ups. Afterwards, we traverse the Trie to find the kth common suffix. 

Here is the JavaScript solution:

```javascript
class Node {
    constructor() {
        this.children = {};
        this.count = 0;
    }
}

class Trie {
    constructor() {
        this.root = new Node();
    }

    reverseInsert(word) {
        let node = this.root;
        for (let i = word.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (!(word[i] in node.children)) {
                node.children[word[i]] = new Node();
            }
            node = node.children[word[i]];
            node.count++;
        }
    }

    kthCommonSuffix(k) {
        let suffixes = [];
        this.dfs(this.root, '', suffixes, []);
        suffixes.sort((a, b) => b.count - a.count);
        if (k > suffixes.length) {
            return `Cannot find the ${k}th common suffix, only ${suffixes.length} common suffixes found.`;
        }
        return suffixes[k - 1].suffix;
    }

    dfs(node, suffix, suffixes, chars) {
        if (node !== this.root && Object.keys(node.children).length === 0) {
            suffixes.push({ suffix: chars.join(''), count: node.count });
        }
        for (let char in node.children) {
            this.dfs(node.children[char], char + suffix, suffixes, [char].concat(chars));
        }
    }
}

function kthCommonSuffix(strings, k) {
    let trie = new Trie();
    for (let string of strings) {
        trie.reverseInsert(string);
    }
    return trie.kthCommonSuffix(k);
}

console.log(kthCommonSuffix(["destruction", "auction", "constriction"], 2));
```

In the script, we construct a Trie class and a Node class. Each Node stores a 'children' object holding its child nodes and a 'count' indicating how many strings pass through the node. When we insert a string into our Trie, we reverse the string because we want to search suffixes. 

After all the strings are inserted, we perform a depth-first search to collect all nodes that are the end of a string. From these, we derive the common suffixes, and sort them in descending order by their counts. 

If k is larger than the actual count of the common suffixes, an error message is returned. Otherwise, we return the kth common suffix. 

This solution can handle large datasets relatively well because the time complexity mainly depends on the total length of all the strings, which is O(N) in the worst case scenario, where N is the total length of all strings. The memory complexity is also O(N).

