Here is a Java program that will effectuate a right twist operation on a 2D square grid. The grid is represented as a 2D array and the operation is performed using a combination of transpose and row reverse operations.

```Java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int N = 3;
        int[][] grid = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}};
        printGrid(grid, N);
        grid = rotateRight(grid, N);
        printGrid(grid, N);
    }

    // Function to print the grid
    public static void printGrid(int[][] grid, int N) {
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) {
                System.out.printf("%2d ", grid[i][j]);
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    // Function to rotate the matrix 90 degree clockwise
    public static int[][] rotateRight(int[][] grid, int N) {
        // Transpose the matrix
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            for (int j = i; j < N; j++) {
                int temp = grid[i][j];
                grid[i][j] = grid[j][i];
                grid[j][i] = temp;
            }
        }

        // Reverse each row
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < N / 2; j++) {
                int temp = grid[i][j];
                grid[i][j] = grid[i][N - 1 - j];
                grid[i][N - 1 - j] = temp;
            }
        }
        return grid;
    }
}
```

The provided code performs the required task of rotating a N x N matrix 90 degrees clockwise. This is done by first transposing the matrix (swapping rows with columns) and then reversing each row.

When you run this code with the sample grid as the initial grid, it first prints the initial grid, rotates it 90 degrees clockwise, then prints the rotated result. 

Please note that the size of the matrix N x N is fixed and is known in the beginning. A different approach might be needed if the size of the array is not known in the beginning.

