Yes, you can obtain host and port information from a `HttpContext` object in an ASP.NET Core web application through a middleware or in a controller action.

In your middleware or controller, inject an `IHttpContextAccessor` to get access to the `HttpContext`. Then, you can access the `Request` object which contains all the information about the HTTP request being processed.

Here's an example of accessing it in an API controller:

```csharp
[ApiController]
public class SomeController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("api/info")]
    public ActionResult<string> Get()
    {
        string host = Request.Host.Host;
        string port = Request.Host.Port.ToString();

        return Ok($"Host: {host}, Port: {port}");
    }
}
```

And if you want to do it in a middleware, you can access the `HttpContext` like this:

```csharp
public class Startup
{
    public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
    {
        app.Use(async (context, next) =>
        {
            string host = context.Request.Host.Host;
            string port = context.Request.Host.Port.ToString();

            // Do something with host and port

            await next();
        });

        // Other middleware
    }
}
```

In both cases, you get host and port information dynamically based on the running environment. Just remember that this information is extracted from the `HttpContext` of the incoming request, so it will change depending on the details of that specific request, including the host and port. These values reflect where the application is running, and how the client is accessing it.

