Below is a Java implementation of reversing a doubly linked list using a while loop.

Firstly, let's assume that we have this simple structure for Node and DoublyLinkedList:

```java
class Node<E> {
    E data;
    Node<E> next;
    Node<E> prev;

    Node(E data) {
        this.data = data;
    }
}

class DoublyLinkedList<E> {
    Node<E> head;
    Node<E> tail;

    void add(E data) {
        if (head == null) {
            head = new Node<>(data);
            tail = head;
        } else {
            Node<E> newTail = new Node<>(data);
            tail.next = newTail;
            newTail.prev = tail;
            tail = newTail;
        }
    }
}
```

Then, the solution for reversing the list:

```java
void reverse() {

    // Initialize current, previous and next pointers
    Node<E> current = head;
    Node<E> prev = null;
    Node<E> next = null;

    // Traverse through the list and swap previous and next pointers for each node
    while (current != null) {
        next = current.next;
        current.next = prev;
        current.prev = next;
        prev = current;
        current = next;
    }

    // Change head and tail pointers
    Node<E> temp = head;
    head = tail;
    tail = temp;
}
```

Finally, the main method to fill the list and reverse it:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
    DoublyLinkedList<Integer> doublyList = new DoublyLinkedList<>();
    doublyList.add(6);
    doublyList.add(9);
    doublyList.add(4);
    doublyList.add(8);
    doublyList.add(5);

    doublyList.reverse();
}
```

