        removeDuplicate(arr);
    }

    public static void removeDuplicate(int[][] arr){
        Set<Integer> set = new LinkedHashSet<Integer>();
        int[][] result = new int[arr.length][];
        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++){
            for(int j = 0; j < arr[i].length; j++){
                // add element to set, if it's already there it won't be added.
                set.add(arr[i][j]);
            }
            // convert the set to an array
            Integer[] temp = set.toArray(new Integer[0]);
            result[i] = new int[temp.length];

            // unboxing Integer values to int values
            for(int k = 0; k < temp.length; k++){
                result[i][k] = temp[k];
            }

            // clear the set for the next row
            set.clear();
        }

        // printing the result
        for(int i = 0; i < result.length; i++){
            System.out.println(Arrays.toString(result[i]));
        }
    }
}
```
The program maintains a `Set` for each row in the 2D array. A `Set` only stores unique elements so when values are added to it, it will automatically discard any duplicate values. The `LinkedHashSet` is used to maintain the order of the elements. The `Set` is then converted back into an array and stored in the result array. This process is repeated for each row in the array.

