Here is a Python solution using two pointers approach and a hashtable to store the count of uppercase and lowercase characters:

```python
def longest_nice_substring(s):
    max_length = 0
    result = ""
    for i in range(len(s)):
        upper = set()
        lower = set()
        for j in range(i, len(s)):
            if s[j].isupper():
                upper.add(s[j])
            else:
                lower.add(s[j].upper())
            if len(upper - lower) == 0 and len(lower - upper) == 0 and j - i + 1 > max_length:
                max_length = j - i + 1
                result = s[i: j + 1]
    return result
```

In this solution, a "sliding window" is created starting from each index of the string s. With each step we add the characters to the corresponding uppercase or lowercase set. If both sets are identical and the length of the current string is more than the maximum length found so far, we update the maximum length and save the current substring. At the end, the function returns the longest 'pleasant' substring found.

This function’s worst case time complexity is O(n^2), where n is the length of the string. This is because the function iterates over the string characters using two nested loops (simplifies to O(n^2) for time complexity). The space complexity would be O(n), in the worst case scenario - due to the storage of the characters in the upper and the lower set.

