One way you might achieve this is by using packet capture libraries such as Pcap.NET or SharpPcap. These libraries allow you to listen to the traffics your network interfaces receive. They are mostly used to create network analysis tools or hack tools. These libraries work at a very low level so you have greater control over the traffic.

They both share similar limitations:
- Requires admin privilege to run
- Might trigger anti-virus or firewall detection
- They listen on all the traffics not only for your app so you would need to filter them manually
- Works at very low level, you would need to inspect each packet then reassemble the HTTP requests.

Quick example of to use SharpPcap:
```CSharp
var devices = CaptureDeviceList.Instance;
var device = devices[0]; // we take the first network interface for demo purpose
device.OnPacketArrival += new PacketArrivalEventHandler(device_OnPacketArrival);
device.Open(DeviceMode.Promiscuous);
device.Capture();

private static void device_OnPacketArrival(object sender, CaptureEventArgs packet)
{
	// Parse the packet using PacketDotNet (a separate Nuget packet)
	var parsedPacket = Packet.ParsePacket(packet.Packet.LinkLayerType, packet.Packet.Data);
	var tcpPacket = PacketDotNet.TcpPacket.GetEncapsulated(parsedPacket);

	// You might want to check if this is HTTP etc...
}
```

Another technique would be to use a HTTP proxy such as Titanium-Web-Proxy or FiddlerCore SDK. They usually work by starting a local proxy server to inspect the HTTP(s) traffic. You route your HTTP requests through these proxies. They are much higher level than the packet capture libraries.

Quick example to start a proxy server using Titanium-Web-Proxy:
```CSharp
var proxyServer = new ProxyServer();

// locally trust root certificate used by this proxy 
proxyServer.CertificateManager.TrustRootCertificate();

// start proxy server
proxyServer.Start();

proxyServer.BeforeRequest += OnBeforeRequest;
proxyServer.BeforeResponse += OnBeforeResponse;

private async Task OnBeforeRequest(object sender, SessionEventArgs e)
{
	// print out the request header
	Console.WriteLine(e.WebSession.Request.Url);
}

private async Task OnBeforeResponse(object sender, SessionEventArgs e)
{
	// read response body as string
	if (e.WebSession.Request.Method == "GET" || e.WebSession.Request.Method == "POST")
	{
		if (e.WebSession.Response.ResponseStatusCode == "200")
		{
			byte[] bodyBytes = await e.GetResponseBody();
			e.WebSession.Response.Body = new MemoryStream(bodyBytes);

			string body = await e.GetResponseBodyAsString();
			Console.WriteLine(body);
		}
	}
}
```

Some disadvantages of HTTP proxy methods:
- Only works with HTTP(s), not other protocols, you might miss some traffics
- Browsers/apps usually warn users about untrusted proxies
- Some traffics might not respect the system proxy setting
- A lot of setups (installing, trusting certificates...)
- Need to handle SSL certs manually

