Unfortunately, the .NET standard `BinaryReader` does not support switching endian-ness and always reads in a little-endian format. So, for a big-endian stream it will always have to be converted. There is no option to specify the endian-ness in `BinaryReader`.

However, to simplify this, you could wrap the conversions into your own BinaryReader subclass, like this:

```csharp
public class BigEndianReader: BinaryReader
{
    public BigEndianReader(Stream input) : base(input) { }

    public override int ReadInt32()
    {
        byte[] bytes = base.ReadBytes(4);
        if (BitConverter.IsLittleEndian)
        {
            Array.Reverse(bytes);
        }
        return BitConverter.ToInt32(bytes, 0);
    }

    public override uint ReadUInt32()
    {
        byte[] bytes = base.ReadBytes(4);
        if (BitConverter.IsLittleEndian)
        {
            Array.Reverse(bytes);
        }
        return BitConverter.ToUInt32(bytes, 0);
    }

    // Implement more overloads if needed...
}
```
This way, you'll get the data exactly as you expect and if you need to ensure the same behaviour elsewhere in your code, you can use `BigEndianReader` exactly as you would use `BinaryReader`.

If efficiency is a big concern and you don't want the overhead from dealing with byte arrays, you could also process the bytes individually for each type. But as this gets repetitive with complex types, a general solution as above is usually sufficient.

