Your method of getting the file size using fseek() function and fgetpos() function should work fine on files that are larger than 4GB as fseek() and fgetpos() operate with long integers, not integers. Therefore, they can handle files of size up to 2^63-1 bytes under 64-bit platforms. But in case if fseek and fgetpos is not supporting larger files, you have the following workarounds:

1. fopen() the file in binary mode ("rb"). Use fread() to read the data in chunks (for example, in 1MB chunks) and count the total bytes read.

2. Use platform-specific functions to get the file size. In Windows, for example, you can use GetFileSizeEx() to get a file size that may exceed the range of a 32-bit integer. In Linux, you can use the stat() system call.

Here is an example of how to change the seek to handle larger files in a platform-independent way:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

#if defined(_WIN32) || defined(_WIN64)
#   include <windows.h>
#else
#   include <unistd.h>
#   include <sys/stat.h>
#endif

long long FileSize(const char *filename)
{
#if defined(_WIN32) || defined(_WIN64)
    HANDLE hFile = CreateFileA(filename, GENERIC_READ, 
        FILE_SHARE_READ | FILE_SHARE_WRITE, NULL, OPEN_EXISTING, 
        FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL, NULL);
    if (hFile==INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE) 
        return -1; // error condition, could call GetLastError to find out more

    LARGE_INTEGER size;
    if (!GetFileSizeEx(hFile, &size))
    {
        CloseHandle(hFile);
        return -1; // error condition, could call GetLastError to find out more
    }

    CloseHandle(hFile);
    return size.QuadPart;
#else
    struct stat st; 
    if (stat(filename, &st) == 0)
        return st.st_size;

    return -1; // error condition, could call strerror(errno) to find out more
#endif
}

int main()
{
    printf("%lld\n", FileSize("largefile"));
    return 0;
}
``` 

In this C example, it will correctly return the size of files larger than 4GB on both Windows and POSIX platforms (e.g., Linux, UNIX). On Windows, the CreateFileA(), GetFileSizeEx(), and CloseHandle() functions from the Windows API are used. On unix-based platforms stat is useful with st_size field.

Please remember this method can be used to get file size only, if you want to seek to any specific location, you will still need fseek() or fseeko().

