Here is how you can solve the problem in Go. The problem is: Given an array, we want to find the smallest cumulative value from any subset within the array. We can leverage DP (Dynamic Programming) to handle this problem.

First, you notice if the range of values inside nums is [-maxVal, maxVal]. The worst possible scenario to get a smallest cumulative value is -maxVal*n if there are negative values. If all numbers are zero or positive, then the result will be the smallest positive number. By using this fact, you can reduce the problem to a simpler one: Find a subset that is the closest to zero.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"math"
)

func Minsubarraysum(nums []int) int {
    totalSum := 0
    minNum := math.MaxInt64
    for _, num := range nums {
        totalSum += int(math.Abs(float64(num)))
        if num < minNum {
            minNum = num
        }
    }
    dp := make([]bool, totalSum+1)
    dp[0] = true

    for _, num := range nums {
        for i := totalSum; i >= int(math.Abs(float64(num))); i-- {
            dp[i] = dp[i] || dp[i-int(math.Abs(float64(num)))]
        }
    }

    result := totalSum
    for i := 0; i <= totalSum/2; i++ {
        if dp[i] {
            result = min(result, totalSum-2*i)
        }
    }

    if result == totalSum && minNum > 0 {
        return minNum
    }
    return -result
}

func min(a, b int) int {
    if a < b {
        return a
    }
    return b
}

func main() {
    fmt.Println(Minsubarraysum([]int{2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 4})) // Outputs 1
    fmt.Println(Minsubarraysum([]int{-1, -2, -3})) // Outputs -6
}
```
In the code above, the dp array represents whether a number can be produced by the subset of the array. The last loop aims to find the smallest non-negative number that can be formed by the subset of the array. Note that if there is a positive number in nums, the smallest cumulated value could be the smallest positive number of nums.

