This is a demanding task to accomplish without using any third-party libraries, but below is a rudimentary implementation in Java. We will use Java's built-in classes `ServerSocket` and `Socket` for networking and we will maintain a list of books as our simple collection.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import java.util.*;

class Book {
    String name;
    String author;
    Book(String name, String author) {
        this.name = name;
        this.author = author;
    }
}

public class SimpleHTTPServer {
    private static List<Book> bookList = new ArrayList<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ServerSocket server = new ServerSocket(8080);
        while (true) {
            Socket socket = server.accept();
            BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(
                    new InputStreamReader(socket.getInputStream()));
            DataOutputStream out = new DataOutputStream(socket.getOutputStream());

            String request = in.readLine();
            StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(request);

            String method = st.nextToken();
            String path = st.nextToken();

            switch (method) {
                case "GET":
                    if (path.equals("/books")) {
                        String books = "[";
                        for (Book book : bookList) {
                            books += "{" + "name:" + book.name + ", author:" + book.author + "},";
                        }
                        books += "]";
                        out.writeBytes("HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\n");
                        out.writeBytes("Content-Type: application/json\r\n");
                        out.writeBytes("Content-Length: " + books.length() + "\r\n");
                        out.writeBytes("\r\n");
                        out.writeBytes(books);
                    }
                    break;
                case "POST":
                    if (path.startsWith("/books?")) {
                        String query = path.split("/books?")[1];
                        String name = query.split("&")[0].split("=")[1];
                        String author = query.split("&")[1].split("=")[1];
                        bookList.add(new Book(name, author));
                        out.writeBytes("HTTP/1.1 201 Created\r\n");
                        out.writeBytes("\r\n");
                    }
                    break;
                case "PUT":
                case "DELETE":
                    // similar to POST
                    break;
                default:
                    out.writeBytes("HTTP/1.1 405 Method Not Allowed\r\n");
                    out.writeBytes("\r\n");
                    break;
            }
            socket.close();
        }
    }
}
```

This server will accept GET requests at path "/books" and will return in JSON format a list of stored books. It accepts POST requests at path "/books", and expects query parameters "name" and "author" to create a new book.

Handling PUT and DELETE requests has been omitted for brevity, but their implementation would function analogously to POST.

Do note that this is an overly simplified example. A real-world HTTP server requires much more complexity, like parsing headers and handling various edge cases. Moreover, this code is blocking and can only serve one client at a time.

To test the server, you can either use a tool like `curl`:

```sh
curl -X POST "http://localhost:8080/books?name=Java&author=Sun"
curl -X GET "http://localhost:8080/books"
```

Or use a browser and visit `http://localhost:8080/books`

