Here's a step by step guide to creating a simple React Redux application to help you manage a list of messages. 

First, install necessary packages. You can install Redux and React-Redux using npm or yarn. 

```
npm install --save redux react-redux
```

Next, create an action to add a message:

```javascript
export const addMessage = message => {
  return {
    type: 'ADD_MESSAGE',
    message
  }
}
```

Next, create a reducer to handle adding a new message:

```javascript
const messages = (state = [], action) => {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'ADD_MESSAGE':
      return [...state, action.message];
    default:
      return state;
  }
}
```

Now, combine reducers and create the Redux store. Though we only have a single reducer here, for larger apps there would be multiple reducers:

```javascript
import { createStore, combineReducers } from 'redux';
import messages from './reducers';

const rootReducer = combineReducers({
  messages
});

export default createStore(rootReducer);
```

Next, wrap your app with the Provider component from react-redux and pass in your store:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';
import { Provider } from 'react-redux';
import store from './store';
import App from './App';

ReactDOM.render(
  <Provider store={store}>
    <App />
  </Provider>,
  document.getElementById('root')
);
```

Then, make a Messages component that will display messages from the Redux store:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { useSelector } from 'react-redux';

const Messages = () => {
  const messages = useSelector(state => state.messages);

  return (
    <ul>
      {messages.map((message, index) => (
        <li key={index}>{message}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

export default Messages;
```

Lastly, create a component to add a new message:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { useDispatch } from 'react-redux';
import { addMessage } from './actions';

const AddMessage = () => {
  const [message, setMessage] = useState('')
  const dispatch = useDispatch();

  const handleSubmit = () => {
    dispatch(addMessage(message));
    setMessage('');
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <input 
        type="text" 
        value={message} 
        onChange={(e) => setMessage(e.target.value)} 
      />
      <button onClick={handleSubmit}>Add Message</button>
    </div>
  )
}

export default AddMessage;
```

In this code, useDispatch refers to the dispatch function that you use to dispatch actions to the Redux store. useSelector is a hook that allows you to extract data from the Redux store state.

Remember to put these components together in your App component. This is a basic example of using Redux and React together. 

This code doesn't include setting up the rest of your React application or configuring Babel and Webpack. Redux also offers tools for logging, hot-reloading, and handling asynchronous actions, not discussed here. Read the Redux documentation and the React-Redux documentation for more details.

