Cloning a WPF object while preserving databindings isn’t a straight-forward task because WPF databindings are not typically serialized, thus not preserved during a deep clone. You can still clone a WPF object, but databindings would be lost.

However, you can use XamlWriter.Save to serialize and XamlReader.Parse to deserialize the WPF object, while also using the ExpressionConverter approach to preserve databindings. This isn't a full proof method because it may break in complex scenarios due to limitations of XamlWriter.

For example:

```csharp
    public static T Clone<T>(T source)
    {
        string objectXaml = XamlWriter.Save(source);
        StringReader stringReader = new StringReader(objectXaml);
        XmlTextReader xmlReader = new XmlTextReader(stringReader);
        T clonedObject = (T)XamlReader.Load(xmlReader);
        return clonedObject;
    }
```

In the above code, we save the source object into a XAML string using `XamlWriter.Save`. We then use `XamlReader.Load` to recreate the object. However, this method doesn't preserve data bindings.

To keep data bindings, you would have to implement an `ExpressionConverter` to manage the serialization of data bindings. This is quite complex and goes beyond a simple example. For a guide on how to do this, follow the link given in your post.

Additionally, remember you should only clone objects that are meant to be duplicated. Some objects may not behave as expected when cloned, especially those which maintain unique identities. And as always, by cloning an object you are doubling the memory usage for that object, which can be a concern in memory-sensitive applications.

