Singleton pattern, a member of the Gang of Four design patterns, allows you to have one and only one instance of an object in the whole JVM. Singleton pattern should be used when a class must ensure that only a single instance should be created and a single object can be used by all other classes.

Here is a step by step guide to implement the Singleton design pattern in Java.

Step 1: Declare the constructor of the class as private. This will prevent the instantiation of the class from any other class.

```java
public class Singleton {
    private Singleton() {
        // private constructor
    }
}

```
Step 2: Declare a private static object of the same class. This object will be the only object of the class.
```java
public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton single_instance = null;

    private Singleton() {
        // private constructor
    }
}
```
Step 3: Create a public static method (also known as static factory method) which returns the single_instance object. See the Singleton object creation inside if block, this is the critical step which ensures that only one instance of Singleton is created. This method provides the global point of access to the Singleton object and can be used to get the instance while being sure that there are no duplicate instances.
```java

public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton single_instance = null;

    private Singleton() {
        // private constructor
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if(single_instance == null)
            single_instance = new Singleton();
        return single_instance;
    }
}
```
Above code gives you thread-unsafe Singleton, if using multiple threads you should ensure thread-safety by using synchronized keyword or double-checked locking.

Here is the thread-safe version of the Singleton class:
```java
public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton single_instance = null;

    private Singleton() {
        // private constructor
    }

    // Thread Safe Singleton
    public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
        if(single_instance == null)
            single_instance = new Singleton();
        return single_instance;
    }
}
```
The above arrangement works perfectly fine in the single-threaded environment, but when it comes to multithreaded systems, it can cause issues if multiple threads are inside the if loop at the same time. 

To manage the Singleton pattern with multiple threads, we can use "Double Checked Locking". Before Java 5, this method can cause issues, but now it's fixed:

```java
public class Singleton {
    private volatile static Singleton single_instance;

    private Singleton() {
        // private constructor
    }

    // Thread Safe Singleton
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if(single_instance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if(single_instance == null)
                    single_instance = new Singleton();
            }
        }
        return single_instance;
    }
}
```
The volatile keyword ensures that multiple threads handle the uniqueInstance variable correctly when it is being initialized to the Singleton instance.

Remember:
Singleton pattern restricts the instantiation of a class and ensures that only one instance of the class exists in the Java virtual machine. It seems to be a very simple design pattern but when it comes to implementation, it comes with a lot of implementation concerns.

