`-i inplace` option in `awk` is used to edit files in place. However, the mechanism of "in place" editing in awk is a bit complicated, it doesn't directly insert data into original file, instead, it creates a temporary file, writes the data into that, and then renames that file to the original file.

However, when using `BEGIN` block in `awk`, it produces the output before processing the lines in the input file. But `-i inplace` option does not apply to the lines produced by `BEGIN` block. Hence, you do not see the line added by `BEGIN` block in the file, you only see it on the shell output.

So, if you want to add a line at the top of a file, you would need to create a new file, place your new line into that, then append your old file into new one, like this:

```bash
echo "first line" > newfile.txt;
cat file.txt >> newfile.txt;
mv newfile.txt file.txt;
```

This will add the line "first line" to the top of `file.txt`.

Although `awk` is a very powerful tool, sometimes we have to resort to other shell commands and operations to achieve what we need. In this case, combination of `echo`, `cat` and `mv` commands may be easier to understand and manage for file editing task like this.

