```java
public boolean correctParenthesis(String parentheses) {
    Stack<Character> stack = new Stack<Character>();
    for (int i = 0; i < parentheses.length(); i++) {
        char c = parentheses.charAt(i);

        if (c == '(') {
            stack.push(c);
        } else if (c == ')') {
            if (stack.empty()) {
                return false; // Closing parenthesis found but no opening one
            } else {
                stack.pop(); // Match found so pop from the stack
            }
        }
    }

    // If stack is not empty then there are unmatched opening parenthesis
    return stack.empty();
}
```
This Java code works by keeping track of the parentheses in the input string. 

It creates a stack and for each character in the string, if it's an opening parenthesis it gets added ("pushed") onto the stack. 

If it's a closing parenthesis, the code checks to see if the stack is empty or not. If it is empty then the closing parenthesis does not have a matching opening parenthesis and false is returned. If it is not empty then there must have been an opening parenthesis and this can be "popped" off the stack. 

Finally, it checks if the stack is empty. If it is not, then there are unmatched opening parenthesis and false is returned. 

So it always returns true only when every opening parenthesis in the input string has a matching closing parenthesis.

Here's a simple implementation in Java that follows the rules of the Collatz sequence while maintaining an ordered list of odd numbers.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Solution {

    public List<Integer> getOddCollatz(int n) {
        List<Integer> oddNumbers = new ArrayList<Integer>();
        while(n != 1){
            // If n is odd, add it to the list
            if(n % 2 != 0){
                oddNumbers.add(n);
            }
            // Apply the Collatz operations
            n = n % 2 == 0 ? n/2 : 3*n + 1;
        }
        // Add 1 at the end, as Collatz sequence always ends at 1
        oddNumbers.add(1);

        // Sorting the list in ascending order
        Collections.sort(oddNumbers);
        return oddNumbers;
    }
}
```

The provided method first checks if `n` is odd. If it is, it's added to the `oddNumbers` list. Then, the next number in the sequence is calculated. 

The process is repeated as long as `n` is not equal to 1. When `n` becomes 1, it's added to the `oddNumbers` list as, according to the Collatz conjecture, every sequence ends with the number 1. 

Finally, the `Collections.sort()` function is used to sort the list in ascending order before the list is returned.

