Here's a simplistic GO microservice example that meets your criteria. This service is using the Eclipse Paho MQTT client for data acquisition, and InfluxDB as the time-series database. We assume that the MQTT messages are in JSON format, and that's what we will try to parse/transform.

```go
package main

import (
	mqtt "github.com/eclipse/paho.mqtt.golang"
	influxdb2 "github.com/influxdata/influxdb-client-go/v2"
	"encoding/json"
	"time"
)

type Data struct {
	Timestamp time.Time `json:"timestamp"`
	Value     float64   `json:"value"`
}

var client mqtt.Client
var influxClient influxdb2.Client

func main() {
	// Create InfluxDB Client
	influxClient = influxdb2.NewClient("http://localhost:8086", "my-token")

	// Create MQTT client options
	opts := mqtt.NewClientOptions().AddBroker("tcp://localhost:1883")
	opts.SetClientID("go_mqtt_client")

	// Set callback function for incoming messages
	opts.SetDefaultPublishHandler(func(client mqtt.Client, msg mqtt.Message) {
		processMessage(msg)
	})

	// Create MQTT client and connect
	client = mqtt.NewClient(opts)
	if token := client.Connect(); token.Wait() && token.Error() != nil {
		panic(token.Error())
	}

	// Subscribe to topic
	if token := client.Subscribe("go_topic", 0, nil); token.Wait() && token.Error() != nil {
		panic(token.Error())
	}

	// Just keep the program running
	select {}
}

func processMessage(msg mqtt.Message) {
	// Unmarshal the JSON data
	var data Data
	if err := json.Unmarshal(msg.Payload(), &data); err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}

	// Use InfluxDB's API to create a write API
	writeAPI := influxClient.WriteAPIBlocking("my-org", "my-bucket")

	// Create a point and add to batch
	p := influxdb2.NewPoint(
		"my_measurement",
		nil,
		map[string]interface{}{"value": data.Value},
		data.Timestamp,
	)
	// Write the data
	if err := writeAPI.WritePoint(p); err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
}
```

This example does everything in the MQTT message callback function for simplicity purposes. In a real-world scenario, you would likely want to separate parts of this code into different services within your microservice to maintain robustness and avoid any performance issues. A multi-stage pipeline with message queues could be implemented to handle processing, transformation and data insertion stages independently. This would not only make the service more flexible, but also more resilient in the face of errors and recovery from failures.

