You can use Java's built-in ExecutorService and CountDownLatch to parallelize the code. Here's how you might do it:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class ParallelProcess {

    private static final int NTHREDS = 10;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(NTHREDS);
        CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(1000 * 1000);

        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < 1000; j++) {
                Runnable worker = new MyRunnable(latch);
                executor.execute(worker);
            }
        }

        latch.await();  // wait until latch counted down to 0
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}

class MyRunnable implements Runnable {
    private final CountDownLatch latch;

    MyRunnable(CountDownLatch latch) {
        this.latch = latch;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(10);
            System.out.println("hi");
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        } finally {
            latch.countDown();
        }
    }
}
```

This piece of code creates a pool of threads and each thread will execute an instance of `MyRunnable`. `CountDownLatch` ensures that the main thread waits for all the worker threads to finish their execution.

It's worth to mention that we have a `InterruptedException` inside the `run()` method. It's a good practice to interrupt the current thread again to let the caller know about the interruption.

The value of `NTHREDS` determines the number of threads in the pool. The optimal value largely depends on the number of available cores and the nature of the tasks.

