Below is a simple implementation of multithreading in Java where two threads are trying to increment the same variable. This scenario could lead to a race condition:

```java
class Counter {
    int count = 0;

    public void increment() {
        count++;
    }
}

class Task implements Runnable {
    Counter counter;

    public Task(Counter counter) {
        this.counter = counter;
    }

    public void run() {
        for(int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
            counter.increment();
        }
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Counter counter = new Counter();

        Thread thread1 = new Thread(new Task(counter));
        Thread thread2 = new Thread(new Task(counter));

        thread1.start();
        thread2.start();

        try {
            thread1.join();
            thread2.join();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        System.out.println("Final count is " + counter.count);
    }
}
```

In this code, there is a possibility that the two threads could call `counter.increment()` at the same time, resulting in missing a count. This is a race condition. 

To prevent this, we can enforce mutual exclusion by making the increment function synchronized:

```java
public synchronized void increment() {
    count++;
}
```

Now on the Runnable interface vs the Thread class in Java:

- The Thread class provides more intrinsic capabilities like `stop()`, `suspend()`, `resume()`, etc. compared to the Runnable interface which only has the `run()` method. However, many of the methods of the Thread class are depricated.
- The Runnable interface represents a task that can be executed concurrently, while the Thread class represents a thread of execution. So if we want to represent a task which can be executed by multiple threads, Runnable is a better choice.
- For the Runnable interface, you can run many tasks on the same thread, which will help in better resource utilization. However, if you need to have separate threads, you will need to define a Thread for each task.
- In terms of code structuring and object-oriented programming, Runnable is preferred because Java does not support multiple inheritances. If you extend the Thread class, you cannot extend any other class, while you can still implement multiple interfaces in Java.

The impact of these two in a multithreaded environment also varies:
- If you extend the Thread class, each of your threads has a unique object associated with it, whereas if you implement Runnable, many threads can share the same object instance, making it easier to achieve inter-thread communication.

Therefore, in most cases, implementing the Runnable interface might be a more preferable choice for many programmers due to its advantages in terms of resource sharing, code structuring and offering the flexibility of extending from other classes.

