In C# and .NET, you can accomplish this by defining a compilation symbol for your custom configuration and then using preprocessor directives in your code to conditionally compile or not compile certain code, based on the symbol you defined.

Here is an example of how to do this:

1. First, define a compilation symbol for your custom solution configuration in Visual Studio.

    - Right-click your project in Solution Explorer and select `Properties`.
    - Go to the `Build` tab.
    - Locate the `Conditional compilation symbols` field under `General`.
    - Enter the name of your custom solution configuration, e.g., `MY_CUSTOM_CONFIG`.

2. Second, use the `#if`, `#else`, `#elif` (optional), and `#endif` preprocessor directives in your code to conditionally compile different pieces of code based on the presence or absence of your custom symbol.

```csharp
// This line of code will only be compiled if `MY_CUSTOM_CONFIG` is defined.
#if MY_CUSTOM_CONFIG
    Console.WriteLine("Custom configuration is enabled.");
#else
    Console.WriteLine("Custom configuration is not enabled.");
#endif
```

Remember that the symbol defined is based on the configuration you're working on (Debug, Release, or your custom configuration); you should ensure the correct configuration is selected when you want to check its value.

Also, this check is done during compile-time, not run-time. This means that if the code gets compiled, it won't "disappear" if you change the configuration during run-time; the compiled output is specific to the configuration it was compiled with. 

If you need to check the configuration during runtime, you should normally store this configuration in a config file or database and read it at the beginning of your program's execution.

If you still really need this, one way (it's crude but works), is to set a variable that depends on your configuration.

```csharp
public class Program
{
    #if MY_CUSTOM_CONFIG
    public static bool IsCustomConfig = true;
    #else
    public static bool IsCustomConfig = false;
    #endif

    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        if (IsCustomConfig)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Custom configuration is enabled.");
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Custom configuration is not enabled.");
        }
    }
}
```

This way the value is baked in at compile time but readable at runtime.

