Yes, this can be done using cURL in PHP. You can make a cURL request and can set the timeout to 1 second to prevent waiting for the response. This method relies on the fact that web servers close the connection as soon as they receive the request, even if they haven't sent a response yet.

Here's how it can be done:

```php
function fire_and_forget($url, $params)
{
    $post_params = array();
    foreach ($params as $key => &$val) {
        $post_params[] = $key . '=' . urlencode($val);
    }
    $post_string = implode('&', $post_params);

    $parts=parse_url($url);

    $fp = fsockopen($parts['host'],
        isset($parts['port'])?$parts['port']:80,
        $errno, $errstr, 1);

    $out = "POST ".$parts['path']." HTTP/1.1\r\n";
    $out.= "Host: ".$parts['host']."\r\n";
    $out.= "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\r\n";
    $out.= "Content-Length: ".strlen($post_string)."\r\n";
    $out.= "Connection: Close\r\n\r\n";
    if (isset($post_string)) $out.= $post_string;

    fwrite($fp, $out);
    fclose($fp);
}

//you can call this function for HTTP request
fire_and_forget('http://www.example.com', ['foo' => 'bar']);
```
Please note that this solution does not work in some cases for servers which are not configured to close the connection immediately after receiving the request. They will wait for the script to time out before closing, so the request will not truly be "fire and forget". 

Please be aware that abusing this technique could be construed as a denial of service attack, causing the server to spawn processes which are then left waiting. Always ensure the server, you're calling in such a way, is either your own or can handle such requests.

Here is a solution to the described problem:

First, we define our subclasses, that inherit from the Animal class and implement the makeSound() method, which is an abstract method in the Animal base class:
```java
public class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("The dog barks");
    }
}

public class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("The cat meows");
    }
}

public class Fish extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        // Fish don't make sound
        System.out.println("The fish doesn't make sound");
    }
}
```

Thereafter, we can demonstrate the use of polymorphism. This is done by creating an ArrayList of animals (which is possible because Dog, Cat, and Fish all extend Animal) and calling each animal's makeSound() method:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class MainClass {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Animal> animals = new ArrayList<>();
        animals.add(new Dog());
        animals.add(new Cat());
        animals.add(new Fish());

        for (Animal animal : animals) {
            animal.makeSound();
        }
    }
}
```
The program will create three types of animals and add them to the list. Even though the actual type of objects are all different (Dog, Cat, Fish), they can be referred to as being the Animal type. This is called as polymorphism. The JVM automatically calls the overriding method of the respective class, which results in the dog barking, the cat meowing and the fish not making any sound. 

So in this program, we can see:
- The use of Inheritance: The Dog, Cat, and Fish classes all inherit from the Animal class.
- The use of Polymorphism: The Animal reference could refer to an object of any of its subclasses.

This is an example of the "Template Method" design pattern, where the superclass (Animal) provides a base structure (template) of methods that the subclasses (Dog, Cat, Fish) will each implement in their unique way.

