The Compact Framework 2.0 doesn't provide inbuilt support for creating live views/partitions on collections. So you'd need to create a wrapper class that provides a view on a subsection of the list.

Below is a simple implementation of this concept:

```csharp
public class ListView<T> : IList<T>
{
    private IList<T> wrappedList;
    private int fromIndex, count;

    public ListView(IList<T> wrappedList, int fromIndex, int count)
    {
        this.wrappedList = wrappedList;
        this.fromIndex = fromIndex;
        this.count = count;
    }

    public T this[int index] 
    {
        get { return wrappedList[fromIndex + index]; } 
        set { throw new NotSupportedException(); } 
    }

    public int Count{ get { return count; }}

    // Other IList<T> methods would also be implemented by forwarding to wrappedList

    public bool IsReadOnly { get { return true; } }

    //... exceptions are thrown for all modification methods
}
```

This is still a live view on your IList> with all changes in your original list reflected here, just like ReadOnlyCollection. It doesn't copy any elements into a new array.

However, the disadvantage here is that if you insert elements into your original list, it might mess up this view. It might be started from wrong index, and have a wrong Count. This might be solved by keeping track of all views on a list and updating them whenever the list is modified, but Compact Framework 2.0 doesn't support events which are necessary for that. You need to manage how to maintain the list yourself carefully and correctly.

Note: All changes applied to original IList<> will be reflected on this custom ListView<> and vice versa, and it will also throw exceptions if any modifying methods are called, thus it is indeed read-only.

