Here is an iterative function to find the maximum product of non-contiguous elements in an array:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

// Function to compute multiplication of a maximum subset of non-contiguous elements
long long maxProductSubset(vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();

    // Special case: If size is 1 then return the only element present.
    if (n == 1)
        return arr[0];

    // Initialize maximum and minimum product from single array element
    long long maxVal = arr[0];
    long long minVal = arr[0];

    // Initialize maximum product
    long long maxProduct = arr[0];

    // Traverse through the array. Following rules:
    // If number is negative, maxVal and minVal swapping their values
    // maxVal/ minVal stores the product of maximum / minimum product ending at current position
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        if (arr[i] < 0)
            swap(maxVal, minVal);

        // maxVal and minVal are updated considering current element
        maxVal = max((long long)arr[i], maxVal * arr[i]);
        minVal = min((long long)arr[i], minVal * arr[i]);

        // Max Product of array is maximum of maxProduct and maxVal
        maxProduct = max(maxProduct, maxVal);
    }

    // Return maximum product.
    // It can never be 0 or negative, as we care only on non-contiguous elements and can ignore a negative.
    return maxProduct;
}
```
The function uses a dynamic programming approach, where at each position in the array it calculates the maximum and minimum product ending at that position. Note, as this solution only takes non-contiguous elements for product, we can ignore negative values, i.e., not to omit next element even if it is negative. It finds the overall maximum of these local maximum products.

Please note, std::vector's max()/min() methods could throw an exception for empty vectors. So never pass an empty vector to this method.

