Folder redirection can change the actual location where the Desktop and other special folders are stored. When you use `Environment.GetFolderPath(Environment.SpecialFolder.Desktop)`, it tries to retrieve the physical path on the local machine. But, because you have folder redirection in place, this doesn't return the actual path to the redirected folder sitting on a server.

Instead, try using the `SHGetKnownFolderPath` function (from Shell32.dll), which replaced the older `SHGetFolderPath`. This function directly interfaces with the Shell API, and should handle redirects correctly.

Here is sample code which makes use of this:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

public static class FolderPaths
{
    public static string GetDesktopFolderPath()
    {
        IntPtr outPath;
        int result = SHGetKnownFolderPath(new Guid("{B4BFCC3A-DB2C-424C-B029-7FE99A87C641}"), 0, IntPtr.Zero, out outPath);
        if (result >= 0)
        {
            return Marshal.PtrToStringUni(outPath);
        }
        else
        {
            throw new ExternalException("SHGetKnownFolderPath failed.", result);
        }
    }

    [DllImport("Shell32.dll")]
    private static extern int SHGetKnownFolderPath([MarshalAs(UnmanagedType.LPStruct)]Guid rfid, uint dwFlags, IntPtr hToken,
            out IntPtr ppszPath);
}
```

In the above code, the GUID `{B4BFCC3A-DB2C-424C-B029-7FE99A87C641}` represents the Desktop folder. You can find other GUIDs for other special folders in the official [documentation](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/shell/knownfolderid).

A constructor in C++ is a special member function of a class which is used to initialize the class objects. A constructor is called automatically when an object is created. 

Three broad categories of constructors exist in C++:

1. Default Constructors: They do not take any arguments. Their function is to provide default values for object attributes at the time of object creation. 

```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass()  {   // default constructor
        // initialization or default values
    }
};
```

2. Parameterized Constructors: They accept at least one or more parameters to initialize the object with specific values at the time of creation.

```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    int x;

    MyClass(int val) {  // parameterized constructor
        x = val;
    }
};
```

3. Copy Constructors: They are used to initialize a new object as a copy of an existing object. The copy constructor's parameter is a reference to an object of the same class.

```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    int x;

    MyClass(const MyClass &obj) {  // copy constructor
        x = obj.x;
    }
};
```

Constructor Overloading in C++:
In C++, we can have more than one constructor in the same class, having different parameters (i.e., their type, order, or number can be different). This is known as Constructor Overloading. It is a kind of polymorphism where the constructor function is overloaded to initialize objects in different ways.

```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    int x;

    MyClass() {  // default constructor
        x = 0;
    }

    MyClass(int val) {  // parameterized constructor
        x = val;
    }

    MyClass(const MyClass &obj) {  // copy constructor
        x = obj.x;
    }
};
```

If a constructor is declared private, the implications are as follows:
- The class cannot be inherited.
- Objects for that class cannot be created outside the class, only inside the class or friend functions or classes.
- Mostly, private constructors are used in Singleton Design Pattern where only one instance of the class is allowed.

```cpp
class MyClass {
private:
    MyClass() {}   // private constructor
public:
    static MyClass* getInstance() {
        static MyClass instance;  
        return &instance;
    }
};
``` 

In the above code, getInstance() is a public method which is used to initialize a single instance of the class. This is Singleton Design Pattern.

