Here's a Java function that uses recursion to generate all non-repeating permutations of a string. Then it uses a HashSet to store and compare the permutations of the two strings. 

Please note that this function works efficiently for small strings only. The time complexity for generating permutations of a string of length n is roughly O(n!), so it can get out of hand quickly with long strings.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static HashSet<String> commonPermutations(String s1, String s2) {
        HashSet<String> set1 = new HashSet<>(), set2 = new HashSet<>();
        permute(s1, 0, s1.length() - 1, set1);
        permute(s2, 0, s2.length() - 1, set2);

        // Retain only the common permutations
        set1.retainAll(set2);
        return set1;
    }

    private static void permute(String s, int l, int r, HashSet<String> set) {
        if (l == r) {
            set.add(s);
        } else {
            for (int i = l; i <= r; i++) {
                s = swap(s, l, i);
                permute(s, l + 1, r, set);
                s = swap(s, l, i);
            }
        }
    }

    private static String swap(String s, int i, int j) {
        char[] arr = s.toCharArray();
        char temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp;
        return String.valueOf(arr);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(commonPermutations("abc", "cab"));
    }
}
```

This program will first generate all permutations of both strings, and then use the HashSet method `retainAll` to keep only the permutations that exist in both sets. It will then print out the common permutations of the two input strings. 

In the main function, you can replace `"abc"` and `"cab"` with any other two strings to test the function with different inputs.

Please be aware of that: This will not work for longer strings due to the computation limits and the nature of permutation calculations can occupy a lot of memory very quickly. Thus better algorithms need to be used for longer strings.

