2. `.pop()` - This method removes the last element from an array and returns that element.

3. `.shift()` - This method removes the first element from an array and returns that removed element.

4. `.unshift()` - This method adds one or more elements to the beginning of an array and returns the new length of the array.


Below is a code demonstrating their behavior:

```js
let array = ["element1", "element2", "element2"];

// Use of .push()
array.push("element3");
console.log(array); // Output: ["element1", "element2", "element2","element3"]

// Use of pop()
array.pop();
console.log(array); // Output: ["element1", "element2", "element2"]

// Use of shift()
array.shift();
console.log(array); // Output: ["element2", "element2"]

// Use of unshift()
array.unshift("element0");
console.log(array); // Output: ["element0", "element2", "element2"]
```
Even if the array is empty, these methods still work. For push and unshift, they will add elements into the array. However, for pop and shift, they will return 'undefined' because there are no elements to remove.

Impacts on Time Complexity:

- `.push()`: This method has O(1) time complexity, it only adds elements to the end of the array.
- `.pop()`: This method also has O(1) time complexity, it only needs to remove an element at the end of the array.
- `.shift()`: This method has O(n) time complexity because when an element is removed from the beginning of an array, it has to re-index all the other elements.
- `.unshift()`: This method also has O(n) time complexity because it adds an element to the beginning and then re-indexes the other elements.

