In Java, string concatenation using the "+" operator creates several intermediate String objects in each concatenation operation, which can lead to unnecessary overhead in terms of memory and performance in situations where large amounts of strings are being concatenated, especially in a loop.

On the other hand, StringBuilder is mutable so it eliminates the need for creating intermediate String objects. When appending, it just adds to the existing character array, if the capacity is sufficient; otherwise it increases the capacity.

Memory wise, "+" operator leads to more usage due to the creation of multiple intermediate String objects. Performance wise, StringBuilder is always faster than using the "+" operator for large numbers of concatenations because it directly operates on the character array, thus minimizing overhead.

Here is a comparative code snippet:

```java
public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Using "+" operator
    long start1 = System.currentTimeMillis();
    String s = "";
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
        s += "randomString";
    }
    long end1 = System.currentTimeMillis();
    System.out.println("Time taken for '+' operator: " + (end1 - start1) + " ms");

    // Using StringBuilder
    long start2 = System.currentTimeMillis();
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
        sb.append("randomString");
    }
    String s2 = sb.toString();
    long end2 = System.currentTimeMillis();
    System.out.println("Time taken for StringBuilder: " + (end2 - start2) + " ms");
  }
}
```
In the generated bytecode of String concatenation using "+", you can see the usage of StringBuilder. But it initializes a new StringBuilder each time which leads to many unnecessary objects, while when you use StringBuilder directly, you initialize it only once. So, when performing string concatenation inside a loop or a method called many times, prefer using StringBuilder or StringBuffer.

To optimize string concatenation for high-performance systems, use StringBuilder explicitly when concatenating in a loop or use the "+" operator when concatenating a known/small number of Strings. Leverage the StringBuilder's "ensureCapacity" method for preventing unnecessary reallocations. Also, use the "setLength" method to clear the contents of the StringBuilder and reuse it to further speed up the performance.

