You can use the ManualResetEvent or AutoResetEvent classes in .NET. 

Here is an example:

```csharp
class Program
{
    private ManualResetEvent _shutdownEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private Thread _thread;

    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Program p = new Program();
        p.StartThread();
    }

    void StartThread()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(DoWork);
        _thread.Start();

        Console.ReadLine();  // Wait for user input

        _shutdownEvent.Set();  // Signal the thread to shut down
        _thread.Join(); // Optional: Wait for the thread to finish
    }

    void DoWork()
    {
        while (!_shutdownEvent.WaitOne(0))
        {
            // This loop continues to execute until the user enters input,
            // which sets the _shutdownEvent.
            Console.WriteLine("Working...");
            Thread.Sleep(1000);
        }
        Console.WriteLine("Shutting down...");
    }
}
```
This creates a manual reset event that starts out unset. In the DoWork loop, it checks at each iteration whether the signal has been set. If it has, it exits the loop (and therefore the function, and therefore the thread). If it has not, it loops again.

In the main thread, when the user inputs anything, it sets the signal, which makes the next iteration of the loop in the worker thread exit.

AutoResetEvent works on a similar principle but resets itself back to an unset state automatically after releasing a waiting thread.

Unfortunately MongoDB's query language doesn't directly support statistical functions such as median, mode, standard deviation and interquartile ranges. So you need to use MongoDB aggregation and some pre/post-processing to achieve this.

Here is how to do it:

1. First, we need to compute the relevant details. Let's assume employee document has `department` and `salary` fields.

    Getting Median:
    ```bash
    db.employees.aggregate([
     { $match: { department: "YourDepartment" } },
     { $sort: { salary: 1 } },
     { $group: {
        _id: null,
        count: { $sum: 1 },
        salaries: { $push: "$salary" }
     } },
     { $addFields: {
        median: { $arrayElemAt: [ "$salaries", { $divide: ["$count",2] } ] }
     } }
    ]);
    ```
    Here replace `"YourDepartment"` with your department name. The result has a field named `median`.

2. You can not find Modal salary directly, you have to manually go through data to search for the most commonly occurring salaries.

3. For salary range, do the following query:

    ```bash
    db.employees.aggregate([
      { $match: { department: "YourDepartment" } },
      { $group: { _id: null, max: { $max: "$salary" }, min: { $min: "$salary" } } }
    ]);
    ```
    Here replace `"YourDepartment"` with your department name. The results will have `min` and `max` fields, which define the salary range.

4. The computation of the interquartile range and standard deviation can be done via post-processing the raw data in a powerful data processing library such as numpy, pandas, scipy, etc. There are many MongoDB drivers available to load the raw data into these libraries easily.

5. For percentile operations, you can sort and then use `$facet` and `$bucketAuto` to split data into percentile brackets. You can replace `"YourDepartment"` with your desired department name.

   ```bash
     db.employees.aggregate([
      { $match: { department: "YourDepartment" } },
      { $sort: { salary: 1 } },
      { $group: { _id: null, salaries: { $push: "$salary" } } },
      { $unwind: { path: "$salaries", includeArrayIndex: "rank" } },
      { $addFields: { percentileRank: { $divide: [ "$rank", { $subtract: [ "$count", 1 ] } ] } } }
     ]);
    ```
   The result will include all salaries and their percentile ranks (as `percentileRank`).

