To calculate the longest common subsequence (LCS) between two strings, you could employ the dynamic programming approach. In PHP, this can be implemented with the use of arrays and nested loops.

Here is a PHP function to calculate and return the length of the LCS:

```php
function lcs_length($str1, $str2) {
    // Convert the strings into arrays
    $str1_arr = str_split($str1);
    $str2_arr = str_split($str2);

    // Length of strings
    $m = count($str1_arr);
    $n = count($str2_arr);

    // Create a 2D array of size (m+1) x (n+1) to store LCS length
    $dp = array_fill(0, $m + 1, array_fill(0, $n + 1, 0));

    // Fill dp[][] in bottom-up manner
    for($i=1; $i<=$m; $i++) {
        for($j=1; $j<=$n; $j++) {
            if($str1_arr[$i-1] == $str2_arr[$j-1])
                $dp[$i][$j] = $dp[$i-1][$j-1] + 1;  // If current character in both strings is same, add 1 to the result of previous character
            else
                $dp[$i][$j] = max($dp[$i-1][$j], $dp[$i][$j-1]);  // If not same, take the maximum value by excluding current character in either string
        }
    }

    // Return LCS length which is stored in dp[m][n]
    return $dp[$m][$n];
}
```
