Java does not provide a way to directly obtain the memory size of an object (like the sizeof function in C/C++). However, we can simulate the effect by assuming that every object takes a certain fixed amount of memory. For simplicity, we can assume that each entry in our data structure, regardless of its actual type, consumes a 'unit' of memory.

Note that this is a very naive way to measure memory size and doesn't represent actual memory usage in Java. But, it will help to demonstrate the concept of keeping track of dynamic memory size.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class MemoryList<E> extends ArrayList<E> {

    // Use this to keep track of the memory size 
    private int memorySize = 0; 

    @Override
    public boolean add(E e) {
        memorySize += 1;  // increases memory size when items are added
        return super.add(e);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean remove(Object o) {
        boolean removed = super.remove(o);
        if (removed) {
            memorySize -= 1;  // decreases memory size when items are removed
        }
        return removed;
    }

    @Override
    public E remove(int index) {
        E removedElement = super.remove(index);
        if (removedElement != null) {
            memorySize -= 1;  // decreases memory size when items are removed
        }
        return removedElement;
    }

    @Override
    public void clear() {
        memorySize = 0;  // clears memory size when list is cleared
        super.clear();
    }

    public int getMemorySize() {
        return memorySize;
    }
}
```

