Here's a simple implementation of quicksort in Java:

```java
public class QuickSort {
    static void quickSort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            int pi = partition(arr, low, high);

            quickSort(arr, low, pi-1);
            quickSort(arr, pi+1, high);
        }
    }

    static int partition(int arr[], int low, int high) {
        int pivot = arr[high];  
        int i = (low-1); 
        for (int j=low; j<high; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < pivot) {
                i++;

                int temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[j];
                arr[j] = temp;
            }
        }

        int temp = arr[i+1];
        arr[i+1] = arr[high];
        arr[high] = temp;

        return i+1;
    }

    public static void main(String args[]) {
        int arr[] = {10, 7, 8, 9, 1, 5};
        int n = arr.length;

        quickSort(arr, 0, n-1);

        System.out.println("Sorted array:");
        for (int i=0; i<n; ++i)
            System.out.print(arr[i]+" ");
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

The quicksort algorithm follows a divide-and-conquer strategy, very similar to merge sort. The key difference is that, while merge sort divides the array in half, quicksort divides the array based on a pivot element such that elements less than the pivot go to its left, and elements greater go to its right, which inherently sorts the pivot.

The time complexity of quicksort in the average case is `O(n log n)`. However, in the worst-case scenario, (i.e., when the smallest or largest element is always chosen as the pivot), the time complexity becomes quadratic `O(n^2)`. This worst-case scenario is unlikely to occur if we choose a random element as the pivot.

The `O(n log n)` average time complexity is because the array is partitioned into two halves at each step, and the algorithm processes each half separately, similarly to a binary search operation. Each half is again partitioned, and so on. So it takes `log n` steps to reduce the problem size by half at each step, and `n` steps to process each element once. Hence, the `n log n` time complexity.

The `O(n^2)` worst-case time complexity occurs when the array is already sorted in ascending or descending order. This happens because, at each step, the algorithm partitions the array into two sections, one with 0 elements and the other with `n-1` elements. Hence, the algorithm takes quadratic time to process the array.

The space complexity is `O(log n)` as it requires space for recursive function calls in the call stack. The worst-case space complexity is `O(n)` which can be the case in a skewed binary tree.

