- We use the Algorithm::Permute module to get all permutations of the list of strings. We initialize a new permutation object `$p`.
- We then run a loop for each permutation using `$p->next` which gives the next permutation of the list.
- For each permutation, we initialize an empty stack `@stack`.
- Then we run a nested loop for each string in the permutation and then for each character in the string (split //, $string will split the string into a list of individual characters).
- If the character is in the keys of the hash (meaning it's an opening bracket), we push it onto the stack.
- Else if the stack is not empty and the character is the corresponding closing bracket for the last opening bracket in the stack (which we get with $brackets{$stack[-1]}), we pop the last element from the stack.
- If neither of these conditions is met, then it's a closing bracket without a matching opening bracket in the stack, so we break out of the loop and move on to the next permutation.
- If we finish going through all the strings in a permutation and the stack is empty (meaning all brackets are matched), then we return 'Yes'.
- If we've gone through all permutations and haven't returned 'Yes', then no permutation has matching brackets, so we return 'No'.

