This problem requires you to return the nth prime Fibonacci number. Fibonacci numbers are the numbers in the integer sequence of 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, .... The sequence starts by 0 and 1, and every next number is the sum of the previous two numbers. A prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself.

The solution is to create two helper functions: one to generate the next Fibonacci number and another one to check if the number is prime. Then in the primeFib method, we loop until we find the nth prime Fibonacci number.

Here is the Java code for completing primeFib:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {

    private int fib(int n) {
      if (n <= 1) return n;
      int a = 0;
      int b = 1;

      for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        int c = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = c;
      }

      return b;
    }

    private boolean isPrime(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return false;
        if (n == 2) return true;

        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++)
            if (n % i == 0) return false;

        return true;
    }

    public int primeFib(int n) {
        int count = 0;
        int i = 0;

        while (count < n) {
            i++;
            if (isPrime(fib(i)))
                count++;
        }

        return fib(i);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution s = new Solution();

        System.out.println(s.primeFib(1)); // outputs 2
        System.out.println(s.primeFib(2)); // outputs 3
        System.out.println(s.primeFib(3)); // outputs 5
        System.out.println(s.primeFib(4)); // outputs 13
        System.out.println(s.primeFib(5)); // outputs 89
    }
}
```
This program will print the first 5 prime Fibonacci numbers.

