Here is a simple C++ program to perform a cyclical permutation on a matrix of integer values.

Let's consider a 3x3 matrix for simplicity. The goal is to shift elements in a clockwise direction.

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

void cycleMatrix(int a[3][3], int m, int n) {
  int t1 = a[0][0], t2 = 0;

  // Cycle through the top row.
  for (int i = 0; i < m-1; i++) {
    t2 = a[0][i+1];
    a[0][i+1] = t1;
    t1 = t2;
  }

  // Cycle through the right-most column.
  for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
    t2 = a[i+1][m-1];
    a[i+1][m-1] = t1;
    t1 = t2;
  }

  // Cycle through the bottom row.
  for (int i = m-1; i > 0; i--) {
    t2 = a[n-1][i-1];
    a[n-1][i-1] = t1;
    t1 = t2;
  }

  // Cycle through the left-most column.
  for (int i = n-1; i > 0; i--) {
    t2 = a[i-1][0];
    a[i-1][0] = t1;
    t1 = t2;
  }
}

int main() {
  int m = 3, n = 3;
  int a[3][3] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}};
  cycleMatrix(a, m, n);
  for(int i=0; i<m; i++) {
    for(int j=0; j<n; j++) {
      cout << a[i][j] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
  }
  return 0;
}
```
This script first cycles through and swaps the elements of the top row, then the right column, bottom row, and finally the left column. After the entire cycle is complete, the elements have been effectively permuted in a clockwise direction. Note to be aware of the dimensions of your matrix and adjust accordingly.

This solution is specifically for a 3x3 matrix. For larger matrices, you would likely need a more complex looping system or recursive function that handles each "layer" of the matrix, so to say.

