The ":hover", ":active", and ":focus" are pseudo-classes in CSS that are used to add special effects to some selectors. 

• ":hover": This pseudo-class is used to select and style an element when the user's pointer hovers over it. It's often used to change the color of a link when the user hovers over it.

• ":active": This pseudo-class is used to select and style an element at the moment it is being activated by the user. In other words, it's style will be applied when clicking or pressing enter on a keyboard (for elements that are focusable).

• ":focus": This pseudo-class is used to select and style an element when it has the focus. The ":focus" pseudo-class is used when inputs, buttons, or links are selected, typically through tab navigation or by clicking into an input field.

Here's an example of how to use these pseudo-classes in a CSS file:

```css
/* Apply initial styles to our box (or your specified element) */
.box {
    background-color: lightgrey;
    width: 200px;
    height: 200px;
    margin: 10px;
    padding: 10px;
    border: 2px solid blue;
    color: black;
    text-align: center;
    line-height: 200px;
    transition: background-color 0.5s ease;
}

/* Change the background color and text color when hovering over the box */
.box:hover {
    background-color: blue;
    color: white;
}

/* Slightly rotate the box when it's active */
.box:active {
    transform: rotate(10deg);
}

/* Outline the box in green when it's in focus */
.box:focus {
    outline: 3px solid green;
}
```
For the ".box:hover", the box changes background-color to blue and the text color to white when the mouse pointer hovers over it. For ".box:active", the box will slightly rotate when the user clicks on it. For ".box:focus", the box will outline in green when it's in focus which can be achieved by tab navigation or by clicking it if it is a focusable element. 

Using CSS transitions, the change in background color will animate over a period of 0.5 seconds.

