This task asks to handle string encoding and decoding, with a specific rule for cycling their characters. Three contiguous characters will be shuffled cyclically, meaning that "abc" becomes "bca". Characters at the end of the string that don't make up a complete set of three should remain as they are.

The code doesn't exist in the question, so here's the full implementation for what's asked, assuming each triplet shuffle in the 'encode_cyclic' function is left-shift, and in 'decode_cyclic' function is right-shift:

```cpp
#include<iostream>
#include<string>
using namespace std;

string encode_cyclic(string input){
    string encoded="";
    for(int i=0;i<input.length();i+=3){
        if(i+2<input.length()) {
            encoded+=input[i+1];
            encoded+=input[i+2];
            encoded+=input[i];
        }
        else
            while(i<input.length()) {
                encoded+=input[i];
                i++;
            }
    }
    return encoded;
}

string decode_cyclic(string input){
    string decoded="";
    for(int i=0;i<input.length();i+=3){
        if(i+2<input.length()) {
            decoded+=input[i+2];
            decoded+=input[i];
            decoded+=input[i+1];
        }
        else
            while(i<input.length()) {
                decoded+=input[i];
                i++;
            }
    }
    return decoded;
}
```

In this code:

1. The `encode_cyclic` function iterates over the string from the start to end, in steps of 3 each time, and checks if a group of 3 characters exist from the current index. If yes, it forms a new code by shifting the characters to the left and appends to the encoded string. If no such group exists (implies we are at the end of the string), it appends the remaining characters as is.

2. The `decode_cyclic` function does the opposite process. If a group of 3 characters exist from the current index, it forms the original characters by shifting the characters to the right and appends to the decoded string. If no such group exists, it appends the remaining characters as is. This decode operation will retrieve the original string.

