Here is a C function that performs the tasks described. To keep maximum efficiency, instead of doing the actual division, it uses bitwise operations which are faster to count the number of continuous trailing zeros in the binary representation of each number. It's a standard method to calculate how many times a number can be divided by two before it turns into an odd number. 

```c
#include <limits.h>

// Function to calculate and return the result array
void calculateDivisions(const int* inputArray, int* outputArray, int n) {
    const int oddResult = -1;

    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int num = inputArray[i];

        // Num is even
        if((num & 1) == 0) {
            int count = 0;
            while((num & 1) == 0) {  // While it is divisible by 2
                num = num >> 1;  // Divide by 2 using right shift operation
                count++;  // Increase the counter
            }
            outputArray[i] = count;
        }
        // Num is odd
        else {
            outputArray[i] = oddResult;
        }
    }
}
```

