This problem can be solved using the concept of line sweep. We will keep track of the events (i.e., the vertical sides of the rectangles) and process them from left to right.

Here is a Python solution.

```Python
class Event:
    def __init__(self, time, is_end, y1, y2):
        self.time = time
        self.is_end = is_end
        self.y1 = y1
        self.y2 = y2

    def __lt__(self, other):
        if self.time != other.time:
            return self.time < other.time
        else:
            return self.is_end < other.is_end


class Solution:
    def isRectangleCover(self, rectangles):
        events = []
        total_area = 0
        min_x, min_y = float('inf'), float('inf')
        max_x, max_y = float('-inf'), float('-inf')

        for x1, y1, x2, y2 in rectangles:
            min_x, min_y = min(min_x, x1), min(min_y, y1)
            max_x, max_y = max(max_x, x2), max(max_y, y2)
            total_area += (x2 - x1) * (y2 - y1)

            events.append(Event(x1, False, y1, y2))
            events.append(Event(x2, True, y1, y2))

        events.sort()

        sweep_line_area = 0
        curr_min_y, curr_max_y = events[0].y1, events[0].y2
        last_time = events[0].time

        for event in events[1:]:
            if event.time != last_time and curr_min_y < curr_max_y:
                if sweep_line_area > (max_x - min_x) * (curr_max_y - curr_min_y):
                    return False

                if last_time >= min_x and event.time <= max_x:
                    sweep_line_area += (max_x - min_x) * (curr_max_y - curr_min_y)

                curr_min_y, curr_max_y = event.y1, event.y2
            else:
                if event.is_end:
                    curr_max_y = max(curr_max_y, event.y2)
                else:
                    curr_min_y = min(curr_min_y, event.y1)

            last_time = event.time

        return total_area == sweep_line_area
```

In the implementation above, we first calculate the total area of all rectangles and the minimum and maximum coordinates in the x and y axes. Then we create events for each side of the rectangles and sort them in non-decreasing order of their x coordinate.

After that, we start processing the events. For each event, we calculate the area that would be swept if we drew a vertical line from the y-coordinate with the smallest value to the one with the largest value at the x-coordinate of that event. We add this to the total area swept so far. If at any point the total area swept is larger than the total area of all rectangles, we return False since there would be an overlap. We also update the smallest and largest y-coordinates so far.

Finally, we check if the total area of all rectangles is equal to the total area swept by the sweep line, and return the result.

This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n log n) due to the sorting, and space complexity of O(n) for storing the events, where n is the number of rectangles.

The algorithm time complexity on average can be reduced to O(k) where k is the total number of vertical sides of rectangles by using bucket sort instead of traditional sorting. This algorithm has also a space complexity of O(n). The reductions of time and space complexities apply only if the input consists of many rectangles with vertical sides at "discrete" positions.

