Assuming you are not publishing a crate:

1. Write the Rust interface of your library in a Rust file. Similar to a C header file, this Rust file should define all the public structures and public functions/types that other Rust applications should use. This Rust file should contain only declarations, not implementations. Since Rust supports namespaces and modules, you can structure your public API more organized than a flat C header file.

2. Rust applications can include this header-like file into their codebase and use the functions and types defined in it. Unlike C++ though, Rust does not have a separate header and implementation in terms of how code is divided; everything is in .rs files that are linked through use. This file would look something like this (just as an example): 
