Bubble sort is not the most efficient sorting algorithm, especially for large data sets since its average and worst-case time complexities are both O(n^2). However, it can be slightly improved by adding a flag to avoid unnecessary passes once the array is sorted. Here's an optimized bubble sort algorithm in JavaScript that sorts in descending order:

```javascript
function bubbleSort(arr) {
  let len = arr.length - 1;
  let sorted = false;

  while (!sorted) {
    sorted = true;
    for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) {
      if (arr[i] < arr[i + 1]) {
        let temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[i + 1];
        arr[i + 1] = temp;
        sorted = false;
      }
    }
    len--;
  }

  return arr;
}

let testArray = [34, 203, 3, 746, 200, 984, 198, 764, 9];
console.log(bubbleSort(testArray));
```

In this algorithm, we use a while loop to continue sorting until no more swaps are needed, indicated by the "sorted" variable being true. We check every pair of adjacent numbers and if the current number is less than the next one, we swap them, as we are sorting in descending order. In such case, we set the "sorted" variable to false again. Every pass through the array allows the highest unsorted value to "bubble" to its correct position, so we can decrease the end index of our inner loop by 1 each time to avoid redundant comparisons.

