To solve this problem we can use a concept of Hashing. We can iterate through the link list, and insert each node's data into a hash. If the data already exists in the hash, that means it is not unique and therefore we ignore it. Below is a C program that solves this problem:

```C
#include<stdio.h>
#include<stdlib.h>
#define SIZE 10

//Structure for Linked list node
struct Node {
  int data;
  struct Node *next;
};

//Structure for Hash table
struct HashTable {
  int val;
  int count;
};

//Function to print unique elements in linked list
void printUnique(struct Node* start) {
  struct HashTable hash[SIZE] = {0, 0};
  while(start != NULL) {
    hash[start->data].val = start->data;
    hash[start->data].count += 1;
    start = start->next;
  }

  int i;
  printf("The unique values are:\n");
  for(i = 0; i < SIZE; i++) {
    if(hash[i].count == 1) {
      printf("%d ", hash[i].val);
    }
  }
}

//Function to push node to list
void push(struct Node** head_ref, int new_data) {
  struct Node* new_node = (struct Node*) malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
  new_node->data  = new_data;
  new_node->next = (*head_ref);
  (*head_ref) = new_node;
}

//Driver code
int main() {
  //Input linked list: 1->2->1->3->2->5
  struct Node *start = NULL;
  push(&start, 5);
  push(&start, 2);
  push(&start, 3);
  push(&start, 1);
  push(&start, 2);
  push(&start, 1);

  printUnique(start);

  return 0;
}
```

In this code, we first push all the list data to the linked list data structure then using the "printUnique" function we find the unique elements by using Hashing technique. Please note this example works with positive numbers and needs to be adjusted for other types of data.

Also, the size of the hash should be dependent on the range of values. In the above example, the size of hash is 10 which means it can handle linked list data till 9 (0-9). If your linked list has the data beyond this range, you might want to increase the size of the hash accordingly.

