Sure. There are two approaches to construct a stack data structure in Java: one using an Array and one using Linked List. 

Approach #1: Stack using Array:

An array can be used to create a stack by keeping track of the "top" element. We increase the top variable when we push an item into stack, decrease it when we pop an item from the stack.

```java
public class StackAsArray {
    private int maxSize;
    private int top;
    private int[] stackArray;

    // constructor - maxSize is the maximum number of items that can be in the queue
    public StackAsArray(int maxSize) {
        this.maxSize = maxSize;
        top = -1; // For an empty array
        stackArray = new int[maxSize];
    }

    // push
    public void push(int value) {
        // check if stack is already full
        if (top >= maxSize - 1) {
            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("Stack is full");
        }
        top++;
        stackArray[top] = value;
    }

    // pop
    public int pop() {
        // check if stack is empty
        if (top < 0) {
            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return stackArray[top--];
    }
}
```
This implementation uses a fixed-sized array, and hence can overflow if there are more push operations than the maximum size. A more flexible implementation can be the usage of Java's `ArrayList` in place of the array, which would dynamically resize itself.

Approach #2: Stack using Linked List:

A singly linked list can be used to create a stack. We can insert and delete elements at the start of the list in order to maintain constant time for push and pop operations.

```java
public class StackAsLinkedList {
    private Node top;

    // Node definition
    private class Node {
        int value;
        Node next;
    }

    // constructor initializes an empty stack
    public StackAsLinkedList() {
        top = null;
    }

    // push
    public void push(int value) {
        Node oldTop = top;
        top = new Node();
        top.value = value;
        top.next = oldTop;
    }

    // pop
    public int pop() {
        // check if stack is empty
        if (top == null) {
            throw new NullPointerException("Stack is empty");
        }
        int value = top.value;
        top = top.next;
        return value;
    }
}
```
This implementation does not face the overflow issue of the array-based implementation, because it allocates new nodes for new items dynamically during runtime. However, it does come with the overhead of allocating and deallocating memory.

