Here is the Java solution:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {

    private static int[] dx = {-1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, -1, -1};
    private static int[] dy = {0, 1, 0, -1, 1, -1, 1, -1};

    private static List<Integer> longestIncreasingPath(int[][] mat) {
        if (mat == null || mat.length == 0) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }
        int n = mat.length, m = mat[0].length;
        int[][] dp = new int[n][m];
        List<Integer>[][] dir = new ArrayList[n][m];

        int max = 1;
        List<Integer> path = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i=0; i<n; i++) {
            for (int j=0; j<m; j++) {
                List<Integer> temp = dfs(i, j, mat, dp, dir);
                if (temp.size() > max) {
                    max = temp.size();
                    path = temp;
                }
            }
        }
        return path;
    }

    private static List<Integer> dfs(int x, int y, int[][] mat, int[][] dp, List<Integer>[][] dir) {
        if (dp[x][y] != 0) {
            return dir[x][y];
        }
        int n = mat.length, m = mat[0].length;
        int max = 1;
        List<Integer> path = new ArrayList<>();
        path.add(mat[x][y]);

        for (int i=0; i<8; i++) {
            int nx = x + dx[i], ny = y + dy[i];
            if (nx>=0 && nx<n && ny>=0 && ny<m && mat[nx][ny]>mat[x][y]) {
                List<Integer> temp = dfs(nx, ny, mat, dp, dir);
                if (1+temp.size() > max) {
                    max = 1+temp.size();
                    path = new ArrayList<>();
                    path.add(mat[x][y]);
                    path.addAll(temp);
                }
            }
        }
        dp[x][y] = max;
        dir[x][y] = path;

        return dir[x][y];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] arr = {
                {9, 4, 2, 5},
                {7, 1, 6, 8},
                {3, 5, 7, 9}
        };

        List<Integer> result = longestIncreasingPath(arr);
        System.out.println("Length of Longest Increasing Sequence: " + result.size());
        System.out.println("Longest Increasing Sequence: " + result);

    }
}
```

In this algorithm, I'm using a two-dimensional dynamic programming table dp to  store the length of the longest increasing path that starts from each cell of the grid. I also dynamically update the length of the longest sequence of each cell to avoid duplicate computation. 

Also for each cell, I use another 2D array(dir) of lists to store list of increasing path. Thus it will return required list of sequence.

