```javascript
function processArray(arr){
    // Function for calculating Mean of Array
    function mean(arr){
        let total = 0;
        for(let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            total += arr[i];
        }
        return total / arr.length;
    }

    // Function for calculating Standard Deviation of Array
    function standardDeviation(arr){
        let meanVal = mean(arr);
        let sum = arr.reduce(function(sum, value){
            return sum + Math.pow(value - meanVal, 2);
        }, 0);
        return Math.sqrt(sum / arr.length); 
    }


    let meanVal = mean(arr);
    let stdev = standardDeviation(arr);

    // Removing outliers
    arr = arr.filter(elem => {
        return Math.abs(elem-meanVal) <= 2*stdev;
    });

    // Sorting array 
    arr.sort(function(a, b){return a - b});

    // Finding median and rearranging array 
    let median_index = Math.floor(arr.length/2);

    let firstHalf =  arr.slice(0,median_index).sort(function(a, b){return a - b});
    let secondHalf = arr.slice(median_index).sort(function(a, b){return b - a});

    return firstHalf.concat(secondHalf);
}


let numbers = [1,9,8,3,4,7,2,6,5,1000];
console.log(processArray(numbers));
```
This function works by first calculating the mean and standard deviation of the original array. Elements that are more than two standard deviations away from the mean are considered as outliers and are removed from the array. After this, the array is sorted in ascending order. The median index is then found - if the length of the array is even, the median is the average of the two middle numbers, otherwise, it's the number in the middle. The array is then cut at this index and the two halves are sorted separately. The first half is sorted in ascending order and the second half is sorted in descending order.

As for the complexity, the time complexity for calculating the mean and standard deviation is O(n), where n is the size of the array. Removing the outliers is also O(n), and sorting the entire array would take O(n log n). Therefore, the overall time complexity is O(n log n). The space complexity is O(n), as we're creating a new array after removing the outliers.

Note: This program will not work as expected on arrays with duplicate numbers. For example, if the array is [1,2,2,3,4,5,6], the median would be 3 but the output would have 2 as the median. It would return [1, 2, 2, 5, 4, 3]. This is because when splitting the array into two halves, if the same number falls exactly in the middle (like 2 in this example), it is placed in the second half of the array which is sorted in descending order. Hence, all duplicate numbers should be removed before processing the array to get accurate results.

