&lt;p&gt;The initial setup of the game can have any configuration of $c$ coins scattered across the squares of the board. It's feasible for multiple coins to share the same square at any given moment. The coins are identifiable, meaning that interchanging two coins results in a new arrangement only if they were initially on separate squares.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During each turn, the player is required to select a coin and shift it either to the left or upwards by $2$, $3$, $5$, or $7$ spaces in a single direction. The only limitation is that the coin must remain within the boundaries of the board.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The game concludes when a player is unable to execute a valid move, thereby conceding victory to the opponent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Assuming Alice initiates the game and both players employ the best possible strategies, let $M(n, c)$ represent the count of potential initial configurations that would guarantee Alice's triumph, given a board of size $n$ by $n$ with $c$ distinct coins.&lt;/p&gt;

