Here is a Go program that uses a stack to solve this problem. It iterates over the characters in the input string one-by-one. If it encounters an opening bracket, it pushes it onto the stack. If it encounters a closing bracket, it pops the last bracket from the stack and checks if they match. If they don't match, or if there's nothing on the stack to pop, then the bracket balancing is incorrect.

```go
import (
    "strings"
)

func CorrectBracketing(brackets string) bool {
    stack := []rune{}
    for _, c := range brackets {
        switch c {
        case '(', '[', '{':
            stack = append(stack, c)
        case ')', ']', '}':
            if len(stack) == 0 {
                return false
            }
            lastBracket := stack[len(stack)-1]
            stack = stack[:len(stack)-1] // Pop from stack
            if (c == ')' && lastBracket != '(') || (c == ']' && lastBracket != '[') || (c == '}' && lastBracket != '{') {
                return false
            }
        default:
            // Ignore non-bracket characters
        }
    }
    return len(stack) == 0 // If stack is empty, all brackets were correctly paired
}
```
Here `rune` is a type that represents a unicode character in Go. Since bracket characters are unicodes, we use a slice of runes as the stack to store them.

The complexity of this algorithm is O(n), because it iterates over the input string once. It uses additional memory proportional to the input size in the worst case, when all brackets are opening brackets. Therefore, its space complexity is also O(n).

