Here is a Java implementation of both linear and binary search algorithms:

```java
public class SearchAlgorithms {

    // Linear Search Function
    public static int linearSearch(int[] array, int x) {
        for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
            if (array[i] == x)
                return i;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    // Binary Search Function
    public static int binarySearch(int[] sortedArray, int x) {
        int low = 0;
        int high = sortedArray.length - 1;
        while (low <= high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            if (sortedArray[mid] == x)
                return mid;

            if (sortedArray[mid] < x)
                low = mid + 1;
            else
                high = mid - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String args[]) {
        int[] arr = {2, 3, 4, 10, 40};
        int x = 10;

        // Testing Linear Search
        int linearResult = linearSearch(arr, x);
        System.out.println("Element found at index " + linearResult);

        // Testing Binary Search
        int binaryResult = binarySearch(arr, x);
        System.out.println("Element found at index " + binaryResult);
    }
}
```

# Time and Space Complexity Analysis

## Linear Search
1. Time Complexity: The worst case time complexity of linear search is O(n). Here, n is the number of elements in the array.
2. Space Complexity: The space complexity of linear search is O(1), since it only uses a small constant amount of space.

## Binary Search
1. Time Complexity: The worst case time complexity of binary search is O(log n). It reduces the search space by half in every iteration.
2. Space Complexity: The space complexity of binary search is O(1), since it also uses a small constant amount of space.

# Condition for each Algorithm's preference:

- Linear Search: It does not require the array to be sorted. So, when you have a small unsorted list, a linear search could be quicker. It uses a straightforward approach to go through every element, thus there's no special requirement for implementing it.

- Binary Search: It requires the array to be sorted. This method is preferable for a large sorted list, as it has a lower time complexity than a linear search. It halves the search space in every iteration, thus finding the element faster in sorted lists.

