```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(intToString(-123456));
        System.out.println(intToString(0));
        System.out.println(intToString(987654));
    }

    public static String intToString(int num) {
        if (num == 0) {
            return "0";
        }

        boolean isNegative = false;
        if (num < 0) {
            isNegative = true;
            // convert num to positive, we will add "-" sign later
            num = -num;
        }

        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        while (num > 0) {
            sb.append(num % 10);
            num /= 10;
        }

        if (isNegative) {
            sb.append('-');
        }

        return sb.reverse().toString();
    }
}
```

In this code:

- A `StringBuilder` is used to efficiently append the characters and then reverse the built string to get the integer representation.
- The modulo operator `%` is used to extract the last digit of `num` and append it.
- `num` is then divided by 10 to remove the last digit.
- If `num` was negative, a minus symbol `-` is appended.
- This process repeats until `num` is depleted.

Regarding limits, this code has been written with Java's limits for the type `int` in mind. The limit for `int` in Java is -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647.

It's important to note that Java's divide and modulo operations are constant-time, and this algorithm is linear, the running time of this algorithm is proportional to the number of digits in `num` (which is roughly proportional to the logarithm of `num`). Therefore, the time complexity is O(log(N)), where N is the value of the number. The additional space required is also proportional to the number of digits in `num`, making the space complexity O(log(N)) as well. This is the best possible time complexity we can achieve for this problem, as we have to at least look at every digit of the number.

