Here is a Java method _spiralPrint_ that prints the elements of 2D array in a spiral order, starting from the top left and moving clockwise.

```java
public class Main {
    static void spiralPrint(int m, int n, int[][] a)
    {
        /* m - ending row index
           n - ending column index 
           i, k - starting row index
           l   - starting column index */
        int i, k = 0, l = 0;

        while (k < m && l < n) {
            // Print the first row from the remaining rows
            for (i = l; i < n; ++i) {
                System.out.print(a[k][i] + " ");
            }
            k++;

            // Print the last column from the remaining columns 
            for (i = k; i < m; ++i) {
                System.out.print(a[i][n-1] + " ");
            }
            n--;

            // Print the last row from the remaining rows 
            if (k < m) {
                for (i = n-1; i >= l; --i) {
                    System.out.print(a[m-1][i] + " ");
                }
                m--;
            }

            // Print the first column from the remaining columns 
            if (l < n) {
                for (i = m-1; i >= k; --i) {
                    System.out.print(a[i][l] + " ");
                }
                l++;    
            }        
        }
    }

    // driver program
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        int R = 3;
        int C = 3;
        int a[][] = { {1, 2, 3},
                      {4, 5, 6},
                      {7, 8, 9} };
        spiralPrint(R, C, a);
    }
}
```

You might run this code on a Java compiler or an integrated development environment (IDE) like Eclipse or IntelliJ IDEA to see the output. The output will be as follows:
1 2 3 6 9 8 7 4 5 

This solution does four traversals of every small cycle. First (k, l) to (k, n-1), second (k+1, n-1) to (m-1, n-1), third (m-1, n-2) to (m-1, l) and fourth (m-2, l) to (k, l) and repeat these steps for remaining inner elements until all elements are printed. Note that every cycle means a round across one complete square (outer or inner).

