The Singleton design pattern ensures a class has only a single instance while providing a global point of access to it. It is generally used for logging, driver objects, caching, thread pools, and database connections.

Follow the steps below to implement a Singleton design pattern accurately in Java.

Step 1: Private Constructor

Make the constructor of Singleton class private. This step prevents the instantiation of the class from any outside class.

```java
public class MySingleton {
    private static MySingleton instance;
    private MySingleton() {
        // private constructor
    }
}
```

Step 2: Create a method to get the Singleton Instance

This method should be public and of the same return type as of Singleton class. Here, we use a method named `getInstance()`. If the `instance` of Singleton class is null, then a new Singleton object is created and returned, else the previously created instance is returned.

```java
public class MySingleton {
    private static MySingleton instance;
    private MySingleton() {
        // private constructor
    }

    public static MySingleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new MySingleton();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Our Singleton class is now complete for a single-threaded environment. If multiple threads are trying to get an instance of Singleton class simultaneously, then more than one instance may be created. To fix this, we make our getInstance method synchronized.

Step 3: Make `getInstance` method synchronized

```java
public class MySingleton {
    private static MySingleton instance;
    private MySingleton() {
        // Private constructor
    }

    public static synchronized MySingleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new MySingleton();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

This approach is thread-safe but it reduces the performance because of the cost associated with the synchronized method. Another way to make your Singleton thread safe by not using synchronized method is to create the instance of Singleton in the static initialization. In this approach, JVM handles the singleton instance creation when the class is being loaded, and also this guarantees thread safety.

Step 4: Eager Instantiation - Create instance during class loading

```java
public class MySingleton {
    private static MySingleton instance = new MySingleton();
    private MySingleton() {
        // private constructor
    }

    public static MySingleton getInstance() {
        return instance;
    }
}
```

In most scenarios, above singleton patterns are acceptable. However, all these methods have one common drawback that if your singleton class is using a lot of resources, for instance, a database connection, then it starts using resources as soon as it's loaded into memory. To avoid this, we can use the 'Bill Pugh Singleton' Design Pattern which uses the concept of inner static classes.

Step 5: Bill Pugh Singleton Design Pattern

```java
public class MySingleton {
    private MySingleton() {
        // private constructor
    }

    private static class SingletonHelper {
        private static final MySingleton INSTANCE = new MySingleton();
    }

    public static MySingleton getInstance() {
        return SingletonHelper.INSTANCE;
    }
}
``` 

The Bill Pugh pattern is the most widely used approach as it doesn’t require synchronization and the Singleton instance is created only when required. Any further calls to `getInstance()` will return the instance created in the SingletonHelper class.

