Here is the Java code using HashSet to find the matching pairs and count of distinct pairs. The time complexity of this function is O(n).

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class PairCounter {
    static class Pair {
        int num1;
        int num2;

        public Pair(int num1, int num2) {
            this.num1 = num1;
            this.num2 = num2;
        }

        @Override
        public boolean equals(Object o) {
            if (this == o) return true;
            if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) return false;
            Pair pair = (Pair) o;
            return (num1 == pair.num1 && num2 == pair.num2) || (num1 == pair.num2 && num2 == pair.num1);
        }

        @Override
        public int hashCode() {
            return num1 + num2;
        }
    }

    public static int countPairsMatchingSum(int[] array, int sum) {
        Set<Integer> nums = new HashSet<>();
        Set<Pair> pairs = new HashSet<>();
        for (int num : array) {
            int complement = sum - num;
            if (nums.contains(complement)) {
                pairs.add(new Pair(num, complement));
            }
            nums.add(num);
        }
        return pairs.size();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int array[] = new int[]{4, 8, 2, 4, 1};
        int sum = 6;
        int count = countPairsMatchingSum(array, sum);
        System.out.println("Count of distinct pairs is " + count);
    }
}
```

The approach is simple:
- For each element in the array, we calculate the complement of the sum and check if it is present in the HashSet.
- If found, we found a pair. We add it to our pairs set. To ensure we don't count duplicates, we override equals and hashCode in the Pair class.
- Next we add the current number to our HashSet for processing future elements.
- At the end, the size of our pairs Set will be the count of distinct pairs.

