First, let's compute the Fibonacci series and determine the angles for each segment.

```javascript
function fibonacci(n) {
    let fib = [0, 1];
    for (let i = 2; i < n; i++) {
        fib[i] = fib[i - 2] + fib[i - 1];
    }

    let sum = fib.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
    let angles = fib.map(f => (f / sum) * 2 * Math.PI);

    return angles;
}

let n = 10;
let angles = fibonacci(n);
```

Then, create an SVG element and add segments according to calculated angles.

```javascript
let radius = 100;
let svg = document.createElementNS('http://www.w3.org/2000/svg', 'svg');
svg.setAttribute('width', 2 * radius);
svg.setAttribute('height', 2 * radius);
document.body.appendChild(svg);

let currentAngle = 0;

for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let segment = document.createElementNS('http://www.w3.org/2000/svg', 'path');
    let nextPoint = [
        radius + Math.cos(currentAngle + angles[i]) * radius,
        radius + Math.sin(currentAngle + angles[i]) * radius
    ];

    let largeArcFlag = angles[i] > Math.PI ? 1 : 0;
    let d = `M ${radius} ${radius} L ${radius} 0 A ${radius} ${radius} 0 ${largeArcFlag} 1 ${nextPoint[0]} ${nextPoint[1]} Z`;

    segment.setAttribute('d', d);
    segment.setAttribute('fill', `hsl(${i / n * 360}, 50%, 50%)`);

    svg.appendChild(segment);
    currentAngle += angles[i];
}
```

In this code, each SVG 'path' element represents a segment of the chart. The 'd' attribute of the path specifies the shape of the segment. Different segment are colored using HSL color space for better visual differentiation. This example doesn't include labels, tooltips or interactivity, but these features could be added using similar techniques.

Remember, if you want a fully responsive SVG, consider using viewBox attribute and preserveAspectRatio attribute to maintain the aspect ratio. You may need to do a little more work to adjust the positioning and size of the SVG elements based on the viewport size.

