The given code is a recursive implementation of the factorial function, which is a computationally expensive operation. It can result in a tall call stack, possibly leading to a stack overflow for large inputs.

One way to optimize this is to use a loop instead of recursion:

```
function factorial(num) {
  let result = 1;
  for(let i = 2; i <= num; i++) {
    result *= i;
  }
  return result;
}
```

This implementation will run more efficiently for larger inputs compared to the recursive version, because it avoids additional function call overheads and potential stack overflow issues. Furthermore, it avoids the repeatedly checking if `num` is less than or equal to 1, and directly starts from 2, multiplying up to `num`.

Instead of creating a new execution context with each recursive function call, the iterative solution performs all of its work in the same execution context, resulting in less memory usage. This makes it a more efficient solution over the recursive approach.

