Every time `PotentialMemoryLeaker` is called, more event handlers are added to `foo.AnEvent`, but are never removed. This could keep objects alive longer than intended and if `PotentialMemoryLeaker` method is called many times it could lead to consuming a significant amount of memory.

To unhook the event handler after `MethodThatFiresAnEvent` has been called, we need to change our anonymous method (lambda expression) to a named method. This is because you can't unsubscribe an anonymous method. Here's an example of how to handle it:

