Here is a Python solution for the problem that applies the operations from last to first. It does so by utilizing stack to keep track of the operations in reverse order. If the given string is "a2345678999999999999999#@" and K is 1, it starts from the end of the string and keeps track of the size of the decoded string. When it hits an @ operation (which reverses the tape), it continues as before because reversing doesn't affect the Kth character. But when it hits a digit, it divides the size of the decoded string with the digit to update the size before the multiplication operation and update the K accordingly. When K is less than size, the Kth character is at the same position in original string. The # operation removes an element from the tape, so when it hits a # it will subtract 1 from the size of the decoded string.

```python
def decodeAtIndex(s, K):
    size = 0
    for c in s:
        if c.isdigit():
            size *= int(c)
        elif c == "#":
            size -= 1
        elif c == "@":
            continue
        else:
            size += 1
    # Apply operations from last to first (reversed)
    for c in reversed(s):
        K %= size
        if K == 0 and c.isalpha():
            return c
        if c.isdigit():
            size /= int(c)
        elif c == "#":
            size += 1
        elif c == "@":
            continue
        else:
            size -= 1
```

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), where n is the length of the string as the string is traversed two times. The space complexity is also O(n), where n is the length of the string as we are storing the string in stack for reversed traversal.

