It's normal behaviour for the browser to scroll to the top when navigating to a new page. However, maintaining the scroll position can improve user experience especially on pages with lots of content. 

NextJS uses its own Link component to handle client-side transitions. It does not use the standard HTML5 pushState API that includes scrollRestoration settings which enables users maintain their scrolling position when navigating different pages.

There are couple ways you could implement it but it usually involves keeping track of the scroll position before leaving a page and then restoring it after navigating back to the page. 

One possible solution is to store the current scroll position in localStorage before navigating away from a page and then reading that value and setting it back when the user navigates back. Here is some sample code.

// Get stored scroll position and Restore it.
```javascript
  useEffect(() => {
    if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
      const yOffset = window.localStorage.getItem('scrollPosition');
      if (yOffset) {
        window.scrollTo(0, yOffset);
        window.localStorage.removeItem('scrollPosition');
      }
    }
  }, []);
```

// Store scroll position before navigating away from a page.
```javascript
  useEffect(() => {
    function storeScrollPosition() {
      if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
        window.localStorage.setItem('scrollPosition', window.pageYOffset);
      }
    }
    window.addEventListener('beforeunload', storeScrollPosition);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('beforeunload', storeScrollPosition);
  }, []);
```

You can place this kind of logic in a custom useRouter hook or where you're handling the routing.

The code uses the window.localStorage to store the scroll position on the window object when the user navigates away from a page. The scroll position is restored by reading the saved value and set the scroll position when the user navigates back to the page.

