function separate_paren_groups(paren_string) {
    let stack = [];  // Defines an empty array as a stack
    let group = [];  // Array to keep track of opening and corresponding closing parentheses as a group
    let result = []; // Array to store results

    for (let char of paren_string) { // Loops through each character in the string
        if (char === ' ')  // Skip spaces
            continue;

        if (char === '(') { // If char is opening parentheses
            stack.push(char); // Push it to stack
            group.push(char); // Push it to group array
        }

        if (char === ')') { // If char is closing parentheses
            stack.pop(); // Remove its opening pair from stack
            group.push(char); // Push it to group array

            // If stack is empty, means we have found a pair of parentheses
            if (stack.length === 0) { 
                result.push(group.join('')); // Convert group elements to string and push to result array
                group = []; // Reset group for next parentheses group
            }
        }
    }
    return result; // Returns the array of separated parentheses groups
}
```

This Javascript function `separate_paren_groups` accepts a string `paren_string` that contains parentheses. It keeps track of properly nested parentheses groups and returns them as separate strings in an `result` array. When it encounters a `(`, it pushes it to the `stack` and `group` array. When it encounters a `)`, it removes the last element of `stack` and adds the `)` to `group`. If `stack` is empty after popping, that means we just closed a parentheses group, so we join all elements of `group` into a string, add this string to `result` and reset `group` for the next parentheses group.

