In Next.js, the `/public` directory is special. Each file inside the `public` directory will be available at the root URL. If your images are in `public/images`, they will be accessible from `/images/`.

There might be two reasons your styles and images are not working:

1. Incorrect links in your CSS:

Make sure that you are linking to the images in your CSS correctly considering the `public` directory as root. For instance, if your CSS file contains:

    ```css
    body {
      background: url("/path/to/image.jpg");
    }
    ```

    The `image.jpg` must be located in the `/public/path/to/` directory.

2. Incorrect import in your `_app.tsx`:

The leading slash in your import statement points to the root directory, so it might not be pointing to your stylesheets properly. Make sure the paths to your CSS files in your import statements are correct. 

```tsx
// Point to the exact path wrt the current file (relative)
import '../styles/layout.css';
import '../styles/common.css';
```

Or
```tsx
// Point to the exact path from the root of your src directory (absolute)
import 'styles/layout.css';
import 'styles/common.css';
```

You may need to configure TypeScript to understand absolute imports by editing the `tsconfig.json`:

```json
{
   "compilerOptions": {
     "baseUrl": ".",
     "paths": {
       "*": ["src/*"]
     }
   }
}
```

With this, anywhere in your app, you can import like so, instead of relative imports:

```js
import 'components/Button'
```

3. Old caching:

Sometimes, the changes in files are not reflected due to old cache. You can delete `.next/` folder in your project root and try restarting your development server.

There are a couple of corrections to be made in your implementation of QuickSort. Here's the corrected Java code for QuickSort with explanation:

```java
public class QuickSort {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {10, 7, 8, 9, 1, 5};
        int n = array.length;
        quickSort(array, 0, n - 1);
        System.out.println("Sorted array: ");
        printArray(array, n);
    }

    // Fix: swap the check condition to low < high instead of low > high
    static void quickSort(int[] array, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {
            // Fix: first find pivot and then call recursively quicksort on two halves
            int pi = partition(array, low, high);

            quickSort(array, low, pi - 1);
            quickSort(array, pi + 1, high);
        }
    }

    static int partition(int[] array, int low, int high) {
        int pivot = array[high]; 
        int i = low - 1;
        // Fix: If array[j] <= pivot then increase "i" and swap array[i] and array[j] 
        for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
            if (array[j] <= pivot) {
                i++;
                swap(array, i, j);
            }
        }
        // Swap pivot with the element at i + 1 to get it in the correct place
        swap(array, i + 1, high);

        return i + 1;
    }

    // Use a helper method to swap two elements in an array
    static void swap(int[] array, int i, int j) {
        int temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }

    static void printArray(int[] array, int size) {
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            System.out.print(array[i] + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

The time complexity of Quicksort is O(n^2) in the worst-case scenario when the array is already sorted, but it can be improved to O(n log n) on average if a good pivot is chosen. The worst-case scenario for Quicksort happens rarely in randomized pivot choice. 

Some optimizations include using hybrid sorting algorithms that switch to insertion sort when the array size becomes small, and using median of three or randomized selection for choosing the pivot. 

However, those optimizations are not included in the basic version of quicksort above to maintain the simplicity of the solution.

