The behavior you're experiencing is due to a common issue with `scanf()` and `fgets()` used together. When you enter your number and press enter, `scanf()` reads the number but leaves the newline character (`\n`) in the input buffer. Then, when `fgets()` reads the input stream, it reads the newline character left by `scanf()` and immediately stops reading, thinking an "end of line" was reached. Therefore, your program thinks you've finished providing input, which isn't what you intended.

Here is a small trick to overcome this issue:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    double nUm;
    char name[100];

    printf("enter number \t:");
    scanf("%lf", &nUm);
    printf("%lf\n", nUm);

    // Add a space before your format specifier
    // This will consume any white-space characters remaining in the input
    printf("enter name \t:");
    scanf(" %s", name); 
    printf("%s", name);

    return 0;
}
```
However, this trick will only read the name until it encounters a whitespace. If you intend to read multi-word names with spaces in between, here's an alternative approach:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    double nUm;
    char name[100];

    printf("enter number \t:");
    scanf("%lf", &nUm);
    printf("%lf\n", nUm);

    // Flush input buffer by reading and discarding characters until a newline or EOF is encountered
    int c;
    while ((c = getchar()) != '\n' && c != EOF);

    printf("enter name \t:");
    fgets(name, 100, stdin);
    printf("%s", name);

    return 0;
}
``` 
This explicitly cleans up the input buffer, ensuring `fgets()` can correctly read your name input.

