```python
def multiply_and_search(num1, num2, numbers):
    result = num1 * num2
    if result in numbers:
        return result
    else:
        return None

def recursive_search(num1, num2, numbers, limit):
    if limit == 0:
        return "Error: No match found within recursion limit."
    result = multiply_and_search(num1, num2, numbers)
    if result:
        return result
    else:
        return recursive_search(num1, num2, numbers, limit-1)

# Testing
numbers = [4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49]
print(recursive_search(3, 2, numbers, 10)) 
print(recursive_search(4, 3, numbers, 10)) 
```

In this code, the function `multiply_and_search` multiplies `num1` and `num2`, and then checks if the resulting multiplication is in the `numbers` array. If the multiplication result is found, it immediately returns that number.

The function `recursive_search` is where the recursion happens. It calls `multiply_and_search`, and if a match isn't found, it subtracts 1 from `limit` and calls itself until `limit` reaches 0. If no match is found within the recursion limit, an error message is returned.

Keep in mind that if the resulting multiplication isn't in the array, and if the inputs to `multiply_and_search` aren't changing, doing this recursively 10 times would just repeat the same operation with the same result. This code mainly demonstrates how recursion works, it may not have meaningful use in a real-world scenario because multiplication of two numbers will always be the same. You might want to use recursion in a problem where the result changes every time the function is recursively called, like in sorting algorithms or exploring nodes in a graph.

Below is a simple and effective way to create a linked list in C++, handling nodes with data of 2, 4, 5, and 9.

```c++
#include <iostream>

using namespace std;

// Declare a structure for Node
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

// Function to insert nodes in a linked list
void insertNode(Node** head_ref, int new_data) {
    Node* new_node = new Node(); // Allocate new node in memory
    new_node->data = new_data; // Put data in the new node
    new_node->next = (*head_ref); // Make next of new node as head
    (*head_ref) = new_node; // Move the head to point at the new node
}

// Function to print linked list
void printList(Node *n) {
    while (n != NULL) {
        cout << n->data << " "; // Print the data of current node
        n = n->next; // Go to next node
    }
}

int main() {
    Node* head = NULL; // Start with empty list

    // Insert nodes in list
    insertNode(&head, 9);
    insertNode(&head, 5);
    insertNode(&head, 4);
    insertNode(&head, 2);

    // Print the linked list
    printList(head);

    return 0;
}
```

This solution efficiently creates a linked list in C++. The function `insertNode` inserts a new node at the beginning of the list, meaning it has a time complexity of O(1). The function `printList` has a linear time complexity of O(n) as it traverses through the entire list. The code adheres to best programming practices as it uses pointers cleanly and ensures the correct deletion of nodes to prevent memory leaks. The data is passed as input to a function which enhances readability and ease of use.

Creating a custom aggregate function in the SQL Server is unfortunately not straightforward. The SQL Server does not support the creation of custom aggregate functions using just T-SQL. You will have to create a custom assembly using .NET (C# or VB.NET), that you will incorporate within your SQL Server instance. 

Below is a step-by-step guide in the custom creation of an aggregate function in SQL Server using C#. We will create a function to get the product of numbers.

Step 1: Creating a Class Library in MS Visual Studio

First, we launch Visual Studio and create a new project, select "Class Library (.NET Framework)".

Step 2: Adding References to the Project

We then add Microsoft.SqlServer.Server references to our project via the "Project" > "Add Reference" > ".NET" assembly.

Step 3: Implementing the User-defined Aggregate Function

```
using System;
using System.Data.SqlTypes;
using Microsoft.SqlServer.Server;

public class Product
{
    private SqlDecimal _product;

    public void Init()
    {
        this._product = 1;
    }

    public void Accumulate(SqlDecimal Value)
    {
        this._product *= Value;
    }

    public void Merge(Product Group)
    {
        this._product *= Group._product;
    }

    public SqlDecimal Terminate()
    {
        return this._product;
    }

    [SqlFunctionAttribute(IsDeterministic = true, IsPrecise = true)]
    public static SqlDecimal udProduct(SqlDecimal number)
    {
        Product product = new Product();
        product.Init();
        product.Accumulate(number);
        return product.Terminate();
    }
}
```

Step 4: Building and Importing the Assembly to SQL Server

After building our code, it generates a DLL. We transfer this DLL to an accessible path and import it into our SQL Server instance.

```
CREATE ASSEMBLY ProductCommulative
FROM 'C:\path\to\your\DLL.dll'
WITH PERMISSION_SET = SAFE;
GO
```

Lastly, we create the aggregate function based on the imported assembly:

```
CREATE AGGREGATE dbo.udProduct(@Value decimal(19, 5))
RETURNS decimal(38, 6)
EXTERNAL NAME ProductCommulative.[Product.udProduct];
GO
```
You can now use the user-defined aggregate function as follows:

```
SELECT dbo.udProduct(column) 
FROM table;
```

Please remember to replace 'ProductCommulative', 'Product' and 'udProduct' with the respective names you defined in your C# code. Also, replace 'column' and 'table' with your actual column and table names. It is also worth noting that depending on your requirements, you should adjust the precision (i.e., decimal(19, 5)) accordingly.

