import string

def pig_latin_converter(text, reverse=False):
    words = text.split()
    convert_sentence = []

    for word in words:
        original_word = word.strip(string.punctuation)
        if original_word.istitle(): # check for title case words
            is_title_case = True
        else:
            is_title_case = False

        if reverse:
            if len(original_word) > 2: 
                eng_word = original_word[-2] + original_word[0:-3].lower() + original_word[-1]
            else:  
                eng_word = original_word

            if is_title_case:
                eng_word = eng_word.capitalize()
            convert_sentence.append(text[text.index(word) + len(word):].lstrip() or " " + eng_word)

        else:
            if len(original_word) > 1:
                pig_word = original_word[1:].lower() + original_word[0].lower() + 'ay'
            else:  
                pig_word = original_word

            if is_title_case:
                pig_word = pig_word.capitalize()
            convert_sentence.append(text[text.index(word) + len(word):].lstrip() or " " + pig_word)

    return "".join(convert_sentence)
```

This function works by splitting the input sentence into words. In forward conversion, it checks if the word is a title case (first letter capitalized), it then manipulates the word by putting the first letter at the end and adding 'ay'. If the original word was title case, it converts first letter of the new word into upper case. On reversal, it takes the last letter, excluding 'ay' and puts it at start. Again, if the original word was title case, it converts first letter of the new English word into upper case.

