The CPU cache behavior is often intricate and intertwined with a variety of factors such as the architecture, operating system management, and compiler optimizations. However, considering a simple and ideal case without much optimization and prefetching, let's analyze this:

The first loop:

```
for (int i = 1; i < 16; i++) {
  arr[i] = arr[i] + arr[i-1];
}
```

If we assume an L1 cache line size is four 32-bit integers (retrieving memory in chunks of four integers at a time), the array `arr` will take up four cache lines, storing integers 0-3, 4-7, 8-11, and 12-15, respectively. When `i = 1`, the first cache line gets loaded into the L1 cache. When `i = 4 (or 5)`, the second line will need to be loaded. Similarly, when `i = 8 (or 9)`, and `i = 12 (or 13)`, subsequent lines will need to be loaded. Therefore, there can be 4 cache line loads. The writes will be made into the cache and written back to main memory later on (write back policy).

The second loop:

```
for (int i = 0; i < 16; i++) {
 arr[i] += arr[i];
}
```

As it iterates through, we are overwriting the array elements. In this case, the processor can, in principle, perform write-combining (collecting several writes to the same cache line and performing them together). If the first loop has just completed and no other operations have forced flushing the cache back to main memory (this will be an ideal case), no reloads are necessary because the data is still in cache from the first loop. But if we're not in this ideal case, we'll see 4 cache line loads like the first loop anyway.

So, CPU has to reload the numbers in cache a theoretical minimum of 4 times for each loop considering an ideal case, but depending on various factors like cache eviction, cache coherence, write policy, cache size, other processes running, operating system behavior, compiler optimizations, etc., the exact number can be different and often higher.

