A palindrome is a word, phrase, number, or other sequence of characters that reads the same backward as forward, ignoring spaces, punctuation, and capitalization. Here is a simple JavaScript function to check if a string is a palindrome:

```javascript
function isPalindrome(str) {
    // convert the string to lowercase and remove non-alphanumeric characters
    var normalizedStr = str.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, '');
    // reverse the normalized string
    var reversedStr = normalizedStr.split('').reverse().join('');
    // the string is a palindrome if it is equal to its reverse
    return normalizedStr === reversedStr;
}

console.log(isPalindrome('A man, a plan, a canal: Panama')); // prints: true
console.log(isPalindrome('Hello World')); // prints: false
```

In this function, we first normalize the string by converting it to lowercase and removing any non-alphanumeric characters. We then reverse this normalized string by splitting it into an array of characters, reversing that array, and joining it back into a string. Finally, we check if the normalized string is equal to its reverse, which would mean the string is a palindrome.

