Please find below a simple Trie data structure implementation in Java. The key methods are `insert` for adding words to the Trie and `search` for checking if a word is in the Trie. This design inherently provides excellent retrieval efficiency for strings sharing common prefixes since traversal just follows the Trie paths, reaching a time complexity of O(n) where n is the length of the word to be queried.

```java
// Define Trie Node.
class TrieNode {
    char c;
    HashMap<Character, TrieNode> children = new HashMap<>();
    boolean isLeaf;

    public TrieNode() {}
    public TrieNode(char c){
        this.c = c;
    }
}

// Define Trie.
public class Trie {
    private TrieNode root;

    public Trie() {
        root = new TrieNode();
    }

    // Inserts a word into the Trie.
    public void insert(String word) {
        HashMap<Character, TrieNode> children = root.children;
        for(int i=0; i<word.length(); i++){
            char c = word.charAt(i);
            TrieNode t;
            if(children.containsKey(c)){
                    t = children.get(c);
            }else{
                t = new TrieNode(c);
                children.put(c, t);
            }

            children = t.children;

            if(i==word.length()-1)
                t.isLeaf = true;
        }
    }

    // Returns if the word is in the Trie.
    public boolean search(String word) {
        TrieNode t = searchNode(word);
        if(t != null && t.isLeaf) 
            return true;
        else
            return false;
    }

    // Returns if there is any word in the Trie that starts with the given prefix.
    public boolean startsWith(String prefix) {
        if(searchNode(prefix) == null) 
            return false;
        else
            return true;
    }

    // Return the node where search ends.
    private TrieNode searchNode(String str){
        HashMap<Character, TrieNode> children = root.children; 
        TrieNode t = null;
        for(int i=0; i<str.length(); i++){
            char c = str.charAt(i);
            if(children.containsKey(c)){
                t = children.get(c);
                children = t.children;
            }else{
                return null;
            }
        }

        return t;
    }
}
```

The `startsWith` function can be used to explore strings with identical prefixes, another approach that this Trie data structure simplifies. The time complexity of `startsWith` is also O(n). Since the Trie only stores relevant nodes (only storing characters represented in the inserted strings and omitting the other characters), we accomplish efficient storage.

