Here is an implementation of a multi-threaded timer using Javascript's async/await functionality:

```javascript
class Timer {
    constructor(startTime = 60) {
        this.startTime = startTime;
        this.time = this.startTime;
    }

    start() {
        this.intervalId = setInterval(() => {
            console.log(`Timer: ${this.time}`);
            this.time--;

            if (this.time < 0) {
                this.time = this.startTime;
            }
        }, 1000);
    }

    stop() {
        clearInterval(this.intervalId);
    }
}

// Function to create multiple timers
async function createTimers(n) {
    let timers = [];
    for(let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        timers.push(new Timer());
        timers[i].start();
        await sleep(1000); // Wait one second before starting the next timer
    }
}

function sleep(ms) {
    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));
}

createTimers(5); // Create 5 timers
```

This script defines a `Timer` class whose instances can start and stop their respective timers independently. The timer counts down from the specified start time until zero, then resets back to the start time and continues. 

Multiple timers can be created via the `createTimers` function. This function uses the `sleep` function to ensure that each timer starts one second after the previous one, in order to simulate the "multi-threading" behavior (since JavaScript itself does not directly support true multithreading due to its single-threaded nature). 

Since Javascript is single-threaded and event-driven, there won't be synchronization issues with this setup and hence no need to handle such errors. Behaviors such as starting, stopping, and resetting timers are done within closures defined by each timer, so they will not interfere with each other. 

Each interval callback function maintains its own state through the `this` reference set when calling the `Timer` constructor. As such, each timer effectively has its own thread of execution.

