Adding instrumentation/logging around database calls (SQL queries or stored procedures) for a .Net application is quite easy with the help of suitable logging frameworks. This can help you trace slow database calls with the help of log files. 

Here's one very quick example using EntityFramework's Interception extension:

```cs
public class CommandInterceptor : IDbCommandInterceptor 
{
    private readonly ILogger<CommandInterceptor> _logger;

    public CommandInterceptor(ILogger<CommandInterceptor> logger) => _logger = logger;

    public void NonQueryExecuting(DbCommand command, DbCommandInterceptionContext<int> interceptionContext) =>
        LogBefore(command);

    public void NonQueryExecuted(DbCommand command, DbCommandInterceptionContext<int> interceptionContext) =>
        LogAfter(command);

    private void LogBefore(DbCommand command) =>        
        _logger.LogDebug("Starting command execution: {CommandText}", command.CommandText);

    private void LogAfter(DbCommand command) => 
        _logger.LogDebug("Finished command execution: {CommandText}", command.CommandText);
}
```

And then add it to your EF configuration:
```cs
DbInterception.Add(new CommandInterceptor(new Logger<CommandInterceptor>(new LoggerFactory())));
```

