The isPrime() method is used to check if a number is prime or not. The number one is not a prime number and all even numbers except 2 are not prime. The number two is the smallest prime number. The function checks divisibility starting from 2 upto square root of the number. Any number that is divisible by some number other than 1 and the number itself is not prime.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        countOddAndPrimes(1, 100);
    }

    public static void countOddAndPrimes(int commencement, int termination) {
        int oddCount = 0;
        int primeCount = 0;

        for (int i = commencement; i <= termination; i++) {
            if (i % 2 != 0) {
                oddCount++;
                if (isPrime(i))
                    primeCount++;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Number of odd integers: " + oddCount);
        System.out.println("Number of prime numbers: " + primeCount);
    }

    public static boolean isPrime(int x) {
        if (x < 2) return false;
        if (x == 2) return true;
        if (x % 2 == 0) return false;
        for (int i = 3; i * i <= x; i += 2)
            if (x % i == 0) return false;
        return true;
    }
}
```

When you run this code with the range set from 1 to 100 (commencement = 1, termination = 100), it will count the odd and prime numbers within this range and display the counts.

