You can check if an object property in JavaScript is undefined using either the typeof operator or the hasOwnProperty() method. 

Here is an example:

```javascript
var obj = {
  prop1: 'value1',
  prop2: 'value2',
};

// Test property with typeof
if (typeof obj.prop3 === 'undefined') {
  console.log('prop3 is undefined');
} else {
  console.log('prop3 is defined');
}

// Test property with hasOwnProperty
if (!obj.hasOwnProperty('prop3')) {
  console.log('prop3 does not exist');
} else {
  console.log('prop3 exists');
}
```

Both of these methods are testing slightly different things. 
The typeof operator will return 'undefined' both when the property does not exist and when the property does exist but its value is explicitly set to undefined. 
The hasOwnProperty method, on the other hand, will only return false if the property does not exist at all. If the property exists but its value is undefined, it will still return true.

