An Armstrong number is a number such as 153, 370, 371, 407 etc. where the number of digits (n) in it are such that the sum of its digits raised to nth power is equal to the number itself. Here's an example in Java, that does exactly that:

```java
public class ArmstrongNumber {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int num = 371;
        boolean isArmstrong = isArmstrong(num);
        if (isArmstrong)
            System.out.println(num + " is an Armstrong number.");
        else
            System.out.println(num + " is not an Armstrong number.");
    }

    static boolean isArmstrong(int num) {
        int digits = 0;
        int temp = num;
        while (temp != 0) {
            digits++;
            temp = temp / 10;
        }

        int sum = 0;
        temp = num;
        while (temp != 0) {
            int remainder = temp % 10;
            sum = sum + power(remainder, digits);
            temp = temp / 10;
        }

        return sum == num;
    }

    static int power(int n, int r) {
        int c, p = 1;
        for (c = 1; c <= r; c++) {
            p = p * n;
        }
        return p;
    }
}
```

This code first defines a method called `isArmstrong` that divides and counts the number of digits in the number. Then it raises each digit to the power of the number of digits and adds them up. If the total is the same as the original number, it is an Armstrong number, and the method returns true; otherwise, it returns false. 

The power function, `power()`, calculates the nth power of a number and is used in the `isArmstrong()` function. 

Finally, `main()` runs the Armstrong check on the number and prints whether it is an Armstrong number.

