In Java, you cannot directly sort two arrays based on one. To sort both arrays while still maintaining their correspondence, you would need a different data structure. A common approach is to create a helper class to hold the pair of `number` and `color` and then sort a list of that class by `number`. After sorting it, you can rebuild your arrays based on the sorted list if you still need separate arrays.

Here is an example:

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Comparator;

class Pair {
    int number;
    int color;

    public Pair(int number, int color) {
        this.number = number;
        this.color = color;
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {4, 2, 1};
        int[] colors = {0x11, 0x24, 0x01};

        int n = numbers.length;

        // create list of pairs
        List<Pair> pairs = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            pairs.add(new Pair(numbers[i], colors[i]));

        // sort the pairs
        pairs.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(pair -> pair.number));

        // rebuild arrays
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            numbers[i] = pairs.get(i).number;
            colors[i] = pairs.get(i).color;
        }

        System.out.println("Sorted numbers: " + Arrays.toString(numbers));
        System.out.println("Corresponding colors: " + Arrays.toString(colors));
    }
}
```

This program will output:

```
Sorted numbers: [1, 2, 4]
Corresponding colors: [1, 36, 17]
```

Although this technique involves a bit more code than simply sorting an array, it allows you to keep the two arrays connected through the sorting process with relatively efficient time-complexity of O(n log n) because sorts are typically implemented as a version of quicksort, mergesort or heapsort. This is the simplest and cleanest approach given the constraints of Java. There's no direct way to accomplish this with a more "library" approach without re-implementing this same concept.

