Operator overloading is a powerful feature of C++ that allows programmers to define how operators work with their own types. With operator overloading, the behavior of operators like +, -, *, /, and others can be customized to work with user-defined types. It simplifies the code and makes it look more intuitive and more readable.

There are some rules related to operator overloading in C++. For example, new operators can't be created and existing ones can't be modified. Also, at least one operand must be a user-defined type. Furthermore, the arity (number of operands) can't be changed, unary operators remain unary, and binary operators remain binary.

Let's illustrate implementing operator overloading for a complex number class in C++:

```cpp
class Complex {
public:
    Complex(): real(0), imag(0) {}
    Complex(double r, double i): real(r), imag(i) {}

    // Operator overloading for addition of complex numbers
    Complex operator + (const Complex& c) {
        return Complex(real + c.real, imag + c.imag);
    }

    // Operator overloading for subtraction
    Complex operator - (const Complex& c) {
        return Complex(real - c.real, imag - c.imag);
    }

    // For readability we can also overload the << and >> operators
    friend std::ostream& operator << (std::ostream& out, const Complex& c) {
        out << c.real;
        out << "+i" << c.imag << std::endl;
        return out;
    }

    friend std::istream& operator >> (std::istream& in, Complex& c) {
        in >> c.real;
        in >> c.imag;
        return in;
    }
private:
    double real;
    double imag;
};
```

Notice how clean and expressive use of these operators becomes. Instead of having to call a method and passing the operands as parameters, we simply use '+' and '-' as if we were dealing with simple integers or floats. Finally, the input and output of complex numbers from and to the console become extremely straightforward through the overloading of '<<' and '>>' operators.

However, operator overloading should be used wisely as incorrect or inappropriate use can lead to confusion and more complex code logic. The overloaded operator should match intuitively with the usual context of the operator. Also, side effects should be taken care of while overloading any operators.

Potential pitfalls

1. Overuse: Overusing operator overloading can lead to code that is difficult to understand and maintain.
2. Misuse: Misusing operator overloading may cause confusion among developers, especially when the overloaded operator behaves in a manner that is not consistent with its usual semantic meaning.
3. Complexity: Incorrect use of operator overloading can increase code complexity, thus making it harder to trace bugs and fix them.
4. Side effects: It's important to identify and handle any potential side effects of operator overloading to prevent any problems with your code.

