Generating an ETag HTTP header for a resource file typically involves your server producing a unique identifier for the particular version of the resource. The exact process depends on the server and language you're using, but here's a simple example in node.js:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const crypto = require('crypto');
const fs = require('fs');

app.get('/your-resource-path', (req, res, next) => {
  fs.readFile('path-to-your-file', (err, data) => {
    if (err) return next(err);

    const hash = crypto.createHash('md5').update(data).digest('hex');
    const etag = '"' + hash + '"';

    const match = req.header('If-None-Match');

    if (match && match === etag) {
      res.sendStatus(304); // resource not modified
    } else {
      res.setHeader('ETag', etag);
      res.send(data);
    }
  });
});

app.listen(3000);
```

In this example, we use the crypto module to generate an md5 hash of the resource's contents as the unique identifier. This allows the ETag to change whenever the file is modified.

The If-None-Match request header field is used to prevent unnecessary data transmission for a resource that has not changed on the server. If the client has a cached version of the resource and the ETag matches, it means the resource has not changed and the server will return a 304 status, indicating that the client should use its cached version of the resource.

If the file was changed, a new ETag is generated, set in the response header and the data of the file is sent back to the client. 

Keep in mind that this approach might not scale well for larger applications or files due to the requirement of reading and hashing the whole file to generate the ETag. For a larger application, you might want to use a more efficient method of generating ETags or use a library that does this for you.

