The core idea behind the algorithm mentioned is not language-specific. It's a conceptually simple algorithm that aims to rank items based on their age and popularity. Every upvote a post gets makes the score of this post grow, but as time passes, the score reduces.

This is often called a "gravity" algorithm because it resembles how objects behave under gravity: they initially start with a lot of potential energy and then lose it over time. The specific implementation can be adjusted to suit your needs, but a very straightforward one could look like this:

```
gravity = 1.8; // can be adjusted to your needs
hoursSincePublication = (current_time - time_of_publication) / 3600;
score = (number_of_votes) / pow(hoursSincePublication + 2, gravity);
```
That is the general idea. You take the number of votes and divide it by the time since publication (in hours, hence the division by 3600 in the example; you need to convert your times to the same units) to some power. The '+2' in the denominator is to prevent division by zero and to diminish the advantage of very fresh posts.

If you want to implement it in C#, it could look something like this:

```csharp
double CalculateScore(int numberOfVotes, DateTime publicationDate, double gravity = 1.8)
{
    double ageInHours = (DateTime.UtcNow - publicationDate).TotalHours;
    return numberOfVotes / Math.Pow(ageInHours + 2, gravity);
}
```

This implementation is simplified and does not take into account other factors like the score of the post or author reputation in ranking items that might be relevant for your application. You would have to extend it to take these into account.

