import java.util.Stack;

public class MainClass {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(correctBracketing("<{([)]}>"));  // Outputs: false
        System.out.println(correctBracketing("<{([])}>"));  // Outputs: true
    }

    public static boolean correctBracketing(String brackets) {
        // A Stack is used to keep track of opening brackets
        Stack<Character> checkBrackets = new Stack<>();

        // Loop through all the characters in the input string
        for (char a : brackets.toCharArray()) {
            switch (a) {
                case '<':
                case '{':
                case '[':
                case '(':
                    // If an opening bracket is found, push it onto the Stack
                    checkBrackets.push(a);
                    break;
                case '>':
                    // If a closing bracket is found, and the top of the Stack is its opening counterpart, discard the pair 
                    if (checkBrackets.empty() || checkBrackets.peek() != '<') return false;
                    else checkBrackets.pop();
                    break;
                case '}':
                    if (checkBrackets.empty() || checkBrackets.peek() != '{') return false;
                    else checkBrackets.pop();
                    break;
                case ']':
                    if (checkBrackets.empty() || checkBrackets.peek() != '[') return false;
                    else checkBrackets.pop();
                    break;
                case ')':
                    if (checkBrackets.empty() || checkBrackets.peek() != '(') return false;
                    else checkBrackets.pop();
                    break;
                default: 
                    // If any other character shows up, return false
                    return false;
            }
        }
        // If all opening brackets have managed to find their closing counterparts (meaning the Stack is empty), the bracketing is correct - return true
        return checkBrackets.empty();
    }
}
```
This is a very common algorithm used to check if brackets/Parenthesis are balanced in a string. The stack data structure is used here as it follows the LIFO (Last In, First Out) rule which is suitable for this scenario where the most recently opened bracket needs to be closed first.

