Yes, there is a way to hook SDL into a Qt application. SDL can be given a native window handle to embed its rendering into but this isn't a feature that's officially supported. It's possible but you should tread carefully as it can present complications.

You can create a widget in Qt, retrieve the native window handle, then pass that handle to SDL using the function "SDL_CreateWindowFrom". This will create an instance of an SDL_Window that you can render to using SDL_RenderPresent.

Here is an example code:
```cpp
// Assume the QWidget *widget has been created
WId id = widget->winId();

// Just to cast the WId to what SDL expects
void *voidid = reinterpret_cast<void *>(id);

// Create SDL_Window from the QWidget
SDL_Window *sdlWindow = SDL_CreateWindowFrom(voidid);
```
It's important to remember that you won't be able to directly interact with the SDL_Window using SDL's event system, since the window's events will be captured by Qt. You would still be able to indirectly influence the window by influencing the QWidget or QWidgetWindow hosting it and will have to use Qt to pick up the user's interaction and to control the dimensions of the window.


As for threading, SDL threads should not present an issue themselves. You would, however, have to make sure any communication between SDL and Qt happens on the same thread that created the QApplication. This is due to the fact that Qt's GUI classes are not thread-safe. Therefore, you can't manipulate them from multiple threads.

Bear in mind, since this isn't an officially supported SDL feature, you run the risk of running into various platform-specific bugs and issues. This wouldn't be a problem if you're only targeting a single platform but if you're aiming for cross-platform compatibility, you might run into problems. If you'd like to remove SDL from the equation, you can consider using QOpenGLWidget (replaces the older QGLWidget) for rendering directly within your Qt application. This would certainly be a more reliable and sustainable solution.

