The problem maps directly to a well known problem called Permutation matrices up to simultaneous permutations and negations. There are many ways to formulate the problem in combinatorial language, but essentially we are looking for the number of non-isomorphic objects under a certain relation.

Let n denote the size of the matrix and let p_n denote the number of non-isomorphic structures. Then the numbers p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4, p_5, ... are 1, 2, 5, 15, 52, 203, 877, 4140, 21147, ... .

The specific formula for p_n is not elementary but is expressible in terms of a summation involving products of Stirling numbers of the second kind:
p_n = Sum_{k=0}^n {n\choose k} (2^k - 1) S2(n,n-k)
where S2(n,k) denotes the Stirling number of the second kind, i.e., the number of ways to partition a set of n labelled objects into k non-empty unlabelled subsets.

Since np_n grows super-exponentially, it can be conjectured that p_n is approximately c^n n(-3/2) for some constant c. The numbers p_1, p_2, p_3, ... listed above indeed support this conjecture and the first 20 values give an estimate of c ≈ 2.438, which can be used as a good approximation of c.

We have a formula for np_n that we can use to implement Python code as follows.

