import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Collections;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedList<Integer> values = new LinkedList<>(Arrays.asList(3, 6, 12, 24, 48));
        Collections.reverse(values);
        long sum = 0;

        for (Integer num : values) {
            String binaryStr = Integer.toBinaryString(num);
            String reversedBinaryStr = new StringBuilder(binaryStr).reverse().toString();
            long reversedBinaryNum = Long.parseLong(reversedBinaryStr, 2);
            sum += reversedBinaryNum;
        }

        System.out.println(sum);

        // check for overflow
        if (sum < 0) {
            System.out.println("Overflow occurred");
        }
    }
}
```
This program first reverses the LinkedList using Java's built-in Collections.reverse() method. Then it iterates through the LinkedList and converts each number to binary using the Integer.toBinaryString() method. After that, it uses StringBuilder to reverse the binary string, and finally parses the string back to a long integer using Long.parseLong() method. The sum of all reversed binary numbers is calculated in the same loop.

If a signed overflow occurs, the sum will become negative due to Java's twos-complement representation of integers, and we print an error message to inform about the occurrence of overflow.

