The Perl language does not have defined types for input parameters like Python does, but its typographical inferences serve to allow most of the same functionality. Logical statements for raising exceptions need to be separately managed, as Perl does not provide the same "Raise Exception" functionality natively. Here is the Perl version of your code:

```perl
sub advanced_sort {
    my ($l_ref, $n) = @_;
    my @l = @$l_ref; 

    die "All elements in list should be integers\n" if grep {$_ !~ /^\d+$/ } @l; 

    die "Second input should be an integer greater than 0.\n" unless ($n =~ /^\d+$/) && ($n > 0);

    # Creating two lists: one for items at indices divisible by n, one for all other items
    my @div_by_n = map { $l[$_] * 2 } grep { $_ % $n == 0 } 0..$#l;
    my @not_div_by_n = map { $l[$_] } grep { $_ % $n != 0 } 0..$#l;

    # Sorting div_by_n in descending order
    @div_by_n = sort {$b <=> $a} @div_by_n;

    # Creating a new array that puts items from not_div_by_n and div_by_n into correct positions
    my @result = ("") x scalar @l; 
    @result[grep { $_ % $n == 0 } 0..$#result] = @div_by_n;
    my $len = ()= grep { $_ % $n == 1 } 0..$#result; 
    @result[grep { $_ % $n == 1 } 0..$#result] = @not_div_by_n[0..($len-1)];
    @result[grep { $_ % $n == ($n-1) } 0..$#result] = @not_div_by_n[$len..$#not_div_by_n];

    return @result;
}
```
Parameters to Perl subroutines are passed as array references, hence they are dereferenced at the beginning of the subroutine. The regex operator =~ is used to check that every item in the list @l is an integer and that $n is a positive integer. Lists are zero-indexed in Perl, just like in Python, and the $#array syntax gives the last index of @array. The grep function is used to filter array elements based on a condition. The sort {$b <=> $a} @div_by_n sorts the @div_by_n array in descending order. The die function is used to raise exceptions.

