The problem here is that you are trying to resolve a scoped service (EFDataContext) from the root provider during the initialization process, which is a Singleton. This conflict in lifetime scopes is causing the exception. When you create the logger, there is no active scope to which you can add scoped services like EFDataContext.

Instead of trying to provide the DbContext via the constructor, you can use the DbContextFactory in .Net Core 5.0 and later, and inject that into the logger which will allow you to create a scoped instance of the DbContext whenever you need.

Here's how you can implement it:

```csharp
public class CustomLogger : ILogger
{
    private readonly IDbContextFactory<EFDataContext> _dbContextFactory; 

    public CustomLogger(IDbContextFactory<EFDataContext> dbContextFactory) 
    { 
        _dbContextFactory = dbContextFactory; 
    } 

    //... the rest of your logger implementation

    public void LogSomething()
    {
        using (var context = _dbContextFactory.CreateDbContext())
        {
            //... use the dbcontext here
        }
    }
}
```
Then in your `Program.cs`:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddDbContextFactory<DataContext>();
builder.Services.AddSingleton<ILogger, CustomLogger>(); 

var app = builder.Build(); 
app.Run();
```

This way, whenever your logger needs a DbContext to log something to the database, it will create a separate scoped instance for only this job. Be aware though that this DbContext will not share transactions or other scopes with other DbContexts being used for HTTP requests or other processing.

