This problem can be solved using a hash map and the palindrome properties.

Create a dictionary with the reverse of every word in the array indexed by its position. Start by examining each word in the array and its reverse in the dictionary.

Add a condition to check for any word that is a palindrome, and if the dictionary contains an empty string, then the word and its reverse permutation will appear as a palindrome pair.

For all other cases, check the word if it can be broken such that, one part is a palindrome, and the other part's reverse is present in the map. If yes, we get a pair. Be sure to do this for both suffix and prefix because they have different index positions.

Here is a python solution following this approach:

```python
def palindromePairs(words):
	revWords = {w[::-1]: i for i, w in enumerate(words)}
	result = []
	for i, word in enumerate(words):
		for j in range(len(word)+1):
			prefix, postfix = word[:j], word[j:]
			if prefix == prefix[::-1] and revWords.get(postfix, -1) != -1 and revWords[postfix] != i:
				result.append([revWords[postfix], i])
			if j != len(word) and postfix == postfix[::-1] and revWords.get(prefix, -1) != -1 and revWords[prefix] != i:
				result.append([i, revWords[prefix]])
	return result
```

This Python code uses dictionary to store and lookup reversed words in O(1) time complexity. For each word, it checks all possible prefixes and postfixes. It checks if they exist in the map and if their remaining parts are palindrome or not. The algorithm runs in O(nk^2) where n is the number of words and k is the maximum length of a word.

