Of course, the best solution would depend on your specific use case. I would consider the `SaveChanges` override for a simple application where only one DbContext is used, and triggering fields are similar throughout all entities. If the application is complex and uses multiple DbContexts or has a variety of triggering fields, I would consider using Triggers (stored procedures) or a more advanced change tracking system.

