Here is a Java implementation of a thread-safe Enum-based Multiton with lazy-loading. This class is named ResourcePool, and it limits the pool size to a pre-defined maximum. All the resource objects are stored in an `ArrayList`. The `obtain()` method is used for resource allocation, and `release()` is used for resource deallocation.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public enum ResourcePool {
    INSTANCE;

    private static final int MAX_POOL_SIZE = 10;
    private final List<Resource> resources = new ArrayList<>(MAX_POOL_SIZE);
    private int currentIndex = 0;

    public Resource obtain() {
        if (currentIndex < MAX_POOL_SIZE) {
            if (currentIndex >= resources.size()) {
                resources.add(new Resource());
            }
            return resources.get(currentIndex++);
        } else {
            throw new RuntimeException("No more resources available.");
        }
    }

    public void release(Resource resource) {
        int index = resources.indexOf(resource);
        if (index == -1 || index >= currentIndex) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid resource.");
        }
        swap(resources, index, --currentIndex);
    }

    private static void swap(List<Resource> list, int i, int j) {
        Resource temp = list.get(i);
        list.set(i, list.get(j));
        list.set(j, temp);
    }

    public static class Resource {
        // Add fields and methods for this class as necessary.
    }
}
```

Multiton differs from Singleton in their allocation behavior. Singleton belongs to the creational design patterns and restricts instantiation of a class to a single instance, ensuring that a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access to it. Meanwhile, Multiton is effectively a generalization of Singleton to control the number of instances, maintaining a map of keys and instances as a cache, giving global point of access.

Singleton is simpler, but it may not be suitable when you need multiple instances that can communicate with each other but still restrict the number of instances somehow, which is where a Multiton comes in. 

However, both patterns can become problematic due to global state they are introducing. State can be mutated by any portion of the calling code, leading to issues that are hard to debug.

Thread safety is vital when implementing Singleton and Multiton as it ensures only one instance (in Singleton) is maintained even in a multithreaded environment. 

Lazy-loading only initializes the Singleton or Multiton instance when needed, which leads to better performance and resource management but increased complexity. In contrast, eager loading initializes the instance beforehand regardless of whether it's needed, reducing complexity but potentially wasting resources.

In conclusion, the use of Singleton or Multiton should be based on the specific requirement. Just be careful not to overuse them and handle their thread safety and initialization properly.

