Concurrency problems occur when multiple processes access and manipulate the same data simultaneously, potentially leading to inconsistent or undesirable results. In the scenario you presented, if two users attempt to select an available serial number at the same time, they could both end up selecting the same serial number before one of them has a chance to set it to unavailable.

To avoid concurrent problems, you could leverage transaction handling and row-level locking.

Here is an example of how this could be done:

```java
try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(dbUrl, username, password)) {
    try {
        conn.setAutoCommit(false); // Disable auto-commit to start transaction

        // Using FOR UPDATE to lock the selected row
        try (PreparedStatement selectStmt = conn.prepareStatement(
                "SELECT id, sn FROM serial_number WHERE available = 1 LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE")) {
            ResultSet result = selectStmt.executeQuery();
            if (!result.next()) {
                throw new RuntimeException("No available serial numbers");
            }
            int id = result.getInt(1);
            // Update the same row within the transaction
            try (PreparedStatement updateStmt = conn.prepareStatement(
                    "UPDATE serial_number SET available = 0 WHERE id = ?")) {
                updateStmt.setInt(1, id);
                updateStmt.executeUpdate();
            }
        }
        conn.commit(); // Complete the transaction
    }
    catch (Exception e) {
        if (conn != null) {
            try {
                conn.rollback(); // Rollback changes in case of an exception
            } catch (SQLException ex) {
                ex.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
}
```

Please ensure that your transaction isolation level allows row-level locking. MySQL's default isolation level is REPEATABLE READ and it does allow this. However, if the isolation level has been changed, you may need to set it back. You can do this by executing the SQL command `SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;`

Also, note that the query `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` locks the selected rows until the transaction is either committed or rolled back. Therefore, it's important to manage your transactions properly and make sure they always get committed or rolled back to avoid deadlocks.

