Here's an example of how to generate Fibonacci sequences and then figure out if they are prime or not, as well as whether they are even or odd:

```javascript
// isPrime function to check if a number is prime or not
function isPrime(num) {
    for(let i = 2, sqrt = Math.sqrt(num); i <= sqrt; i++)
        if(num % i === 0) return false; 
    return num > 1;
}

// generateFib function to generate the Fibonacci sequence up to a specified index
function generateFib(index) {
    let arr = [0, 1];
    for(let i = 2; i < index; i++) {
        arr.push(arr[i - 2] + arr[i - 1]);
    }
    return arr;
}

// Main function
function primeFib(z) {
    let sequence = generateFib(z);
    let primeFibs = [];
    let evens = 0, odds = 0;
    for(let num of sequence) {
        if(isPrime(num)) {
            primeFibs.push(num);
            if(num % 2 === 0) evens++;
            else odds++;
        }
    }
    console.log('Even prime Fibonaccis: ' + evens);
    console.log('Odd prime Fibonaccis: ' + odds);
    return primeFibs;
}

console.log(primeFib(10000));
```
However, note that this program is likely to be unworkably slow for large inputs (like 10,000) because of the inefficiency of both Fibonacci sequence generation and primality testing algorithms. Both of these problems are well-known examples of problems where naive algorithms have poor performance, and various optimization strategies exist.

Here are more efficient ways to generate Fibonacci sequences and prime numbers, but they might be a bit more difficult to understand if you are a beginner. They require more advanced programming knowledge.

Optimization can be smart for Fibonacci numbers because each number depends on just the two before it, so it's not necessary to store the entire sequence in an array (which uses a lot of memory and time). Instead, you can just store the last two numbers.

Optimization is possible for prime numbers because you can use the fact that a number is prime if it has no divisors other than 1 and itself. So to check if a number is prime, you don't need to try dividing it by EVERY smaller number to see if you find a divisor. You only need to check divisors up to the square root of the number.

