I'll explain each step of the Go code with comments:

```go
package main

import "fmt"

func trifib(n int) int {
	// Check if n is below 0. The sequence doesn't exist for such values, so return -1.
	if n < 0 {
		return -1 // In Go, there is no direct equivalent to Python's None. -1 can be used as an equivalent here.
	}

	// Initialize a slice (Go's version of a dynamic array) with the base cases for the tribonacci sequence.
	trifibSlice := []int{0, 0, 1}

	// Loop until our trislice has n + 1 elements.
	for len(trifibSlice) <= n {
		// Calculate the next number in the sequence by summing the last three numbers of the trislice.
		nextNum := trifibSlice[len(trifibSlice) - 1] + trifibSlice[len(trifibSlice) - 2] + trifibSlice[len(trifibSlice) - 3]
		// Add this number to trifibSlice.
		trifibSlice = append(trifibSlice, nextNum)
	}

	// Get the n-th number from our trislice.
