You can solve this problem by using two data structures - a 'deck' which will serve as the list of numbers, and a 'prefix' array which will store the prefix product of our numbers. 

The last element in prefix is the total product of our list. To get the product of the 'k' last numbers, you can divide the last element in prefix by the kth to last element of the prefix (prefix(Size() - k)).

First you initialize a new 'deck' and a new prefix array, where you push back '1' to the prefix array.

When you add a number, you multiply it with the last element of the prefix and add it to the end.

Whenever you get a product of 'k' numbers, you return prefix[deck.size() - 1] divided by prefix[deck.size()-1-k].

When you remove a number from the deck you pop back the last number, then do the same for the prefix array.

Here is an implementation using C++:

```cpp
class ProductOfNumbers {
private:
    deque<int> deck;
    vector<int> prefix;
public:
    ProductOfNumbers() {
        prefix.push_back(1);
    }

    void add(int num) {
        deck.push_back(num);

        if(num == 0) {
            prefix.clear();
            prefix.push_back(1);
        }
        else {
            int prod = prefix.back() * num;
            prefix.push_back(prod);
        }
    }

    int getProduct(int k) {
        if(k >= prefix.size()) return 0;
        else return prefix.back() / prefix[prefix.size()-1-k];
    }

    void removeLast() {
        if(deck.back() == 0) {
            prefix.clear();
            prefix.push_back(1);
        }
        else {
            prefix.pop_back();
        }
        deck.pop_back();
    }
};
```

In this code, when you add a '0', you need to clear the prefix array and start over. The reason is that all the numbers after a '0' in the deck list will give a product of '0' because any number multiplied by '0' gives you '0'. However, the numbers before the '0' are irrelevant to the product of numbers after '0' in the deck list. This is why you can clear the prefix array and start again.

