public class Main {
  public static int findLongestCommonSubsequence(String str1, String str2){
    int n = str1.length();
    int m = str2.length();
    int[][] dp = new int[n+1][m+1];

    for(int i=0; i<=n; i++){
      for(int j=0; j<=m; j++){
        if(i == 0 || j == 0)
          dp[i][j] = 0;
        else if(str1.charAt(i-1) == str2.charAt(j-1))
          dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1;
        else
          dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]);
      }
    }
    return dp[n][m];
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    System.out.println(findLongestCommonSubsequence("AGGTAB", "GXTXAYB"));
  }
}
```

The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n*m) where n and m are the lengths of the two input strings, because each cell of the dp array is computed once. The space complexity is also O(n*m) due to the 2D dp array that is created.

The function `findLongestCommonSubsequence` uses a technique called Dynamic Programming to compute the length of the longest common subsequence. It does so by creating a 2D array `dp`, where `dp[i][j]` is the length of the longest common subsequence of the first `i` characters of `str1` and the first `j` characters of `str2`.

The dp array is filled in an iterative manner. When `i` or `j` is 0, which means one of the strings has no characters, the longest common subsequence is of length 0. When the characters at `str1[i-1]` and `str2[j-1]` are equal, the longest common subsequence includes this character and is of length `dp[i-1][j-1] + 1`. Otherwise, the longest common subsequence is the maximum of those obtained by removing the last character from either `str1` or `str2`.

The last cell of the dp array, `dp[n][m]`, gives the length of the longest common subsequence of the two strings.

