In `UIKit`, `UIWindow` is a variable that you have to initialize yourself, and the system then calls its properties and methods as needed. But in case of `SwiftUI`, `body` is not a variable but a computed property, that the system is expecting as a requirement to construct your views. If you don't provide `body` property in your `View`, SwiftUI won't know how to render your view.

So, No, you cannot change the name. In the `View` protocol, the `body` property isn’t just a random name that SwiftUI happens to use – it’s a core part of the protocol, so all conforming types must use it exactly as is.

If you look at the `View` protocol definition you will see something like this:

```
public protocol View {

    // The type of view representing the body of this view.
    associatedtype Body : View

    // The view's body.
    @ViewBuilder var body: Self.Body { get }
}
```

As you can see, `body` is declared as a requirement of the `View` protocol, meaning, anything complying with the protocol HAS to implement that property, there is no choice as to its name or the way it's implemented. 

So, changing it to 'presentation' means you are not providing the `body` property as required by the protocol, hence the error. By the way, you can still use `presentation` in combination with `body` if you like. You simply return `presentation` inside your `body` implementation like so:

