A common way to solve this problem is to implement a thread-safe stack using condition variables and counting semaphores.

Class `ThreadSafeStack` is used to implement Stack with `push()` and `pop()` methods. It has a semaphore to hold and synchronize the count of the elements. `push()` method is used by producer threads to insert elements into the stack. `pop()` method is used by consumer threads to remove and get element from the stack. `size()` method returns the size of the stack.

Here is an implementation in C++:

```cpp
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <vector>
#include <stdexcept>
using namespace std;

class ThreadSafeStack
{
private:
    vector<int> data;
    size_t capacity;
    mutex m;
    condition_variable not_empty;
    condition_variable not_full;

public:
    ThreadSafeStack(size_t capacity) : capacity(capacity)
    {
    }

    void push(int element)
    {
        unique_lock<mutex> lock(m);

        not_full.wait(lock, [this] { return data.size() != capacity; });

        data.push_back(element);
        not_empty.notify_one();
    }

    int pop()
    {
        unique_lock<mutex> lock(m);

        not_empty.wait(lock, [this] { return !data.empty(); });

        int result = data.back();
        data.pop_back();
        not_full.notify_one();

        return result;
    }

    size_t size()
    {
        lock_guard<mutex> lock(m);
        return data.size();
    }
};
```

This implementation uses a std::vector to hold the stack data. We use a std::mutex (m) to ensure mutual exclusion when accessing the stack and two std::condition_variable (not_empty and not_full) to block threads when necessary.

When a producer thread calls push, it first acquire the lock, then it checks if the stack is full. If the stack is full, it will wait on the not_full condition variable, releasing the lock, until another thread fires a notification on this condition variable (i.e., a full stack has an item popped from it), then it will attempt to re-acquire the lock. Once the producer thread successfully re-acquires the lock, it checks the condition again, if the condition is met (the stack is not full), it will continue to push the item to the stack, then notify a blocked consumer thread (if any) by firing a notification on the not_empty condition_variable.

The pop behavior is symmetric with the exception that consumer thread waits on the not_empty condition variable and notifies not_full condition variable. The size() function simply locks the mutex and return the size of the data.

This implementation ensures that threads attempting to call push on a full stack or pop on an empty stack will block until the stack is not full / empty. Threads calling size() will always get an accurate size. It guarantees thread safety and dead lock free as long as the number of push calls is no less than pop calls.

