Here is the Java code that demonstrates the solution using `Semaphore` and multithreading.

```java
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;

// Represent the buffer between producer and consumer
class Buffer {
    private LinkedList<Integer> buffer;
    private int size;

    public Buffer(int size) {
        this.buffer = new LinkedList<>();
        this.size = size;
    }

    public void add(int num) {
        this.buffer.add(num);
    }

    public int remove() {
        return this.buffer.remove();
    }

    public boolean isFull() {
        return this.buffer.size() == this.size;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return this.buffer.size() == 0;
    }
}

// Represent the producer in producer-consumer problem
class Producer extends Thread {
    private Buffer buffer;
    private Semaphore mutex;
    private Semaphore empty;
    private Semaphore full;

    public Producer(Buffer buffer, Semaphore mutex, Semaphore empty, Semaphore full) {
        this.buffer = buffer;
        this.mutex = mutex;
        this.empty = empty;
        this.full = full;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            empty.acquire();    //decrease empty count
            mutex.acquire();    //acquire exclusive lock

            // section of producing an item and adding it to the buffer 
            buffer.add(1);

            System.out.println("Produced an item. Buffer Size : " + buffer.size());

            mutex.release();    //release exclusive lock
            full.release();     //increase full count
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}

// Represent the consumer in producer-consumer problem
class Consumer extends Thread {
    private Buffer buffer;
    private Semaphore mutex;
    private Semaphore empty;
    private Semaphore full;

    public Consumer(Buffer buffer, Semaphore mutex, Semaphore empty, Semaphore full) {
        this.buffer = buffer;
        this.mutex = mutex;
        this.empty = empty;
        this.full = full;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            full.acquire();     //decrease full count
            mutex.acquire();    //acquire exclusive lock

            // section of consuming an item from the buffer 
            buffer.remove();

            System.out.println("Consumed an item. Buffer Size : " + buffer.size());

            mutex.release();    //release exclusive lock
            empty.release();    //increase empty count
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}

public class ProducerConsumerSolution {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int numProducer = 4;
        int numConsumer = 3;
        int bufferSize = 3;

        Buffer buffer = new Buffer(bufferSize);
        Semaphore mutex = new Semaphore(1); // binary semaphore for mutual exclusion, 1 means it is unlocked
        Semaphore empty = new Semaphore(bufferSize); // counting semaphore for empty slots in the buffer
        Semaphore full = new Semaphore(0); // counting semaphore for full slots in the buffer

        while (true) {
            for (int i = 0; i < numProducer; i++) {
                new Producer(buffer, mutex, empty, full).start();
            }

            for (int i = 0; i < numConsumer; i++) {
                new Consumer(buffer, mutex, empty, full).start();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, we create a buffer shared by producer and consumer threads. We use three types of semaphores: 

- `mutex` for ensuring mutual exclusion when accessing the buffer (this prevents race conditions where two threads might try to modify the buffer simultaneously). 
- `empty` to keep track of the number of empty slots in the buffer (this prevents a producer from adding to the buffer when it is full). 
- `full` to keep track of the number of items in the buffer (this prevents a consumer from trying to consume when the buffer is empty). 

This way, we ensure synchronized access to the shared buffer between multiple producers and consumers while preventing and handling potential race conditions.

