Here is a simple program that demonstrates the functionality you are asking for. It involves creating multiple threads and a thread-safe shared resource (in this case, a `StringBuilder`). After all threads finish their execution, the `StringBuilder` is then used to produce a concatenated string that represents the items in the list in reverse order.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class Main {
    private static StringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4, 5);

        // Reverse the order of the list
        Collections.reverse(numbers);

        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(numbers.size());

        for (Integer number : numbers) {
            executor.execute(new PrintTask(number));
        }

        executor.shutdown();
        executor.awaitTermination(1, TimeUnit.MINUTES);

        System.out.println(builder.toString());
    }

    static class PrintTask implements Runnable {
        private int number;

        PrintTask(int number) {
            this.number = number;
        }

        @Override
        public void run() {
            synchronized (builder) {
                builder.append(number);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This program creates a thread for every item in the list. Each thread is then responsible for appending its respective item to the shared resource represented by `builder`. Since buffer builder access is not thread-safe by default, it is necessary to synchronize the block of code that modifies it. Note also that the list of numbers is reversed before creating the threads to ensure the output is in reverse chronological order as per the requirement. The `ExecutorService` is a higher-level replacement for working with threads directly and provides a number of useful features for managing and controlling thread execution.

