```

- In-Order Traversal: In inorder traversal a node is visited after its left child but before its right child. The order of traversal is left child -> root node -> right child. If applied to a binary search tree, it visits the nodes in ascending order.

- Pre-Order Traversal: In pre-order traversal a node is visited before its children. The order of traversal is root node -> left child -> right child. This can be used to create a copy of the tree, or to get prefix expression of an expression tree.

- Post-Order Traversal: In post-order traversal a node is visited after its children. The order of traversal is left child -> right child -> root node. Used to delete the tree, or to get the postfix expression of an expression tree.

Note: Since the root node is initially created empty in the original code, it has been modified such that the root node is created with a value. Also, tree traversal methods are included expecting a node as an argument, to start traversal from a certain node within the tree and not just the root. You can use it on the root with tree.in_order_traversal(tree.root).

