Below is a Java program using ExecutorService and Callable from the java.util.concurrent package to sum the values in a dynamically filled array in a multi-threaded way. This program creates a fixed thread pool executor with chunk size and divides the array into chunks, each processed by a separate thread. It avoids concurrency issues by not sharing mutable data between threads.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.concurrent.Callable;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;

public class ConcurrentSum {
    private static final int ARRAY_SIZE = 1_000_000;
    private static final int CHUNK_SIZE = 50_000;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Create and fill the array
        int[] arr = new int[ARRAY_SIZE];
        for (int i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE; i++) {
            arr[i] = i + 1;
        }

        // Calculate the sum with multiple threads
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(ARRAY_SIZE / CHUNK_SIZE);
        List<Future<Integer>> futures = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE; i += CHUNK_SIZE) {
            final int start = i;
            final int end = Math.min(i + CHUNK_SIZE, ARRAY_SIZE);
            futures.add(executor.submit(new Callable<Integer>() {
                public Integer call() {
                    return sumArray(arr, start, end);
                }
            }));
        }

        int totalSum = 0;
        for (Future<Integer> future : futures) {
            totalSum += future.get();
        }

        executor.shutdown();
        System.out.println("Total Sum: " + totalSum);
    }

    private static int sumArray(int[] arr, int start, int end) {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = start; i < end; i++) {
            sum += arr[i];
        }
        return sum;
    }
}
```

This program assumes that the array can only be updated after the calculation has started, and that no other threads are modifying the array concurrently. If that is not the case, you would need to use some form of concurrency control, like the synchronized keyword or a lock, to ensure safe access to the array.

