To design the solution for this problem, we can use a Hashmap data structure. The hashmap can store each contact details with key as the full name and the value as an object of class Contact having details as email address, phone number and physical address. This will ensure constant time complexity O(1) for both inserting and searching.

The Contact class can be designed like:

```
class Contact {
    String name;
    String emailAddress;
    List<String> phoneNumbers;
    String physicalAddress;
    //constructor, getters and setters 
}
```

Here, we are using a list for phoneNumbers to accommodate multiple contact numbers for a single contact.

1. For Insertion (allowing a new contact), we simply create a new object of contact and put it into the map. 
2. For Deletion, remove the corresponding key-value pair from the hashmap.
3. For Updating the details, get the object by using the key and update the contents of the object.
4. For Search, we first search by name (key in the map) which takes O(1) time. In order to search by email address, we would need to iterate through all the entries in the map which takes O(n) time as we need to compare the email address of n entries.
5. For adding multiple contact numbers or email addresses, just add to the list.
6. For sorting by last name, convert the map values to list then sort the list using any sorting algorithm. The time complexity for this operation would be O(nlogn).
7. For removing duplicates, consider full name, phone number and email address. This can be done by comparing every Contact object with every other contact object which would take O(n^2) time.

However, to optimize search by email and deletion of duplicates, we can maintain an additional hashmap for email and a set for contacts. The additional structures would take more space, but give a better time complexity. For search by email, the time complexity becomes O(1) and for deletion of duplicates, the time complexity becomes O(n) by using a set. 

This data structure solution offers a balance between efficiency and complexity - it is arguably one of the simplest data structures to understand and use, and most operations are very efficient.

