Python is selected as the language to implement this. This data structure is implemented by using doubly linked list along with two variables to store the smallest and largest elements. The smallest and largest elements are updated with every insertion and deletion to ensure the O(1) access.

```python
class Node:
  def __init__(self, value):
    self.value = value
    self.next = None
    self.prev = None


class DoublyLinkedList:
  def __init__(self):
    self.head = None
    self.tail = None
    self.smallest = None
    self.largest = None

  # Insertion at the end of the list
  def insert(self, value):
    new_node = Node(value)

    if not self.head:
      self.head = self.tail = new_node
      self.smallest = self.largest = value
    else:
      new_node.prev = self.tail
      self.tail.next = new_node
      self.tail = new_node

      self.smallest = min(self.smallest, value)
      self.largest = max(self.largest, value)

  # Deletion from the end of the list   
  def delete(self):
    if not self.head:
      return

    if self.head == self.tail:
      self.head = self.tail = None
    else:
      self.tail = self.tail.prev
      self.tail.next = None

    # If the largest or smallest value is deleted, recalculate them
    if self.head.value == self.largest or self.head.value == self.smallest:
      node = self.head
      self.smallest = self.largest = node.value

      while node:
        self.smallest = min(self.smallest, node.value)
        self.largest = max(self.largest, node.value)
        node = node.next

  # Access the smallest and the largest elements
  def access_elements(self):
    return {'Smallest': self.smallest, 'Largest': self.largest}
```
With this implementation, any insertions and deletions from the end of the list are done in O(1) time as we directly manipulate pointers. Updating the smallest and largest elements also take O(1) time while insertion but can take up to O(n) time in case of deletion as we might have to recalculate them traversing through the list again.

