This would be a simple way to implement a lock combination algorithm in Java using a string array, where each string represents a combination code. A correct combination code can open the lock.

```java
public class Lock {
    private String[] lockCombination;
    private boolean isOpen;

    public Lock(String[] lockCombination) {
        this.lockCombination = lockCombination;
        this.isOpen = false;
    }

    public boolean open(String[] attempt) {
        if (isCorrect(attempt)) {
            this.isOpen = true;
            System.out.println("Lock is open.");
            return true;
        } else {
            System.out.println("Failed to open the lock.");
            return false;
        }
    }

    public void close() {
        this.isOpen = false;
    }

    private boolean isCorrect(String[] attempt) {
        if (attempt.length != lockCombination.length) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < attempt.length; i++) {
            if (!attempt[i].equals(lockCombination[i])) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

In this example, the `Lock` class is initialized with an array of strings representing the correct combination. When the method `open` is called, an attempt to open the lock is made using the input array of code. If the method `isCorrect` returns true, the lock is opened, otherwise it remains closed. The lock can also be closed using the `close` method.

Here is an example usage of the `Lock` class:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a lock with combination {"A", "B", "C"}
        Lock lock = new Lock(new String[]{"A", "B", "C"});

        // Try to open the lock with a wrong combination {"X", "Y", "Z"}
        lock.open(new String[]{"X", "Y", "Z"}); // Print: Failed to open the lock.

        // Now try to open the lock with a correct combination {"A", "B", "C"}
        lock.open(new String[]{"A", "B", "C"}); // Print: Lock is open.

        // Close the lock
        lock.close();
    }
}
```

