Let's now answer your other question regarding CSS priorities.

CSS priorities operate on a notion called specificity where certain types of selectors are considered more specific than others and therefore take precedence when conflicting rules are applied to the same HTML element.

Here's a break-down of the CSS specificity hierarchy:

1. Inline Styles: An inline style is attached directly to the element to be styled. These styles are most specific and have the highest priority. Example: `<p style="color: blue;">This is a paragraph.</p>`

2. ID Selectors: These are styles applied by unique id. Example: `#uniqueParagraph {color: blue;}`

3. Class Selectors: These are styles applied by class. Example: `.blueText {color: blue;}`

4. Tag Selectors: At the lowest specificity are tag selectors. Example: `p {color: blue;}`

5. Universal Selectors: These have no specificity value. Example: `* {color: blue;}`

CSS styles also follow a top-down parsing which means that rules that come later and have the same specificity as preceding rules will overwrite the preceding rules.

If there's a conflict between external, internal, and inline styles, the style that has the highest specificity will be applied.

Here's how you can mitigate conflicts:

1. Try to avoid using inline styles: While these have the highest specificity, they are not a good practice and can lead to confusion and conflicts.

2. Use classes instead of IDs: While using IDs can be tempting due to their high specificity, it can lead to conflicts in the CSS. It is good practice to use classes that can be reused.

3. Keep your CSS DRY: DRY stands for Don't Repeat Yourself. Try to avoid repeating CSS rules.

4. Be consistent in your CSS structure: Try to maintain a consistent structure in your CSS. This will help you to avoid conflicts.

5. When situations call for higher specificity, you can employ chaining of class selectors and/or use CSS combinators wisely to increase the specificity.

