Prototype inheritance is a crucial concept in JavaScript. It is based on prototypes where you can create an object which can serve as a prototype for other objects. The objects created from these prototypes inherit properties and methods. 

In JavaScript, each object has a private property which holds a link to another object called its prototype. That prototype object has a prototype of its own, and so on until an object is reached with null as its prototype. Null, by definition, has no prototype, and acts as the final link in this prototype chain.

When trying to access a property that does not exist in an object, JavaScript tries to find this property in the object's prototype. This explains the so-called prototype chain.

