def median(l):
    """Returns the median of the elements in the list l, without sorting it or using built-in functions.
    Handles types with even and odd numbers of elements, as well as duplicate elements."""
    n = len(l)
    if n < 1:
        return None
    if n % 2 == 1:    
        return find_kth_smallest(l, n // 2)
    else:
        return ( find_kth_smallest(l, n // 2 - 1) + find_kth_smallest(l, n // 2) ) / 2.0

def find_kth_smallest(l, k):
    if l:
        pivot = l[0]
        smaller_list = [x for x in l if x < pivot]
        bigger_list = [x for x in l if x > pivot]
        equal_list = [x for x in l if x == pivot]
        m = len(smaller_list)
        n = len(equal_list)
        if k < m:
            return find_kth_smallest(smaller_list, k)
        elif k < m + n:
            return equal_list[0]
        else:
            return find_kth_smallest(bigger_list, k - m - n)

print(median([3, 1, 2, 4, 5]))  # expecting 3

print(median([-10, 0, 6, 1000, 10, 20])) # expecting 15.0

print(median([1, 2, 2, 4, 5])) # expecting 2
```

Below is the implementation of a program in Java that generates all permutations of a given sequence of characters.

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        char[] characters = { 'A', 'B', 'C' };
        generatePermutations(characters, 0, characters.length-1);
    }

    private static void generatePermutations(char[] characters, int left, int right) {

        if (left == right) {
            System.out.println(characters);
        } else {
            for (int i = left; i <= right; i++) {
                swap(characters, left, i);
                generatePermutations(characters, left+1, right);
                swap(characters, left, i);
            }
        }
    }

    private static void swap(char[] characters, int i, int j) {
        char temp = characters[i];
        characters[i] = characters[j];
        characters[j] = temp;
    }

}
```
In this program, we are using the concept of Backtracking to generate all the permutations. The `generatePermutations` method implements the backtracking. It aims to position every item in every position once.

Note: This code lists unique permutations only when all elements are distinct. If there are repetitions in the input sequence, the output will also have repeated permutations.

