```c
#include <stdio.h>

void cyclicSwap(int* x, int* y, int* z)
{
    if(*x != *y && *x != *z && *y != *z) {
        *x = *x ^ *y;
        *y = *x ^ *y;
        *x = *x ^ *y;

        *y = *y ^ *z;
        *z = *y ^ *z;
        *y = *y ^ *z;
    }
    /* else case isn't required as the numbers are the same, so no swap needed */
}

int main()
{
    int x = 2, y = 4, z = 6;
    printf("Before swap: x = %d, y = %d, z = %d\n", x, y, z);
    cyclicSwap(&x, &y, &z);
    printf("After swap: x = %d, y = %d, z = %d\n", x, y, z);
    return 0;
}
```

In this function, the XOR operator is used to swap the values of the numbers. XOR is a bitwise operator in C that returns 1 if the two compared bits have opposite values, otherwise it returns 0. Therefore, the XOR operation can be used to swap the values of two numbers without needing a temporary variable.

