Here is an example of how you can create a resolver function that will retrieve the most recent publications from a NoSQL DB in a GraphQL server (I am assuming you're using a Node.js environment). This example uses MongoDB and Mongoose. You'll also be using graphql-relay for pagination.

### Step 1. Required Installations:
Before starting, make sure you install these packages via npm into your Node.js project:
```bash
npm install graphql graphql-relay mongoose
```
### Step 2. Create a Publication Mongoose model:
```javascript
var mongoose = require('mongoose');
var Schema = mongoose.Schema;

var PublicationSchema = new Schema({
  title: String,
  author: String,
  date: { type: Date, default: Date.now }
});

module.exports = mongoose.model('Publication', PublicationSchema);
```

### Step 3. Create Resolver & Schema:
In the resolver, you are retrieving the publications and sorting them based on some attribute. Account for pagination and potential performance issues by using the `connectionFromPromisedArray` function of 'graphql-relay'. Also, include error handling with a try and catch block.

The GraphQL schema for the publications (named PublicationSchema) would look something like this:

```javascript
const graphql = require('graphql');
const { GraphQLObjectType, GraphQLString, GraphQLSchema, GraphQLID, GraphQLList } = graphql;
const { connectionFromArray, connectionArgs, connectionDefinitions } = require("graphql-relay");
const Publication = require('./models/Publication');

const PublicationType = new GraphQLObjectType({
  name: ‘Publication’,
  fields: () => ({
    id: { type: GraphQLID},
    title: { type: GraphQLString },
    author: { type: GraphQLString },
    date: { type: GraphQLString },
  }),
});

const { connectionType: PublicationConnection } = connectionDefinitions({
  nodeType: PublicationType,
});

const Query = new GraphQLObjectType({
  name: 'Query',
  fields: {
    publications: {
      type: PublicationConnection,
      args: connectionArgs,
      resolve: async (_, args) => {
        try {
          let results = await Publication.find().sort({ date: -1 });
          return connectionFromArray(results, args);
        } catch (error) {
          console.log('Error', error);
          throw new Error('Error while fetching publications.'); // You can throw GraphQL errors here
        }
      },
    },
  },
});

module.exports = new GraphQLSchema({ query: Query });
```

With this setup, a sample query may look like this:
```graphql
query {
  publications(first: 10){
    edges {
      node {
        title
        author
        date
      }
      cursor
    }
    pageInfo {
      hasNextPage
      endCursor
    }
  }
}
```
Change 'first: 10' to the desired number of results per page. Pagination is handled via `edges` and `pageInfo`.

NOTE: This is a basic example, and can be extended, optimized and adjusted based on the project's requirements such as caching technique, batch loading etc.

