To create a React application for searching and displaying books, we would have to break down the application into following components:

1. Search Bar Component (SearchBar)
2. Book List Component (BookList)
3. Book Item Component (BookItem)

Here is the simplest way to create these components (I will only discuss the components and logic here, not the CSS styling). Note, we assume that we have a Books API (could be Google Books API) that receives a keyword for a book and returns an array of book objects.

**#1. SearchBar Component:**
This is a stateful component that renders an input field and handles its onChange event. 

```javascript
import React from 'react';

class SearchBar extends React.Component {

    state = { term: '' };

    onInputChange = event => {
        this.setState({ term: event.target.value });
    };

    onFormSubmit = event => {
        event.preventDefault();

        // pass to parent App
        this.props.onFormSubmit(this.state.term);
    };

    render() {
        return (
            <div>
                <form onSubmit={this.onFormSubmit}>
                    <input
                        type="text"
                        value={this.state.term}
                        onChange={this.onInputChange}
                    />
                    <button type="submit">Search</button>
                </form>

            </div>
        )
    }
}
```

**#2. BookItem Component:**
This is a stateless component that receives a book object as a prop and displays its data.

```javascript
import React from 'react';

const BookItem = ({ book }) => {
    return (
        <div>
            <h2>{book.title}</h2>
            <h3>{book.author}</h3>
            <a href={book.link}>Read More</a>
        </div>
    )
}

export default BookItem;
```

**#3. BookList Component:**
This is also a stateless component that receives an array of books as a prop. It maps through the array and renders a list of BookItem components.

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import BookItem from './BookItem';

const BookList = ({ books }) => {
    const renderedList = books.map(book => 
        <BookItem book={book} key={book.id} />
    );

    return <div>{renderedList}</div>;
}

export default BookList;
```

**#4. The App component:**

Here is where you define your state and implement your call to the API. 

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import SearchBar from './SearchBar';
import BookList from './BookList';
import api from '../apis/booksApi';//Assuming we have booksApi.js file configured to call our Books API 

class App extends React.Component{
    state = { books: [] };

    onTermSubmit = async term => {
        const response = await api.get('/search', {
            params: { query: term }
        });

        this.setState({ books: response.data });
    };

    render() {
        return(
            <div>
             <SearchBar onFormSubmit={this.onTermSubmit} />
             <BookList books={this.state.books} />
            </div>
        );
    }
}

export default App;

```

