As you've already mentioned, the `HttpClient.Timeout` property sets the timeout for the entire response, not just the connection. Currently, there's no built-in mechanism in .net framework to set a connection-specific timeout.

However, to get around this, you might try a `CancellationToken` which you can cancel after a certain time period.

Here's an example:

```csharp
int timeout = 30000;
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
HttpClient client = new HttpClient();

try {
    var response = await client.GetAsync("http://example.com", cts.Token);
    // Process response here...
}
catch (OperationCanceledException)
{
    if(cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // A timeout occurred.
    }
} 
```

The `CancellationTokenSource` creates a token that becomes cancelled after `timeout` milliseconds, and `GetAsync` throws an `OperationCanceledException` when it observes this cancellation.

Please note that this approach will still not be able to differentiate a connection timeout from a read timeout. It just gives the ability to control how long you wait for the request to complete.

About your last concern, HttpClient maintains a pool of connections, which means that once a connection is established to a particular server, it can be reused for multiple requests, which minimizes the overhead of establishing a new connection every time a request is sent. So, it doesn’t necessarily mean a new connection is established every time you make a new request, even though creating a HttpClient for each request is an anti-pattern.

