In Perl, the regular expression pattern matching operations are performed primarily using the m//, s/// and qr// operators. These accept the following search modes:

1. Global search (g): This option allows the regular expression to match as many times as possible in the given string.
2. Case-insensitive search (i): This option allows the regex search to be case-insensitive.

Here’s how to use them:

1. Matching a pattern:

```perl
if ($string =~ m/pattern/)
```
E.g., If you want to see if the word 'Cute' (case-insensitive) is in `$line`:

```perl
if ($line =~ /Cute/i) {
  print "Found 'Cute' in line: $line \n";
}
```

2. Global matching:

```perl
while ($string =~ m/pattern/g) 
```
This will match all the occurrences of 'pattern' in the string. 

```perl
while ($line =~ /Cute/gi) {
  print "Found another 'Cute' in line: $line\n";
}
```

This example matches all 'Cute' occurrences, case-insensitive. 

3. Escaping special characters

Backslashes `\` allow for escaping special characters in Perl regex.

Example: To match $20, we'll use `\` to escape `$`
```perl
if ($line =~ /\$20/)  { 
    print "Line contains $20\n"; 
}
```

4. Using substitute operator s///

The 's' operator replaces all instances of a pattern within a string.

```perl
$string =~ s/pattern/replacement/gi;  

#Example:
$line =~ s/Cute/Adorable/gi; #Replace cute with Adorable, global and case-insensitive
```

5. `qr//` operator

This operator allows for the creation of regex objects which can be used in pattern match and substitution operations.

```perl
$pattern = qr/Cute/i;
if($line =~ $pattern) {
    print "'Cute' found in line \n";
}
```

6. Boundaries

Boundaries such as `^` (start of the string), `$` (end of the string), and `\b` (word boundary) can be used to refine the match:

```perl
if ($line =~ /^Cute/i) { # Match 'Cute' at start of line
  print "Line starts with 'Cute'\n";
}

if ($line =~ /Cute$/i) { # Match 'Cute' at end of line
  print "Line ends with 'Cute'\n";
}

if ($line =~ /\bCute\b/i) { # Match 'Cute' as whole word
  print "'Cute' found as a whole word\n";
}
```

