The Steps to Design a Thread-Safe Singleton Design Pattern in Java

1. Private Constructor:
Singleton Pattern demands a private constructor to prevent direct instantiation by other classes.

```Java
private singletonClass() {};
```

2. Singleton Instance Creation:
For making Singleton pattern thread-safe we can use a technique called lazy initialization, where the Singleton object is initialized when required. But simply applying lazy initialization, the object would not be thread-safe, there needs to be synchronization among threads to make instance creation thread-safe.

```Java
private static volatile singletonClass instance;

public static singletonClass getInstance() {
    if(instance == null){
        synchronized(singletonClass.class) { 
            if(instance == null) {
                instance = new singletonClass();
            }
        }
    }
    return instance;
}
```

This “double-check locking” ensures that only a single thread can initialize an instance and thus ensuring that instance will always be a singleton and also thread-safe.

Java Reflection API and the Breaking of Singleton:

Java Reflection API provides various methods to reflection classes, interfaces, enum and their methods. It allows you to create an object of a class at runtime and call its methods.

```Java
Constructor[] constructors = singletonClass.class.getDeclaredConstructors();
for (Constructor constructor : constructors) {
    constructor.setAccessible(true);  
    singletonClass secondInstance = singletonClass.class.cast(constructor.newInstance());  
}
```

This code will create a new instance of the class, thus breaking the Singleton. Let's discuss how to prevent this from happening:

Use ENUM to create a Singleton. Enum guarantees that any enum value is instantiated only once in a Java program. Since the Java Enum values are globally accessible, they can be used for singletons. Its only drawback is that it is not flexible i.e it does not allow lazy initialization.

```Java
public enum EasySingleton{
    INSTANCE;
}
```

Serialization and Singleton:
Serialization is used to convert an object of byte stream and save in a file or send over a network. If the class should be able to support serialization, it should implement java.io.Serializable interface.

If we deserialize a serialized singleton class, it will create a new instance, thus breaking the singleton pattern.

```Java
ObjectOutput out = new ObjectOutputStream(new FileOutputStream("file.text"));
out.writeObject(instance);
out.close();

ObjectInput in = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream("file.text"));
singletonClass instanceTwo = (singletonClass) in.readObject();
in.close();
```

Here, instanceTwo is a new instance, thus the Singleton breaks. 

To overcome this scenario, we need to include `readResolve()` method in our singletonClass. `readResolve` is used for replacing the object read from the stream. Here, we will return the same instance that makes our class singleton.

```Java
protected Object readResolve() {
    return getInstance();
}
```

After this, the new instance will not be created at the time of deserialization, thus maintaining the Singleton property of the Singleton class.

