If you are using elements state to render the nodes and edges in your flow, you should place fitView() inside a useEffect call, that will execute as a callback after the rendering has finished. Here's an example of how to do it:

1. You have to get the `reactFlowInstance` first from the `onLoad` event of your `ReactFlow` component.
2. Store the `reactFlowInstance` in your local state.
3. Call `fitView` function from the stored `reactFlowInstance`.

Here is how you could set this up:

```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import ReactFlow, { Controls } from 'react-flow-renderer';

function FlowComponent() {
  const [reactFlowInstance, setReactFlowInstance] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (reactFlowInstance) {
      reactFlowInstance.fitView();
    }
  }, [reactFlowInstance]);

  return (
    <div>
      <ReactFlow elements={elements} onLoad={setReactFlowInstance}>
        <Controls />
      </ReactFlow>
      <button onClick={() => changeNodesLayout()}>
        Change layout
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default FlowComponent;
```

In above example after every update of reactFlowInstance it will re-center the viewport. The onLoad prop function is triggered after the first render of the ReactFlow component with the instance of the component as a parameter.

Please note that the fitView function will be called every time the ReactFlow instance changes. If you want to manually trigger the fitView, you should implement some kind of flag or mechanism to do it. For instance, useEffect could be dependent on a specific state (like an array of nodes, edges) that changes when your button is clicked. Then, when that state updates, fitView can be called:

