You can achieve a wiggle animation in Jetpack Compose by using the `animatedFloat` function, which is similar to `animateIntAsState`. The `animatedFloat` function allows you to create smooth animations between two or more floating-point values.

For example, suppose you want to animate a single Composable object (for instance, a rectangle) smoothly along the x-axis when a boolean value changes.

```kotlin
@Composable
fun AnimatedRectangle() {
    // Create a MutableState variable that will control the animation
    var isAnimated by remember { mutableStateOf(false) }

    // Use the animatedFloat function to animate the position of the rectangle
    val xPosition = animateFloatAsState(if (isAnimated) 200f else 0f)

    Column(
        modifier = Modifier.fillMaxSize(),
        horizontalAlignment = Alignment.CenterHorizontally,
        verticalArrangement = Arrangement.Center
    ) {
        // Create a rectangle that will move along the x-axis
        Box(
            modifier = Modifier.padding(top = xPosition.value.dp, end = xPosition.value.dp)
                .size(50.dp)
                .background(Color.Blue)
        )

        Spacer(modifier = Modifier.height(30.dp))

        // Create a button that will start the animation when clicked
        Button(
            onClick = { isAnimated = !isAnimated },
            modifier = Modifier.padding(16.dp)
        ) {
            Text("Animate")
        }
    }
}
```
The `xPosition` will act as offset for your wiggle animation. Theoretically, you can have any number of `MutableState` variables controlling different aspects of your animation (e.g. rotation, alpha, position, etc.). Depending on how complex is the animation, you might want to abstract it into its own separate function and/or re-usable Composable.

