Below is a Perl equivalent for your Java code. It calculates the sequence of 4th order Fibonacci-like sequence and can find the position of a certain value in that sequence.

It's important to notice that Perl stores array's index from 0, which is different from other languages starts from 1 for the index. So you need to change your indices accordingly in Perl.

Here is the equivalent Perl code:

```perl
use strict;
use warnings;

sub fib4 {
    my $n = shift;
    return 0 if $n<=2 && $n>=0;
    return 2 if $n==3;

    my @mem = (0, 0, 2, 0);
    for my $i (4..$n) {
        $mem[$i] = $mem[$i - 1] + $mem[$i - 2] + $mem[$i - 3] + $mem[$i - 4];
    }
    return $mem[$n];
}

sub fib4N {
    my $N = shift;

    my @sequence = ();
    for my $i (0..$N-1){
        push @sequence, fib4($i);
    }
    return @sequence;
}

sub fib4Find {
    my $target = shift;

    my $i = 0;
    while(1) {
        my $val = fib4($i);
        return $i if $val == $target;
        return -1 if $val > $target;
        $i++;
    }
}

# Test the functions:
print fib4(7), "\n";    # Prints `10`

my @sequence = fib4N(10);
print "@sequence\n";   # Prints `0 0 2 0 2 4 6 10 18 32 58`

print fib4Find(18), "\n";  # Prints `8`
print fib4Find(100), "\n"; # Prints `-1` as 100 is not part of the sequence
```

