The DocumentBuilder class in Java is not thread-safe. You should not reuse instances across threads. 

You can verify this in the JavaDoc for Java 7 which states: "A DocumentBuilder is NOT thread-safe. In other words, it is recommended to have at least one DocumentBuilder instance per thread. If a DocumentBuilder is used from more than one thread then it may lead to unexpected results."

Instead of creating a new DocumentBuilder for each thread, it may be more efficient to use a ThreadLocal variable to store the DocumentBuilder. This creates one instance per thread that can be reused throughout the life cycle of the thread. 

Please note that although DocumentBuilderFactory is thread-safe, the DocumentBuilder instances it creates are not. 

So use the following pattern:

```java
private static final ThreadLocal<DocumentBuilder> builderLocal = new ThreadLocal<DocumentBuilder>() {
    @Override
    protected DocumentBuilder initialValue() {
        try {
            return DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance().newDocumentBuilder();
        } catch (ParserConfigurationException e) {
            throw new ExceptionInInitializerError(e);
        }
    }
};
```

Then each thread should call `builderLocal.get().parse(input)`. 

Also, don't forget to set the features/security settings on the factory before you create your DocumentBuilder if your xml content comes from an untrusted source. That way, you can prevent various types of XML attacks.

