.NET manages the memory of objects in its runtime and there is no direct mapping between the object and its memory usage due to automatic memory management, garbage collection, and so on. Therefore, finding out the memory usage of a particular .NET class instance can be a tricky and not totally accurate business. Hence, it's very hard to obtain an exact size of class in managed code.

If you use `GC.GetTotalMemory(true)`, this can give you a rough idea of how much memory your process is using before and after an object allocation. This method will return the approximate number of bytes currently allocated in managed memory. Here's an example of how you might use it:

