For a more detailed and technical approach, you could use the .NET Profiling API to accurately track memory usage, but this would likely be too complex for most applications.

It's worth noting that in general, focusing on the memory usage of individual objects in a .NET application can be a misuse of effort. The .NET runtime and garbage collector are designed to efficiently handle memory management at a much higher level, and unless you're dealing with a very specific performance problem, it is likely more productive to focus on higher-level optimizations.

