You can use dynamic programming to solve the problem efficiently using the iterative bottom-up approach. And for negative inputs, you can simply add the Fib4 series from 0 up to the absolute value of the input.

Here is the code in C++:

```C++
#include<vector>
#include<algorithm>
using namespace std;

int fib4(int n){
    if(n==0)
        return 0;
    n = abs(n); // Make sure n is not negative
    vector<int> dp(max(4,n+1)); // Declare the array with size = max(4, n+1)
    dp[0]=0, dp[1]=0, dp[2]=2, dp[3]=0;  // Initial values

    for(int i=4;i<=n;i++)
        dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2] + dp[i-3] + dp[i-4]; // Bottom-up computation

    int sum = 0;
    for(int i=0;i<=n;i++)
        sum += dp[i]; // Adding the Fib4 series from 0 to n

    return sum - dp[n]*(n<0 ? -1 : 1); // Subtract dp[n] if n is negative

}
```

The time complexity of this function is O(n) and its space complexity is also O(n) because of the dynamic programming array. The function works for all integer inputs, both positive and negative. It computes the sequence iteratively, thus avoiding the overhead of recursion, and it maintains only a single array for the computed values, leading to a memory-efficient solution. Moreover, the function is robust against negative inputs. The sequence is defined to be the same for negative inputs, except the sign.

