Here is a complete Java code:

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Solution {
    public int doAlgebra(List<String> operator, List<Integer> operand) {
        Stack<String> opStack = new Stack<String>();
        Stack<Integer> numStack = new Stack<Integer>();

        // Loop over each character
        for (int i = 0; i < operand.size(); i++) {

            // If the current character is a number, push it into numStack
            numStack.push(operand.get(i));

            // If the current character is an operator
            if (i < operator.size()) {
                while (!opStack.isEmpty() && getPrecedence(operator.get(i)) <= getPrecedence(opStack.peek())) {
                    numStack.push(calculate(opStack.pop(), numStack.pop(), numStack.pop()));
                }
                opStack.push(operator.get(i));
            }
        }

        // Finally, do the remaining operations
        while (!opStack.isEmpty()) {
            numStack.push(calculate(opStack.pop(), numStack.pop(), numStack.pop()));
        }

        return numStack.pop();
    }

    private int getPrecedence(String op) {
        switch (op) {
        case "+":
        case "-":
            return 1;
        case "*":
        case "/":
            return 2;
        case "**":
            return 3;
        default:
            return -1;
        }
    }

    private int calculate(String op, int num2, int num1) {
        switch (op) {
        case "+":
            return num1 + num2;
        case "-":
            return num1 - num2;
        case "*":
            return num1 * num2;
        case "/":
            return num1 / num2;
        case "**":
            return (int) Math.pow(num1, num2);
        default:
            return 0;
        }
    }
}
```

In this function, we scan each operator and operand from left to right. If the current operator has higher precedence than the operator on the top of the stack, we push it onto the stack. If it has lower or equal precedence, we pop operators and operands from their stacks, calculate the result and push the result onto the numStack until we find an operator on the top of the stack that has lower precedence. Then, we finally push the current operator onto the opStack.

Note: Java does not support operator '**' for exponentiation, we use Math.pow() here instead. Therefore, you might want to adjust the problem to match Java's valid syntax.

