1. The server currently only handles POST requests, while the prompt indicates that it needs to handle PATCH requests. This results in the server sending back a 'Invalid request method' response when a PATCH request is sent to the server.
2. The use of ES6 template literals allows for injection attack since the body is inserted directly into the page HTML.
3. The content-type headers for both the PATCH request and error message do not enforce a specific charset, which can lead to unexpected behavior when dealing with non-ASCII characters.
4. There is no error handling in case something goes wrong while processing the request.

Solution:
To resolve these issues, the server could be rewritten as follows:

```javascript
const http = require('http');

http.createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.method === 'PATCH') {
    let body = '';

    req.on('data', chunk => {
      body += chunk;
    });

    req.on('error', (error) => {
      console.error('An error occurred:', error);
      res.writeHead(500, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain; charset=utf-8' });
      res.end('An error occurred while processing your request.');
    });

    req.on('end', () => {
      res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/html; charset=utf-8' });
      res.end(`
        <!DOCTYPE html>
        <html>
          <body>
            <script>
              document.body.textContent = '${encodeURIComponent(body)}';
            </script>
          </body>
        </html>
      `);
    });
  } else {
    res.writeHead(405, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain; charset=utf-8' });
    res.end('Invalid request method');
  }
}).listen(3000, () => console.log('Server listening on port 3000.'));
```

Changes:
1. The server now checks if the request method is PATCH, instead of POST.
2. The server now uses the encodeURIComponent function to prevent injection attacks.
3. The server now includes the charset=utf-8 property in the content-type headers for both the PATCH request and error message.
4. The server now includes error handling using the on('error') event.
5. The server now responds with a 405 status code if the request method is not PATCH, rather than a 200 status code. 405 is the HTTP status code for "Method Not Allowed".
6. The way body is rendered in html is changed from innerHTML to textContent to prevent any HTML or JavaScript elements contained in body from being executed.

