To count unique good triplets, you could create a list, or more efficiently, a set (since it doesn't allow duplicate elements), storing the good triplets as you find them. Then return both the total count and the length of your set.

Here's a corrected version of your function:

```python
def countGoodTriplets(arr, a, b, c):
    count = 0
    unique_triplets = set()
    for i in range(len(arr)):
        for j in range(i+1, len(arr)):
            for k in range(j+1, len(arr)):
                if abs(arr[i] - arr[j]) <= a and abs(arr[j] - arr[k]) <= b and abs(arr[i] - arr[k]) <= c:
                    # Use frozenset to allow storing of tuples with mutable elements in a set
                    unique_triplets.add(frozenset([arr[i], arr[j], arr[k]]))
                    count += 1
    return count, len(unique_triplets)
```

This version stores each good triplet as a frozenset in the `unique_triplets` set. Frozensets are used instead of regular sets because they are hashable, and thus can be stored in a set. Since a set doesn't allow duplicate elements, it has only the unique good triplets at the end. The count of unique good triplets is then returned as the length of this set.

A frozenset is used instead of a tuple because the order of the elements doesn't matter — the triplet `(arr[i], arr[j], arr[k])` should be considered the same as `(arr[j], arr[i], arr[k])` and `(arr[k], arr[j], arr[i])`.

