Embedding raw binary data directly into XML is not permitted due to the fact that not all binary byte sequences constitute valid characters sequences in the encoding that the XML document is using, and even if they did, they might not be permissible in the context where they appear in the XML.

A popular way to embed binary data in XML is to first encode the binary data as Base64. Base64 encoding is a method for converting binary data into an ASCII string format that can be safely inserted into an XML document.

The Apache Commons Codec library that you mentioned provides a convenient way in Java to do Base64 encoding and decoding. Here is an example of how you can use it:

```
import org.apache.commons.codec.binary.Base64;

// Your binary data
byte[] binaryData = ...;

// Encode the binary data as Base64
byte[] encodedData = Base64.encodeBase64(binaryData);

// Now you can convert the encoded data into a string
String encodedString = new String(encodedData);

// And embed it into your XML
String xml = "<myElement>" + encodedString + "</myElement>";
```

When you receive the XML message at the other end, you can extract the Base64 string, decode it back into its original binary form, like:

```
import org.apache.commons.codec.binary.Base64;

// Extract the Base64 string from the XML
String encodedString = ...;

// Convert the string back into bytes
byte[] encodedData = encodedString.getBytes();

// And decode it back into binary
byte[] binaryData = Base64.decodeBase64(encodedData);
```

This way, you can safely embed and extract binary data in XML messages in a way that any SAX parser will be able to handle. One thing to remember is that Base64 increases the size of the data by about 33%, so you may want to take that into consideration if you are dealing with large amounts of data.

