This task could be solved using a combination of stack data structure and a heap (priority queue). Below is a detailed explanation of the python solution:

```python
from heapq import *
class Solution:
    def avoidFlood(self, rains: List[int]) -> List[int]:
        cnt, emptyDays, map = {}, [], {}
        res = [-1]*len(rains)
        for i, lake in enumerate(rains):
            if not lake: 
                emptyDays.append(i)
                continue
            if lake not in map: 
                map[lake] = []
            map[lake].append(i)
        for i, lake in enumerate(rains):
            if lake: 
                if lake in cnt and cnt[lake] >= len(map[lake]): 
                    return []
                cnt[lake] = cnt.get(lake, -1) + 1;
                if cnt[lake] < len(map[lake]): 
                    heappush(emptyDays, (-map[lake][cnt[lake]], i))
            elif emptyDays:
                _, idx = heappop(emptyDays)
                res[idx] = rains[i]

        while emptyDays: 
            _, idx = heappop(emptyDays)
            res[idx] = 1;
        return res
```
In the python solution above, an empty dictionary `map` is declared to store filled lakes' indexes, `cnt` records the times of clearing water from a lake, and `emptyDays` is a heap to store the dates without rain in descending order. 

1. If it is a rainy day, check if the lake will overflow, which is indicated by `cnt[lake] >= len(map[lake])`. If it does, return an empty list. If it does not, push the next rainfall day into `emptyDays` heap.
2. If it is an empty day, pop and clean the lake which will overflow first (has the smallest index in the rainy days).
3. On each empty day, clean a lake. If there is no overflowing lake, clean the lake #1.

The time complexity is `O(n log n)` because of heap operations, and the space complexity is `O(n)`.

Here is the Java implementation of the Observer design pattern using an Abstract class and interfaces:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// Observable interface
interface Observable {
    void addObserver(Observer observer);
    void removeObserver(Observer observer);
    void notifyObservers();
}

// Observer interface
interface Observer {
    void update();
}

// Observable implementation
class Subject implements Observable {
    private List<Observer> observers = new ArrayList<>();
    // You can also add a state variable if necessary and notify observers when it changes

    @Override
    public void addObserver(Observer observer) {
        observers.add(observer);
    }

    @Override
    public void removeObserver(Observer observer) {
        observers.remove(observer);
    }

    @Override
    public void notifyObservers() {
        for (Observer observer : observers) {
            observer.update();
        }
    }

    public void changeState() {
        // implement your own logic of changing state
        notifyObservers();
    }
}

// Observer implementation
class ConcreteObserver implements Observer {
    private Subject subject;

    ConcreteObserver(Subject subject) {
        this.subject = subject;
        subject.addObserver(this);
    }

    @Override
    public void update() {
        // implement your own logic of handling updates
        System.out.println("State changed! Handling the update");
    }
}
```

In this example, Subject is an Observable that notifies all registered Observers when its state changes. When a ConcreteObserver registers itself with the Subject, it then receives updates whenever the Subject changes state. The Object's state is maintained internally and can't be bypassed outside, as any changes in state will immediately notify all the Observers.

Here's a C++ program that computes the longest common subsequence of two strings using dynamic programming. This approach involves creating a matrix `L` where `L[i][j]` stores the length of LCS of the substrings `string1[0..i-1]` and `string2[0..j-1]`.

```cpp
#include<bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

std::string longestCommonSubsequence(const std::string& string1, const std::string& string2) {
    int m = string1.size();
    int n = string2.size();

    int L[m + 1][n + 1];

    // Build L[m+1][n+1] in bottom up fashion
    for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {
            if (i == 0 || j == 0)
                L[i][j] = 0;
            else if (string1[i - 1] == string2[j - 1])
                L[i][j] = L[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
            else
                L[i][j] = max(L[i - 1][j], L[i][j - 1]);
        }
    }

    // Following code is used to print LCS
    int index = L[m][n];
    char lcs[index + 1];
    lcs[index] = '\0'; // Set the terminating character

    // Start from the right-most-bottom-most corner and
    // one by one store characters in lcs[]
    int i = m, j = n;
    while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
        // If current character in string1 and string2 are same, then
        // current character is part of LCS
        if (string1[i - 1] == string2[j - 1]) {
            lcs[index - 1] = string1[i - 1]; // Put current character in result
            i--;
            j--;
            index--;
        }

        // If not same, then find the larger of two and go in the direction of larger value
        else if (L[i - 1][j] > L[i][j - 1])
            i--;
        else
            j--;
    }

    // Convert char array to string and return
    return std::string(lcs);
}

int main() {
    string string1, string2;
    cin>>string1>>string2;

    cout << "Longest Common Subsequence is: " << longestCommonSubsequence(string1, string2) << "\n";

    return 0;
}
```
This program takes two inputs, `string1` and `string2`, and outputs the longest common subsequence. If there is no common subsequence, it outputs an empty string.

Note: When you are solving a problem related to string processing, `cin` and `cout` could lead to exceed the time limit. You should use `scanf` and `printf` for input and output, respectively.

