In C++, the STL (Standard Template Library) offers utilities like `min_element` and `max_element` to swiftly find smallest and largest elements in an array or vector without any explicit searching or sorting.

Here's how you'd tackle the problem leveraging STL and using raw pointers for linear transformation.

```cpp
#include<vector>
#include<algorithm>
using namespace std;

vector<float> rescale_to_unit(vector<float>& numbers) {
    // find min and max numbers in vector
    auto min_num = *min_element(numbers.begin(), numbers.end());
    auto max_num = *max_element(numbers.begin(), numbers.end());

    // compute range
    auto range =  max_num - min_num;

    // if range is 0 then all values are the same. Return 0 for all of them
    if (range == 0) {
        fill(numbers.begin(), numbers.end(), 0);
        return numbers;
    }

    // apply linear transformations
    for (auto& num : numbers) {
        num = (num - min_num) / range; // linear transformation
    }
    return numbers;
}
```

In this code, `min_element` and `max_element` help in finding the smallest and largest elements of `numbers` respectively. The 'range' is then used to scale all numbers to the [0, 1] interval by subtracting `min_num` from each number in the list and then dividing it by the range. This does just what was asked: sets the smallest number to 0 and the largest to 1. If all numbers are the same (i.e., range is 0), they all become 0. 

All operation done in the function are O(n) - thus making the whole function as efficient and optimized as possible.

