The `@JsonAlias` annotation from Jackson won't work with `@ModelAttribute` annotation as they are related to different technologies and aren't meant to work together. 

The `@ModelAttribute` annotation in Spring binds request parameters to an object on a one-to-one basis based on the names of the parameters and the names of the fields in the object. It's not aware of the `@JsonAlias` annotation.

The `@JsonAlias` annotation from Jackson is used during deserialization of JSON, not during binding of HTTP request parameters to an object.

If you need your HTTP request parameters in snake case to be converted to camel case for your object, you have  two possibilities:

1. Use snake case in your java class:

```java
@Data
public class RequestPojo {
    private String fooSnake;
}
```

and `fooSnake` will be automatically match with `FOO_SNAKE` or `foo_Snake` or `foosnake` during HTTP request because Spring is smart enough to ignore case and underscore differences.

2. Write a custom data binder to manually convert the request parameters from snake case to camel case, such as:

```java
@InitBinder
public void initBinder(WebDataBinder binder) {
    binder.registerCustomEditor(String.class, "fooCamelAlias", new PropertyEditorSupport() {
        @Override
        public void setAsText(String text) {
            setValue(convertSnakeCaseToCamelCase(text));
        }
    });
}

private static String convertSnakeCaseToCamelCase(String input) {
    if (input == null) return null;     
    String[] parts = input.split("_");
    StringBuilder camelCaseString = new StringBuilder(parts[0].toLowerCase());
    for (int i = 1; i < parts.length; i++) {
        camelCaseString.append(parts[i].substring(0, 1).toUpperCase());
        camelCaseString.append(parts[i].substring(1).toLowerCase());
    }
    return camelCaseString.toString();
}
```

Depending on your use case, using the first option (adjust the naming in your java class) would be the simplest and best approach. If it's not possible due to some constraints then you can use the second approach.

