Here is an improved Java solution which uses priority queue to find out the maximum water well and uses bucket to fetch water from it each time till it's empty then goes to the next maximum water well.

In the code below, a `Pair` is used to store both the index (`i`, `j`) and water depth (`depth`) of each well. The PriorityQueue is constructed to order wells based on their water depth in descending order:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {

    class Pair implements Comparable<Pair> {
        int i, j, depth;

        Pair(int i, int j, int depth) {
            this.i = i;
            this.j = j;
            this.depth = depth;
        }

        @Override
        public int compareTo(Pair other) {
            return other.depth - this.depth;  
        }
    }

    public Pair<List<int[]>, Integer> optimalFillStrategy(List<List<Integer>> grid, int capacity) {
        PriorityQueue<Pair> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();
        for(int i = 0; i < grid.size(); i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j < grid.get(0).size(); j++) {
                if(grid.get(i).get(j) == 1) {
                    maxHeap.add(new Pair(i, j, 1));
                }
            }
        }

        List<int[]> strategy = new ArrayList<>();
        int refills = 0;

        while(!maxHeap.isEmpty()) {
            for(int i = 0; i < capacity && !maxHeap.isEmpty(); i++) {
                Pair maxWell = maxHeap.poll();
                strategy.add(new int[]{maxWell.i, maxWell.j});
            }
            refills++;
        }

        return new Pair<>(strategy, refills);
    }
}
```
The function `optimalFillStrategy` works as follows:
- First, it creates a max heap using depth as the comparison (with largest on top).
- Second, it offers all wells to the max heap.
- Third, it starts a loop until the max heap is empty.
  - It picks up a well from max heap exactly `capacity` times, adds them to the strategy to be returned and refills the bucket.
The process continues until there are no more wells in the max heap.

The function finally returns the optimal strategy and the minimum number of refills. The time complexity of this solution is O(m*n*log(m*n)), where m and n are the dimensions of the grid.

