The Delayed Delivery extension (XEP-0203) allows an entity to communicate the fact that a stanza has been stored and then delivered at a later time, usually because the intended recipient was not available at the time the stanza was originally sent.

While QXmpp supports XEP-0203, checking for the delay element directly is not natively supported as per the official documentation.

Therefore, you can check for a delay element by accessing the xml from the message and parsing it for the delay tag, although this is a kind of workaround.

Here is a way to check for a delay element in the incoming message:

```cpp
QXmppMessage msg;

//Check if the msg has a delay
QString xml = msgToXml(msg);
QDomDocument doc;
doc.setContent(xml);
QDomElement msgElem = doc.documentElement();
QDomElement delayElem = msgElem.firstChildElement("delay");
if (!delayElem.isNull()) {
    // This is an offline message
} else {
    // This is a real-time message
}
```
In the above code snippet, `msgToXml(msg)` should return a QString containing the Xml content of the incoming `QXmppMessage msg`.

This solution assumes that your QXmpp library successfully parses incoming XMPP stanzas and adds `<delay/>` appropriately as described by XEP-0203. 

Do note that this is more of a workaround, and the ideal solution would be if QXmpp adds direct support for checking the Delayed Delivery. However, for the current version (1.3.2) of QXmpp, there doesn't seem to be a direct function call or property to check that.

