A `Set` is a JavaScript object where all the values are unique. Converting the array into a set automatically removes any duplicate values. You can then convert it back into an array (if needed). Below is the code to do that:

```javascript
function removeDuplicates(arr) {
    return [...new Set(arr)];
}
```

Core Algorithmic Concept: Here we're just leveraging inbuilt JavaScript functionality. When the `Set` is created, it automatically removes any duplicate values because, by definition, a set can only contain unique values.

Method 2: Using Array Filter

The `filter()` method creates a new array with all elements that pass the test implemented by the provided function. We can use this method to check if the current element is the first occurrence of the value and if so, include it in the new array.

```javascript
function removeDuplicates(arr) {
    return arr.filter((value, index, self) => self.indexOf(value) === index);
}
```

Core Algorithmic Concept: We are iterating through each element and checking if the index of its first occurrence is the same as the current index. If it is, then we know this is the first time we are encountering this value. If it's not, this value has appeared before and is therefore a duplicate.

Method 3: Using an Object/Hash Map

This concept uses a JavaScript object to track the unique elements that you've encountered. You iterate through the array and for each element, you check if this is already a key in the object. If it is, you know it's a duplicate and you can ignore it. If it's not, you add it to the object and to the result array.

```javascript
function removeDuplicates(arr) {
    let hash = {};
    let result = [];

    for (let value of arr) {
        if (!hash[value]) {
            hash[value] = true;
            result.push(value);
        }
    }

    return result;
}
```

