If you run a background task in your ASP.NET application, you must make sure that your task will get finished. Here are the basics:

1. Register your task with the HostingEnvironment class. This gives ASP.NET a chance to wait for your task completion before it recycles/terminates an idle worker process.

2. Lock the application object while the task is running. This prevents ASP.NET to terminate the application domain when there are no active requests. Just implement a reference counter in your application object. Increment the counter from your main thread before you start a background task, and decrement it from the background task.

3. In your background thread, periodically check HostingEnvironment.ShutdownReason property. If it's not None, that means ASP.NET is shutting down your process for some reason and your task must immediately complete.

Now, regarding the Java EE world, a background worker thread wouldn't always work due to the specification that doesn't permit starting them directly from your web application. Therefore, it's usually recommended to start the Timer or the Thread from a ServletContextListener.

You can however use Java EE's managed scheduled tasks facility if you are on a Java EE 6 compatible container. You may be able to use asynchronous methods in a singleton EJB that get fired upon application startup and then are scheduled to run at fixed delays/rates.

Here's a simplified example:

```java
@Startup
@Singleton
public class ConfigBean {

    @Resource
    private TimerService timerService;

    @PostConstruct
    private void init() {
        // schedule the timers on application startup
        timerService.createCalendarTimer(
            new ScheduleExpression().hour("*").minute("*").second("0"),
            new TimerConfig(null, false)
        );
    }

    // this method will automatically be invoked by the container every minute
    @Timeout
    private void execute(Timer timer) {
        // perform some periodic task
    }

}
```
Use try/catch blocks and make sure you handle all exceptions properly in each timer task. If an exception is thrown and not caught, the container will automatically stop future executions.

