Here is the C code implementing the logic to print the nested equilateral triangle up to 'n' levels. The nested levels are distinguished by different characters: 'O' for odd levels and 'X' for even levels. The user is asked to input a number to determine the depth of the triangle.

Note: Since the problem did not specify how much should scale down each subsequent triangle, this implementation assumes that each subsequent triangle is half the size of its parent triangle.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void drawTriangle(int n, int row, int col, char arr[1024][1024], char symbol) {
    if (n <= 0) return;

    int size = 1 << n; // Calculate triangle size by 2^n

    // Draw the triangle
    for (int i=0; i<size; i++) {
        for (int j=0; j<=i; j++) {
            arr[row-i][col-j] = symbol;
            arr[row-i][col+j] = symbol;
        }
    }
    // Draw the middle inner triangle
    drawTriangle(n-1, row-(size/4), col, arr, symbol == 'O' ? 'X' : 'O');
}

int main() {
    int levels;
    char arr[1024][1024] = {0};

    printf("Enter number of levels: ");
    scanf("%d", &levels);

    drawTriangle(levels, (1 << levels) - 1, (1 << levels) - 1, arr, 'O');

    // Print the triangle pattern
    for (int i=0; i<(1<<levels); i++) {
        for (int j=0; j<(1<<levels); j++) {
            if (arr[i][j] == 0) printf(" ");
            else printf("%c", arr[i][j]);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```
Please note that this code does not draw 'true' equilateral triangles, but rather right triangles, due to the limitations of ASCII art. Text characters are taller than they are wide, so a 'true' equilateral triangle isn't possible. 

Also, users need to input the depth less than or equal to 10 (including 10) otherwise it may exceed array size or result in segmentation fault/error for too big arrays.

