In the current code, `margin-left:auto; margin-right:auto;` are being used in an attempt to center the .box in the middle horizontally but that will only work if the .box is a block-level element. On the other hand, `margin-top:auto; margin-bottom:auto;` aren't an effective way to vertically align the content in CSS.

Here's a way to revise the CSS using Flexbox:

```css
.container {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100vh;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
}

.box {
    width: 50%;
    height: 50%;
}
```

Here, `display: flex;` is used to turn .container into a flexible container, which enables the use of Flexbox properties. `justify-content: center;` and `align-items: center;` are used to horizontally and vertically center the .box in the middle of the .container, respectively. 

Note that the width and height of .box are kept the same to keep the box half the size of its container. If you want the .box to take up the whole .container, simply change the width and height to 100%.

This code ensures that the .box is centered both vertically and horizontally in the .container, regardless of the .container's size.

