The initial problem with the CSS script was that the style rules were applied to the entire unordered list (ul) element, which includes both the list markers and the list items (li). Therefore, the color and font size were not being applied solely to the list markers as intended.

To correct this issue, you can use the "::marker" pseudo-element selector, which allows you to style just the list markers. This pseudo-element is also responsive, as it will automatically adjust according to the computed ‘font-size’ of the list item.

Unfortunately, the color property is one of the very few properties that can be applied to ::marker, so we can't directly set the typographic scale (font size) of the markers. But, here's a workaround - we can reduce the list item font size and increase the overall list font size.

Here's the modified script:

```HTML
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<style>
/* Increase overall font size of the list */
ul {
  font-size: 1.5em;
}

/* Apply the color and font size to the list markers only */
ul li::marker {
  color: blue;
}

/* Downscale the list item's font size */
ul li {
  font-size: 0.7em;
}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h2>Three Elements List:</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Item 1</li>
  <li>Item 2</li>
  <li>Item 3</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>
```

This script should provide the expected outcome. The color blue is now applied to list markers only, their typographic size is also bigger but the text inside the list items remains at usual size.

