Yes, calling `free()` on the same pointer twice leads to undefined behavior according to the C standard. This is called double freeing. 

The reason is that after you `free(myString)` for the first time, `myString` no longer points to a valid memory block. If you then try to `free(myString)` a second time, the memory management system doesn't know what to do, because `myString` is no longer connected to a block of memory. 

This could lead to a variety of different problems, such as program termination, corruption of data, security risks, or other unpredictable behaviors. 

To prevent this, you should always set your pointers to `NULL` after freeing them:

```c
char * myString = malloc(sizeof(char)*STRING_BUFFER_SIZE);
free(myString);
myString = NULL; // This will prevent double free if checked correctly.
```
And check for `NULL` before `free()`:

```c
if (myString != NULL)
{
    free(myString);
    myString = NULL;
}
``` 

These are best practices to prevent such issues.

