Here's a Python solution which defines a LinkedList class with the required functionalities.

```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value
        self.next = None


class LinkedList:
    def __init__(self):
        self.head = None

    def push(self, new_value):
        new_node = Node(new_value)
        new_node.next = self.head
        self.head = new_node

    def detect_cycle(self):
        slow_pointer = self.head
        fast_pointer = self.head
        while slow_pointer and fast_pointer and fast_pointer.next:
            slow_pointer = slow_pointer.next
            fast_pointer = fast_pointer.next.next
            if slow_pointer == fast_pointer:
                return True
        return False

    def find_middle(self):
        slow_pointer = self.head
        fast_pointer = self.head
        while fast_pointer and fast_pointer.next:
            slow_pointer = slow_pointer.next
            fast_pointer = fast_pointer.next.next
        if fast_pointer:
            return slow_pointer.value, slow_pointer.next.value
        else:
            return slow_pointer.value

    def search(self, value):
        temp = self.head
        while temp:
            if temp.value == value:
                return True
            temp = temp.next
        return False
```

This code defines a basic Node class which will be the building blocks of our linked list. Then we define a LinkedList class which contains methods for adding elements to the list (`push`), detecting a cycle (`detect_cycle`), finding the middle element(s) (`find_middle`), and searching for a given value (`search`).

`detect_cycle` uses the Floyd's cycle-finding algorithm, also known as the "tortoise and the hare" algorithm. The idea is to have two pointers moving at different speeds (one moves two nodes each step, the other moves one node each step). If there's a cycle in the list, these two pointers will eventually meet.

`find_middle` uses a similar approach, but this time when the "hare" pointer reaches the end of the list, the "tortoise" pointer will be at the middle.

`search` simply traverses the list until it finds the required value (or reaches the end of the list).

All of these operations have a time complexity of O(n) where n is the size of the linked list. This is because each operation involves traversing the list once. The space complexity is O(1) as we are not using any extra space that scales with the input size.

