The HtmlAgilityPack doesn't have a built-in way to encode node names, but you can do it manually by iterating through all the nodes in the document. Here's a way you could do it:

```csharp
foreach (var node in doc.DocumentNode.DescendantsAndSelf())
{
    foreach (var attribute in node.Attributes.ToList())
    {
        attribute.Value = HttpUtility.HtmlEncode(attribute.Value);
    }

    if (node.NodeType == HtmlNodeType.Text)
    {
        var text = (HtmlTextNode)node;
        text.Text = HttpUtility.HtmlEncode(text.Text);
    }
}

var allowedTags = new[] { "img", "a", "h1", "h2", "em" };
foreach (var node in doc.DocumentNode.DescendantsAndSelf())
{
    if (!allowedTags.Contains(node.Name) && (node.Name == "a" || node.Name =="img"))
    {
        node.SetAttributeValue("href", null);
        node.SetAttributeValue("src", null);
    }
}
```

The first `foreach` loop encodes all attribute values and text nodes. The second loop removes the href and src attributes from anchor and image tags that aren't in the allowedTags array.

A few things to note:
 - The `DescendantsAndSelf()` method returns all descendants of a node, and the node itself. This is how we're able to iterate through all nodes in the document.
 - There's no need to call `HttpUtility.HtmlEncode` on the node name itself - in an HTML document, node names are always ASCII and don't have special characters, so they don't need to be encoded.
 - This code will remove all href/src attributes from disallowed anchor/image tags, even if the attribute value is a valid http URL. If you want to keep the attribute if its value is a valid http URL, you'll need to add a check for that condition.
 - The list of allowed tags is specified in an array for easy updating - if you want to change the set of allowed tags in the future, just update this array.
 - The `HttpUtility.HtmlEncode` method is in the System.Web namespace. If you're not already using this namespace, you'll need to add a `using System.Web;` directive at the top of your code file.

