Since Java does not have a built-in method that generates uniform random floating numbers without using the Random class, we can take advantage of Math.random(). It generates a floating number between 0 (inclusive) and 1 (exclusive).

However, it may still generate a number that is not within the required range. To overcome this, we should normalize our min and max into the range [0, 1). Then we scale the result back to the required range.

Here is a implementation considering above factors:

```java
public class Main{
  public static void main(String[] args){
    System.out.println(generateRandomFloat(0.3f, 0.7f));
  }

  public static float generateRandomFloat(float min, float max){
    if(min >= max){
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("max must be greater than min");
    }

    float result;
    do{
      result = min + ((float)Math.random() * (max - min));
      // rounding to 3 digits after the decimal point
      result = Math.round(result * 1000.0f) / 1000.0f;
    }while(result < min || result > max);
    return result;
  }
}
```

Now, every time you call generateRandomFloat(0.3f, 0.7f), it should generate a random floating number in the range 0.3 - 0.7 with up to 3 digits after the decimal point.

