1. Instance constructors are not necessarily thread-safe by default. If multiple threads call the constructor at the same time, it may lead to race conditions causing unpredictable behavior.

2. The lock statement prevents other threads from accessing the code block locked on the same object until the lock is released. However, in your code you are locking on `this`, which refers to a specific instance of `MyClass`. Each instance you will create, will be a different object and therefore the lock would not be effective across multiple objects (or threads that instantiate said objects). If you want to synchronize access to the static 'counter' variable, you need to lock on an object which is common to all the instances.

One way to achieve this is to use a static object to lock. This will ensure that the object used for locking is the same for all instances and hence, the lock will make sure that `counter` is incremented in a thread-safe way. Here's how it can be done:

```C#
public class MyClass {

    private static Int32 counter = 0;
    private static readonly object counterLock = new object();

    private Int32 myCount;

    public MyClass() {
        lock(counterLock) {
            counter++;
            myCount = counter;
        }
    }
}
```
The common lock object `counterLock` guards the increment operation on the `counter` ensuring that the operation is atomic and that the `myCount` for each instance of `MyClass` gets a unique value.

