The provided code contains a few mistakes. The major issues are how the for loops are set up and the initialization of the max variable. 

1. The loops currently run from 0 to 4 but arrays in Java are zero-indexed, meaning the valid indices for these arrays run from 0 to the length of the array minus one. This discrepancy may cause "ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException".

2. Initializing the `max` variable at zero may lead to incorrect results if all array elements are negative. Instead, it is better to initialize `max` to `Integer.MIN_VALUE` which is guaranteed to be lower than or equal to all possible integer values. 

3. To check for duplicate maximum values, a counter (`maxCounter`) can be used. This variable is increased every time `max` is updated or the same maximum value is encountered.

Below is the corrected and improved code:

```java
public class MainClass {
    public static void main(String args[]) {
        int [][] arr = new int [][] { {2, 5, 1}, {4, -3, 6}, {8, 4, 7} };
        int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        int maxCount = 0;

        for(int i=0; i<arr.length; i++) {
            for(int j=0; j<arr[i].length; j++) {
                if(max < arr[i][j]) {
                    max = arr[i][j];
                    maxCount = 1; // New maximum found! Reset counter.
                }
                else if(max == arr[i][j]) {
                    maxCount++; // Same maximum found! Increase counter.
                }
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Maximum element is :" + max);
        if(maxCount > 1)
            System.out.println("There are " + maxCount + " duplicates of the maximum value.");
        else
            System.out.println("No duplicate maximum value found.");
    }
}
