function reverseAndRemove(arr, n) {
  return arr.map(subArr => 
    subArr
    .reverse()
    .filter((_, index) => (index + 1) % n !== 0)
  );
}
```

This function uses the `Array.prototype.map()` method to iterate over each sub-array of the input array. For each sub-array, it first reverses the elements using the `Array.prototype.reverse()` method, and then creates a new array that includes only those elements whose 1-based index is not a multiple of the input integer `n`. This is achieved using the `Array.prototype.filter()` method and the modulo operation. 

The `%` operator used in `(index + 1) % n !== 0` calculates the remainder when the 1-based index is divided by `n`. If the remainder is 0, this means that the index is a multiple of `n`, so the element at that index is excluded from the new array. If the remainder is not 0, this means that the index is not a multiple of `n`, so the element at that index is included in the new array. 

