In Java, we can solve this problem using the concept of dynamic programming. Here is the Java code solution to find the longest palindromic subsequence.

```java
class Solution {
    public String longestPalindrome(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        String res = null;

        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n][n];

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                dp[i][j] = s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j) && (j - i < 3 || dp[i + 1][j - 1]);

                if (dp[i][j] && (res == null || j - i + 1 > res.length())) {
                    res = s.substring(i, j + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        return res;
    }
}
```

This algorithm works by checking all substrings and marking them as palindrome if the characters at both ends are equivalent, and the substring does not count more than two characters. The longest palindromic substring is updated when a longer palindromic substring is found. The time complexity is O(n^2) and space complexity is O(n^2).

