Your first approach, by using a transaction, is the correct one. This is because transactions are typically used to ensure data consistency and to handle errors that might arise during the database operation.

In case of an error, you would want to rollback whatever changes have been made to the database which is exactly what transactions are for. They give you the reliability and atomicity required in such scenarios.

The second approach, using Tasks, doesn't take care of any error handling in the event of an exception. It will also not ensure data consistency as different tasks might complete at different times. Further, while Task.WhenAll will wait for all tasks to complete, it won't rollback the other tasks if one fails which could result in inconsistent data.

Another point to consider is that the image upload to the cloud storage is itself not transactional. If the database save fails, you can rollback the transaction, but you cannot "rollback" the upload to the cloud storage.

A better approach would be to:
1. Start a database transaction
2. Save the data to the database
3. Upon successful save, then upload the images to cloud in the same transaction and then commit the transaction.
4. If the upload fails, you rollback the transaction.

Remember to use transaction.Rollback() in a finally block (or use a fluent approach to do the same) to ensure that the rollback happens regardless of where the error occurred.


