In case of sending array of integers or any other complex objects as request body, first important point is to set `Content-Type` header as `application/json` in the request.

You could use `@RequestBody` instead of `@RequestParam`.

Try something like this in your controller:

```java
@PostMapping(value = "/your_endpoint")
public int acceptArray(@RequestBody int[] array) {
    if (array == null || array.length == 0) {
        throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, "You entered an empty array");
    }

    return arraySolver.arraySolver(array);
}
``` 

And the request would be something like this:

```json
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

You should put this value in the body of your request. Note that you don't need to define keys, you only need an array of integers because it's `int[]` type in your controller, and don't forget the Content-Type: application/json note.

However, if you want to handle the case when the incoming JSON is not an array you might want to use the below approach in your code:

```java
   @PostMapping(value = "/your_endpoint")
   public int acceptArray(@RequestBody Object array) {

       if (array instanceof List) {
            List<Integer> list = (List<Integer>) array;
            int[] ints = list.stream().mapToInt(i -> i).toArray();
            return arraySolver.arraySolver(ints);
       } else {
            throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, "Expected an array");
       }
    }
```

This code will convert array of integers received as JSON to `List<Integer>` and then convert it back to `int[]` to reuse your `arraySolver` method. If the received JSON is not an array, it will throw a `ResponseStatusException`.

