Below is a Python solution to solve this problem using a linked list. The class Node represents a node in the linked list and the class LinkedList represents the linked list.

```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, data=None):
        self.data = data
        self.next = None

class LinkedList:
    def __init__(self):
        self.head = None

    def append(self, data):
        if not self.head:
            self.head = Node(data)
        else:
            new_node = Node(data)
            temp = self.head
            while temp.next:
                temp = temp.next
            temp.next = new_node

    def remove_sequential_duplicates(self):
        if not self.head:
            return
        temp = self.head
        data_set = {temp.data.lower()}
        while temp.next:
            if temp.next.data.lower() in data_set:
                temp.next = temp.next.next
            else:
                data_set.add(temp.next.data.lower())
                temp = temp.next

    def print_list(self):
        temp = self.head
        while temp:
            print(temp.data)
            temp = temp.next

def test():
    word_list = ['Hello', 'World', 'World', 'This', 'Is', 'Is', 'Sample', 'Hello', 'Sample']
    linked_list = LinkedList()
    for word in word_list:
        linked_list.append(word)
    linked_list.remove_sequential_duplicates()
    linked_list.print_list()

if __name__ == '__main__':
    test()
```

When you run this program, the output will be:
```
Hello
World
This
Is
Sample
```

Time complexity analysis:

Iterating over a list to create the linked list is O(n) due to having to append each of the n items onto the list. Removing the duplicates by iterating through the linked list is also O(n) because you have to check and possibly remove each of the n items. Therefore, the total time complexity is O(n + n) which simplifies to O(n).

Space complexity analysis:

The space complexity of the solution is also O(n), where n is the number of items in the list. This is because in the worst case scenario, a new linked list node is created for each item in the list, and a new entry is created in the set for each unique item.

