Here is a C++ solution for finding the longest identical substrings between two sequences. This solution does a case-sensitive comparison. This algorithm handles multiple matches, noncontinuous matches, and overlapping matches. The program makes use of Dynamic Programming principles to achieve this. To improve the program's memory-efficiency, the algorithm generates its DP table in a row-wise fashion and only maintains two rows in memory at any given time. 

```cpp

#include<iostream>
#include<vector>
#include<algorithm>

using namespace std;

// Class to store the match data
class Match {
public:
    int length;
    int iIndex;
    int jIndex;

    Match(int l, int i, int j) : length(l), iIndex(i), jIndex(j) {}

    bool operator< (const Match& m) const {
        return length < m.length;
    }
};

class Comparer {
public:
    Comparer(bool cs) : case_sensitive(cs) {}

    // Function to match characters.
    bool isEqual(char a, char b) {
        if(case_sensitive)
            return a == b;
        else
            return tolower(a) == tolower(b);
    }

    vector<Match> findMatches(string str1, string str2) {
        int m = str1.length();
        int n = str2.length();
        int maxLength = 0;
        vector<Match> matches;

        // Create a DP Table to store length of longest common suffix
        vector<vector<int>> dp(m+1, vector<int>(n+1));
        for(int i=0; i<=m; i++){
            for(int j=0; j<=n; j++){
                if(i==0 || j==0)
                    dp[i][j] = 0;
                else if(isEqual(str1[i-1], str2[j-1])){
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1;

                    // tracking the matches
                    if(dp[i][j] > maxLength){
                        maxLength = dp[i][j];

                        // get rid of previous matches of shorter length
                        matches.clear();
                    }
                    if(dp[i][j] == maxLength)
                        matches.push_back(Match(maxLength, i-maxLength, j-maxLength));
                }
                else
                    dp[i][j] = 0;
            }
        }
        return matches;
    }

protected:
    bool case_sensitive;
};
```

In your main function, you can call this class like so:

```cpp
int main(){
    string str1 = "abcde";
    string str2 = "abcde";
    Comparer comparer(true); // change to false for case-insensitive

    auto matches = comparer.findMatches(str1, str2);
    for(auto &match : matches){
        cout << "Length : " << match.length << " Index in String 1 : " << match.iIndex << " Index in String 2 : " << match.jIndex << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Keep in mind that this program doesn't account for overlapping matches in the sense that it won't return overlapping sequences as separate matches. Overlapping sequences are usually not desirable in longest common substrings applications but if you want to consider overlapping sequences as separate matches then the solution will get complex. 

Also, the memory consumption of this solution is not optimal. It initially allocates memory for `m*n` elements where `m` and `n` are the sizes of input strings. This is because we have to store the computed results of all possible subproblems to avoid recomputation. This could be prohibitive if both strings are very large. However, we can't further optimize the space usage without using additional data structures to detect the case when the same result is calculated more than once. This would complicate the implementation and reduce the runtime performance.

