Creating a class in C++, involves following various steps. These steps include defining the class, declaring attributes and methods, creating constructors, and destructors for the class. Let's break the entire process down into steps:

Step 1: Defining a Class

Classes in C++ are user-defined types or data structures declared with the keyword 'class' that has data and functions as its members whose access is governed by the three access specifiers - private, protected or public.

```cpp
class ClassName {
  // attributes and methods go here
};
```

Step 2: Declaring Attributes and Methods

Within a class, you can declare attributes (variables) and methods (functions) that are specific to that class. By default, all members of a class in C++ are private if no access modifier is specified.

```cpp
class MyClass {
  public:    // Access specifier
    int myNum;   // Attribute (int variable)
    string myString; // Attribute (string variable)

    // method (function)
    void printDetails() {
      cout << "Number: " << myNum << ", String: " << myString;
    }
};
```

Step 3: Creating Constructors

A constructor in C++ is a special method that is automatically called when an object of a class is created. Although it has the same name as the class, it does not have a return type.

```cpp
class MyClass {
  public:
    MyClass() {  // Constructor 
      cout << "Object is created\n";
    }
};
```

Step 4: Creating Destructors

A destructor is a special method that is automatically called when an object goes out of scope. It is identified by a tilde (~) followed by the class name.

```cpp
class MyClass {
  public:
    MyClass() {  // Constructor 
      cout << "Object is created\n";
    }
    ~MyClass() {  // Destructor
      cout << "Object is destroyed\n";
    }
};
```
These are the fundamental steps for creating a class object in C++. They form the backbone of object-oriented programming in C++, providing a clear modular structure for organizing code for scalability, readability, and reusability.

