The problem can be solved using a depth-first search (DFS) approach.

Algorithm:

1. Assume the robot initially faces upwards. We maintain directions as up (0,-1), right (1,0), down (0,1), and left (-1,0) initially.
2. Implement a cleanRoom method to initiate the process. Call the DFS method with initial position (0,0) and direction 0 (up).
3. Implement the DFS method which takes in the position and direction as input parameters. Before calling DFS, clean the current cell if it hasn't been cleaned before.
4. Iterate the 4 possible steps for the robot from the current direction, -1 to the left and +1, +2, and +3 to the right. Call DFS for each subsequent direction and backward step.
5. Backtracking is necessary in the case where all cells in the current direction are explored. Return to the previous cell and change direction to the previous direction.
6. If a path is already visited or blocked, return without any operation.

Implementation in Java:

```java
class Solution {
    private final int[][] directions = {{-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}};
    private Set<Pair<Integer, Integer>> cleaned = new HashSet<>();

    public void cleanRoom(Robot robot) {
        backtrack(robot, 0, 0, 0);
    }

    private void backtrack(Robot robot, int row, int col, int d) {
        Pair<Integer, Integer> current = new Pair<>(row, col);
        robot.clean();
        cleaned.add(current);

        for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
            int newD = (d + i) % 4;
            int newRow = row + directions[newD][0];
            int newCol = col + directions[newD][1];

            Pair<Integer, Integer> nextCell = new Pair<>(newRow, newCol);

            if (!cleaned.contains(nextCell) && robot.move()) {
                backtrack(robot, newRow, newCol, newD);
                goToPrevious(robot);
            }

            robot.turnRight();
        }
    }

    private void goToPrevious(Robot robot) {
        robot.turnRight();
        robot.turnRight();
        robot.move();
        robot.turnRight();
        robot.turnRight();
    }
}
```

As per the given APIs, we don’t know the layout of the room and the initial position of the robot. So, we treated the initial position of the robot as (0,0) to start with and maintain the map accordingly. The map is only for bookkeeping purposes and won’t influence the robot’s movement. Note that only relative movements matter.

We use a set called 'cleaned' to record all the cells that have been cleaned already by using a hash set. We encode the status into an integer by encoding the pair of two integers into a single integer using the Pair library.

