Here is a simple implementation of a priority queue with basic CRUD operations:

```JavaScript
class PriorityQueue {
  constructor() {
    this.collection = [];
  }

  print() {
    console.log('Current top five countries (by population) in queue:', this.collection);
  }

  // Add new country to the queue
  create(country, population) {
    if(this.collection.length === 0) {
      this.collection.push({country, population});
    } else {
      let added = false;
      for(let i = 0; i < this.collection.length; i++) {
        if(population > this.collection[i].population) {
          this.collection.splice(i, 0, {country, population});
          added = true;
          break;
        }
      }
      if(!added) {
        this.collection.push({country, population});
      }

      // Only keep top 5
      if(this.collection.length > 5) {
        this.collection.pop();
      }
    }
  }

  // Update country's population
  update(country, population) {
    this.delete(country);
    this.create(country, population);
  }

  // Remove country from queue
  delete(country) {
    this.collection = this.collection.filter(item => item.country !== country);
  }
}

// Sample usage
let queue = new PriorityQueue();
queue.create("China", 1300);  // population in millions
queue.create("India", 1200);
queue.create("USA", 350);
queue.create("Indonesia", 250);
queue.create("Pakistan", 210);
queue.create("Brazil", 210);  // This will replace the least ones since queue only contains top 5
queue.print(); // Top five countries with largest populations

queue.update("USA", 360); // Update population of USA
queue.print();

queue.delete("India"); // Delete country from queue
queue.print();
```

Some assumptions have been made for this problem: 
1. The populations given when updating or creating a country is always correct and we don't have to compare it with an old value.
2. If a country with lower population than the current top five is added, it will not be included in the queue and simply will be discarded.
3. If more than one country has the same population, they will be ordered by the order they come in.

This priority queue is implemented using a sorted array to keep track of the top five countries. It consequently keeps the queue sorted by population at all times. The complexity of delete or create operation is O(n), since JavaScript splice method rearranges all elements. Update operation is calling delete and create so it also has O(n) complexity. Read operation has O(1) complexity.

def unique_odd_prime_sum_elements(x):
    """Given a list of positive integers x, return a sorted list of all 
    _unique_ elements that have an odd sum of their _prime_ digits and do not appear 
    multiple times in the input list.

    Note: Returned list should be sorted in increasing order.

    Prime digits: 2, 3, 5, 7

    For example:
    >>> unique_odd_prime_sum_elements([15, 33, 1422, 1, 15])
    [1, 15]
    >>> unique_odd_prime_sum_elements([152, 323, 1422, 10])
    [323]
    """

    result = []
    unique_numbers_check = {}

    prime_digits = [2, 3, 5, 7]

    for num in x:
        digit_sum = sum([int(digit) for digit in str(num) if int(digit) in prime_digits])

        if digit_sum % 2 == 1:
            if num not in unique_numbers_check:
                unique_numbers_check[num] = 1
            else:
                unique_numbers_check[num] += 1

    for key, value in unique_numbers_check.items():
        if value == 1:
            result.append(key)

    return sorted(result)

# Test cases
print(unique_odd_prime_sum_elements([15, 33, 1422, 1, 15])) # [1, 15]
print(unique_odd_prime_sum_elements([152, 323, 1422, 10])) # [323]
print(unique_odd_prime_sum_elements([993, 345, 222, 210])) # [345]

