This program first generates a frequency map of all the strings in each input array, then finds the intersection of all maps (common elements), and finally computes the sum of their instances (occurrences).

Code:

```Java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[][] arrays = {{"cat", "dog", "bird", "fish"}, {"dog", "cat", "mouse", "bird", "bird"}, {"bird", "cat", "dog", "cow"}};
        Map<String, Integer> commonElementsMap = findCommonElements(arrays);
        System.out.printf("Common elements with their instances: %s%n", commonElementsMap);
    }

    public static Map<String, Integer> findCommonElements(String[][] arrays) {
        List<Map<String, Integer>> frequencyMaps = new ArrayList<>();
        for (String[] array : arrays) {
            Map<String, Integer> frequencyMap = new HashMap<>();
            for (String string : array) {
                frequencyMap.put(string, frequencyMap.getOrDefault(string, 0) + 1);
            }
            frequencyMaps.add(frequencyMap);
        }

        Map<String, Integer> commonElementsMap = new HashMap<>(frequencyMaps.get(0));
        for (int i = 1; i < frequencyMaps.size(); i++) {
            commonElementsMap.keySet().retainAll(frequencyMaps.get(i).keySet());
            for (String key : commonElementsMap.keySet()) {
                commonElementsMap.put(key, Math.min(commonElementsMap.get(key), frequencyMaps.get(i).get(key)));
            }
        }
        return commonElementsMap;
    }
}
```

This program will output: Common elements with their instances: {bird=1, cat=1, dog=1}

Analysis:

Time Complexity: The time complexity of this program is O(n), where n is the total number of strings across all input arrays. This is because every string is processed once (for building frequency map).

Space Complexity: The space complexity of this program is also O(n), where n is the total number of unique strings across all input arrays. This is because these strings need to be stored in the frequency map for comparison.

Notice: This would also count the unique instances of each common strings and find the minimum occurrences across all arrays. If the requirement is just to find the unique common strings, we could simplify the code by using HashSet instead of HashMap.

