On the other hand, a HashTable is a data structure which implements an associative array abstract data type, a structure that can map keys to values. It uses a hash function to compute an index into an array in which an element will be inserted or searched. The main advantage of the HashTable is its speed. HashTable can insert, delete and search elements in O(1) average time complexity.

BSTs are good for "ordered data" and "range queries". Also, they have an additional advantage that we can order keys in-order, pre-order, and post-order. While HashTables are generally used when you need to keep track of a set of elements, but have no interest in their order of arrangement.

