Here's a simple example in Rust that uses both a Fibonacci number generator and vector inversion. The output is a vector. If the size of the vector exceeds the MAX_LIMIT, an error is returned. The Fibonacci number generator uses memoization to optimize for large data sets.

```rust
use std::error::Error;

fn fibonacci(n: usize, memo: &mut Vec<i32>) -> i32 {
    if n <= 1 {
        n as i32
    } else {
        if memo[n] == 0 {
            memo[n] = fibonacci(n - 1, memo) + fibonacci(n - 2, memo);
        }
        memo[n]
    }
}

fn get_fib_seq(limit: usize) -> Result<Vec<i32>, Box<dyn Error>> {
    const MAX_LIMIT: usize = 10;
    if limit > MAX_LIMIT {
        return Err("Vector size limit exceeded".into());
    }

    let mut memo = vec![0; limit + 1];
    let mut sequence = vec![];
    for i in 0..limit {
        sequence.push(fibonacci(i, &mut memo));
    }

    Ok(sequence)
}

fn invert<T: Clone>(v: Vec<T>) -> Vec<T> {
    let mut inverted_v = vec![v[0].clone(); v.len()];
    let len = v.len();
    for i in 0..len {
        inverted_v[len - i - 1] = v[i].clone();
    }
    inverted_v
}

fn main() {
    let limit = 10;
    match get_fib_seq(limit) {
        Ok(mut sequence) => {
            println!("{:?}", sequence);
            sequence = invert(sequence);
            println!("{:?}", sequence);
        }
        Err(e) => println!("An error occurred: {}", e),
    }
}
```

This program starts by creating a Fibonacci sequence of the specified size limit using the `get_fib_seq` function. If the limit is beyond the defined `MAX_LIMIT`, an error is returned.

The `fibonacci` function uses memoization to store already calculated Fibonacci numbers, which optimizes memory usage.

The `invert` function performs the inversion operation on the vector.

In the `main` function, we try to get the Fibonacci sequence of the size limit and if successful, we print it, then invert it and print the inverted sequence. If an error occurs, we print it out.

Here's a very basic C++ code snippet that performs a circular right shift operation (rotate right), and shows a pop and insert operation in this shifting process for an array:

```C++
#include<bits/stdc++.h>

using namespace std;

// function to rotate array to right by 1
void rotateRight(int arr[], int n){
    // pop last element
    int temp = arr[n-1];

    // shift array elements to right by 1
    for(int i=n-1; i>0; i--) {
        arr[i] = arr[i-1];
    }

    // insert popped element at first position
    arr[0] = temp;
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);

    cout << "Original array:\n";
    for(int i=0; i<n; i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }

    rotateRight(arr, n);

    cout << "\nArray after right rotation:\n";
    for(int j=0; j<n; j++) {
        cout << arr[j] << " ";
    }
    return 0;
}
```
When you execute this code, it first prints the original array {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}. After we call the `rotateRight` function on this array, the last element of the array goes to the first position and all other elements move one position to the right resulting in the new array {5, 1, 2, 3, 4} which is then printed.

You can also adjust this code to rotate left if you want, or even rotate multiple positions by calling the `rotateRight` or `rotateLeft` function in a loop. Note that in real-world code, you may want to check for things like null or empty arrays, etc. for added robustness. 

Remember that C++ has no intrinsic circular shift operation, circular shift of an array is implemented manually as seen in the above code. Also, C++ arrays do not support pop and insert operations inherently like Python, so we manually showed these operations in the above code by copying elements and replacing them at appropriate positions.

