To effectively implement a Promise in JavaScript to handle AJAX operations, you would need to wrap your AJAX call inside a Promise object. Promises in JavaScript are used to handle asynchronous operations, providing a more flexible way to organize and chain them. 

```JavaScript
function ajaxPromise(url) {
    return new Promise(function(resolve, reject) {
        // Standard XHR to load an image
        var request = new XMLHttpRequest();
        request.open('GET', url); // By setting async to false, it changes the request to a synchronous request
        request.responseType = 'json';
        request.onload = function() {
            if (request.status === 200) {
                // Resolve the promise with the response text
                resolve(request.response);
            }
            else {
                // Otherwise reject with the status text
                // which will hopefully be a meaningful error
                reject(Error('AJAX error occurred: ' + request.statusText));
            }
        };

        request.onerror = function() {
            // Also deal with the case when the entire request fails to begin with
            // This is probably a network error, so reject the promise with an appropriate message
            reject(Error('There was a network error.'));
        };

        // Send the request
        request.send();
    });
}
```

This function makes an AJAX call to a URL and returns a Promise. If the AJAX call is successful, the promise is resolved with the returned data. If the AJAX call fails due to an error, the Promise is rejected and the error message is returned.

This allows us to fully leverage the power of promises, providing an effective way to handle asynchronous operations. You can easily chain promises together and manage errors using catch blocks. Here is how you can use it:

```JavaScript
ajaxPromise('https://api.github.com/users/octocat')
    .then(data => console.log(data))
    .catch(error => console.error(error));
```
Remember, using Promises can help you write cleaner and more modular code, as well as make error handling more intuitive. It also makes it easier to manage multiple asynchronous operations.

