Here is a solution using the Floyd's Cycle Detection algorithm (also known as the "tortoise and the hare"):

To utilize this algorithm, two pointers, which move at different speeds, traverse the linked list. In a cyclic list, the faster pointer will eventually meet the slower pointer again.

```csharp
public class Node 
{
    public int Value { get; set; }
    public Node Next { get; set; }
}

public class LinkedList 
{
    public Node Head { get; set; }

    public bool HasLoop()
    {
        if (Head == null)
            return false;

        Node slowPointer = this.Head;
        Node fastPointer = this.Head;

        while (fastPointer != null && fastPointer.Next != null) 
        {
            slowPointer = slowPointer.Next;
            fastPointer = fastPointer.Next.Next;

            if (slowPointer == fastPointer)
                return true; // Loop detected
        }

        return false; // No loop detected
    }
}
```

The helper `Node` class represents each node in the LinkedList.

The `HasLoop` function inside the LinkedList class utilizes two pointers: `slowPointer` and `fastPointer`. The `slowPointer` moves one step at a time while `fastPointer` moves two steps at a time.

If there is a loop, `fastPointer` will eventually catch up to `slowPointer`. If there is no loop, `fastPointer` will reach the end of the LinkedList (`null` value).

Please note that this algorithm works only if there's no self-loop, i.e., a node's `next` node is not itself.

