```javascript
function setIntersection(s1, s2) {
    // Initialize a set for storing the intersection elements
    let intersection = new Set();

    // Traverse each element in the first set
    for (let elem of s1) {
        // If the current element exists in the second set, add it to the intersection set
        if (s2.has(elem)) {
            intersection.add(elem);
        }
    }

    // Convert the set to a list for sorting
    intersection = Array.from(intersection);

    // Sort the array in ascending order
    intersection.sort();

    return intersection;
}
```

Please note that JavaScript does not have a built-in set type in the same way that Python does. Therefore, we use the `Set` object in JavaScript, which was introduced in ES6 and has wide support now in modern browsers and Node.js. The `Set` object allows us to store unique values of any type. In the case of handling duplicates, it will automatically handle it for us when we add elements into it.

