However, if you still wish to go ahead, see the following code to add 'removeItem' method to the Array prototype:

```JavaScript
Array.prototype.removeItem = function(item) {
    let index = this.indexOf(item);

    if (index !== -1) {
        this.splice(index, 1);
    } 
    return this;
};
```

In this function, we first check if the item is in the array using the 'indexOf' function. If the item is in the array ('indexOf' does not return -1), we use the 'splice' function to remove it.

Let's now consider the edge cases:

1. If the array does not contain the item, 'indexOf' returns -1 and the 'splice' function is not called. The array is therefore returned without any modifications.
2. If the item occurs more than once in the array, only the first occurrence of the item is removed, since 'indexOf' only returns the index of the first matching item.
3. If the array is initially empty, 'indexOf' returns -1, 'splice' function is not called and the empty array is returned.

However, if you want to remove all instances of an item, you could modify the function like so:

```JavaScript
Array.prototype.removeItem = function(item) {
    let index = this.indexOf(item);
    while (index !== -1) {
        this.splice(index, 1);
        index = this.indexOf(item);
    }
    return this;
};
```

