PHP provides the function `session_destroy()` which is used to destroy all of the data associated with the current session. It does not unset any of the global variables associated with the session, or unset the session cookie.

Here's a simple example of how you can use this function to create a session logout function:

```php
function logout() {
    // 1. First, we check if a session is active by checking if the session has a name
    if(session_name() === '') {
        session_start();
    }

    // 2. Then, we unset and destroy the session.
    $_SESSION = array();

    // 3. If it's desired to kill the session, also delete the session cookie.
    // Note: This will destroy the session, and not just the session data!
    if (ini_get("session.use_cookies")) {
        $params = session_get_cookie_params();
        setcookie(session_name(), '', time() - 42000,
            $params["path"], $params["domain"],
            $params["secure"], $params["httponly"]
        );
    }

    // Finally, destroy the session.
    session_destroy();
}
```

Usage:

```php
// sometime later in another part of the script
logout();
```

This function checks if a session is active first. After checking that a session is active, we unset all session variables by setting the $_SESSION global variable to an empty array. Then, we destroy the session by calling session_destroy().

In addition, this function also checks the php directive `session.use_cookies` to see if we should also unset the session cookie. If it's true, we use `setcookie()` to set the session cookie's expiration date to a date in the past (effectively, immediately deleting it).

This function will effectively log the user out by destroying all session data.

