The use of `@@Symbol` methods, such as `[Symbol.search]`, in RegExp might not be very common, but it can be handy in certain situations.

`Symbol.search` in particular, is used to overwrite the default behavior of string search for a regular expression. By default, calling search on a string with a regex loosely translates into a call to regex.exec() which returns the index position of the first match or -1 if no match is found.

However, there might be use cases where the developer wants to use the String.search() method, but with a different behavior than the default one. That's when `[Symbol.search]` comes in.

There can be many reasons to do this. For instance, if an application is working with very large data sets a developer might want to use a more efficient search algorithm optimized to their specific needs. Another scenario might be when the developer wants to apply some custom pre-processing to the input or result data.

Most commonly, you are going to use it when you want to extend RegExp and customize its behavior based on your application's needs.

An example use case:

Let's say, in your application, you want to perform case insensitive search using `.search()` method. JavaScript doesn't directly allow this. You can achieve this by using `Symbol.search`:

```javascript
class MyRegExp extends RegExp {
    constructor(str) {
        super(str, 'i'); // 'i' flag for case insensitive
    }
    [Symbol.search](str) {
        return super[Symbol.search](str);
    }
}

console.log('Hello World'.search(new MyRegExp('hello'))); // 0
console.log('Hello World'.search(new MyRegExp('WORLD'))); // 6
```

In this example, 'hello' and 'WORLD' are differently cased than the words in the string, but our `.search()` method returns the correct index. 

Remember, you would only use something like this in specific cases where default JavaScript behavior doesn't suit your needs. Always consider performance, browser support, and code readability as well.

