The optimized version of the above code is as follows:

```C++
struct Node
{
  int data;
  struct Node* left;
  struct Node* right;
} *root;

void insert(int data)
{
  struct Node* newNode = new Node;
  newNode->data = data;
  newNode->left = newNode->right = NULL;

  if (root == NULL)
  {
    root = newNode;
  }
  else
  {
    struct Node** current = &root;
    while (*current != NULL)
    {
      if (data < (*current)->data)
      {
        current = &((*current)->left);
      }
      else
      {
        current = &((*current)->right);
      }
    }
    *current = newNode;
  }
}
```

This implementation works efficiently for large binary trees since the insert operation occurs in O(log n) time complexity (n being the number of nodes in the tree), assuming the tree is reasonably balanced. This is because in a binary search tree, each comparison allows us to skip over half of the tree, decreasing it to a logarithmic scale. 

However, the above time complexity is only for ideal/balanced binary search trees. For skewed binary search trees, the time complexity can be O(n) where n is the number of nodes. In order to ensure an efficient binary search tree operations for all conditions, self-balancing Binary Search Trees like AVL, Red-Black tree etc. can be used. 

This new implementation improves the readability of the code, maintains the same efficiency, and handles edge cases properly.

