Here's Rust-compliant code for your function:

```rust
fn complex_brackets(input: Vec<&str>) -> bool {
    let brackets: std::collections::HashMap<char, char> = [
        (')', '('),
        ('}', '{'),
        (']', '[')
    ].iter().cloned().collect();
    let input_string: String = input.into_iter().collect();
    let mut stack: Vec<char> = Vec::new();

    for bracket in input_string.chars() {
        if brackets.values().any(|&x| x == bracket) {
            stack.push(bracket);
        } else if brackets.keys().any(|&x| x == bracket) {
            if stack.is_empty() || brackets[&bracket] != stack.pop().unwrap() {
                return false;
            }
        }
    }
    stack.is_empty()
}

fn main() {
    assert_eq!(complex_brackets(vec!["{", "} [", "))", "[("]), true);
    assert_eq!(complex_brackets(vec!["}", "}", "{", "{"]), false);
}
```

In Rust, you need to specify the type of each variable. The first line initializes a HashMap where the keys are closing brackets and the values are their corresponding opening brackets. In Rust, you need to call the `iter()` method to iterate over an array and `cloned()` to literally clone every element available from that iterator. Then `collect()` is used to transform collected iterator values into a collection (in this case, into a HashMap).

In `let input_string: String = input.into_iter().collect();`, we are converting our vector of string slices into a single String object. The `into_iter()` function consumes the input vector, transforming it into an iterator. The `collect()` function then consumes the iterator, transforming it into a specified type (`String`).

We use a mutable vector as our stack, as described above. The `Vec::new()` function initializes a new, empty vector.

We then iterate over each character in the string. If the character is in the values of the brackets HashMap (meaning it is an opening bracket), we append it to our stack. If it is in the keys of the HashMap (meaning it is a closing bracket), we verify if the stack is empty or the last open bracket in the stack does not match with the closing bracket; if either case is true, the function returns false because the brackets are not balanced. 

The function finally returns whether the stack is empty or not. If it is not, this means that there are unbalanced brackets left in the stack, so the function returns false. If it is, this means all brackets were successfully balanced, so the function returns true. 

The `main()` function in Rust is the entry point of the program where you call the function with some test cases. You can see that vectors in Rust are defined using `vec![]` and strings are defined raw or using `String::from()`.

