This class uses the CSS `transform` property to accurately center the div both horizontally and vertically regardless of the parent's size. The `position: absolute` together with `top: 50%` and `left: 50%` properties moves the top left corner of the element to the center of its parent. Then we use a negative margin to pull the element to the left and up by half of its size. The CSS `transform` property then shifts the element by 50% of its height and width, which, considering the prior manipulations, perfectly centers the element within its parent.

The `transform` property is supported in Internet Explorer 9 and later versions, and the `-ms-transform` property serves as a fallback for Internet Explorer 9 specifically, while the `-webkit-transform` property serves as a fallback for Chrome, Safari, and Opera. Please note, this solution only works if the parent div has a `position` of either `relative`, `absolute` or `fixed`.

