The issue is likely due to the way that Android Alarm Manager works; it operates in a different Isolate (Thread in the context of Java) as opposed to the UI Thread where you have instantiated the controller class. This means the function callback you pass to the Android Alarm Manager needs to be a top-level or static function.

The problem arises because alarm callbacks are not capable of executing within the same Isolate as your Flutter application. Attempting to reference your controller in the callback function will result in an error, because that controller doesn't exist in the new Isolate that's created for the callback.

Instead, your function `controller.testFromController` is tied to a particular instance of the controller. This instance (and by extension, the function) doesn't exist in the context of the other Isolate that Alarm Manager is attempting to call it from.

To solve this issue, consider a few approaches:

1) Make your function a top-level function, not bound to any class instance:

```
void alarmCallback() {
  print('alarm callback');
}
...
AndroidAlarmManager.oneShot(Duration(seconds: 3), 1, alarmCallback);
```

2) Use a static function:

```
class TestClass {
  static void testFunc() {
    print('testFunc');
  }
}
...
AndroidAlarmManager.oneShot(Duration(seconds: 3), 1, TestClass.testFunc);
```

3) Use `IsolateNameServer` to pass data between isolates:

https://api.dart.dev/stable/2.9.3/dart-isolate/IsolateNameServer-class.html

Remember, when scheduling an alarm, avoid closing the entire application before the alarm goes off. Background operations like this won't keep your entire app in memory.

