Algorithm Selection: Merge Sort

Reason: Merge sort is a divide and conquer algorithm that has a time complexity of O(n log n) and it naturally handles 'stability' (preserves relative order of same elements in the input, which in some problems, is desirable).

Pseudo Code of Merge Sort :

1. Check if array length is 1, if yes return.
2. Find the middle point to divide the array into two halves.
3. Firstly, call merge sort for the first half.
4. Secondly, call merge sort for the second half.
5. Merge the two halves sorted in step 3 and 4.

Java Code Implementation:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {23, 10, 4, 15, 66, 28, 11, 23, 5, 89, 13};
        mergeSort(array, 0, array.length - 1);

        for (int num : array) {
            System.out.print(num + " ");
        }
    }

    public static void mergeSort(int[] array, int start, int end) {
        if (start < end) {
            int median = (start + end) / 2;
            mergeSort(array, start, median);
            mergeSort(array, median + 1, end);
            merge(array, start, median, end);
        }
    }

    public static void merge(int[] array, int start, int median, int end) {
        int n1 = median - start + 1;
        int n2 = end - median;

        int[] leftArray = new int[n1];
        int[] rightArray = new int[n2];

        for (int i = 0; i < n1; ++i) {
            leftArray[i] = array[start + i];
        }
        for (int j = 0; j < n2; ++j) {
            rightArray[j] = array[median + 1 + j];
        }

        int i = 0, j = 0;
        int k = start;
        while (i < n1 && j < n2) {
            if (leftArray[i] <= rightArray[j]) {
                array[k] = leftArray[i];
                i++;
            } else {
                array[k] = rightArray[j];
                j++;
            }
            k++;
        }

        while (i < n1) {
            array[k] = leftArray[i];
            i++;
            k++;
        }

        while (j < n2) {
            array[k] = rightArray[j];
            j++;
            k++;
        }
    }
}
```

Exceptions considered: In this code, we have handled all corner cases. array is divided until there are single elements (start < end). Index-Out-Of-Bounds exception may occur if not handled rightly while splitting and merging. But this code handles those exceptions. We use a temporary left and right arrays for merging, to avoid any index related exceptions. So, as far as this code and merge-sort algorithm is concerned, all possible exceptions have been handled.

