TypeScript does not support optional chaining to extract nested type from an object. If your property could potentially be undefined at any level, you need to handle that within your typings otherwise TypeScript infers that the full object might be undefined If there is no optional mark in any of the nexted object then you code will compile.

Optionally, you could create a shorthand for the type, to make it easier to use:

```TypeScript
type TMyType = foo['bar']['baz']['property'];

function f (input: TMyType) {
    console.log(input);
}
```
But if there's an optional property, the only option to solve this is defining every level of nested objects as a separate type, like so:

```TypeScript
interface Foo {
    bar?: Bar;
}
interface Bar {
    baz: Baz;
}
interface Baz {
    property: string;
}

function f(input: Baz['property']) {
    console.log(input);
}
```

This way, you ensure that the types are correctly referenced at every level, and you can use them easily in your function definitions.

