Here is a JavaScript solution wrapped in a class, which includes methods setStrings and evaluate:

```js
class Anagram {
    constructor() {
        this.string1 = '';
        this.string2 = '';
    }

    setStrings(str1, str2) {
        if (typeof str1 !== 'string' || typeof str2 !== 'string') {
            throw new Error('Inputs must be strings');
        }

        this.string1 = str1.replace(/\s/g, '').toLowerCase();
        this.string2 = str2.replace(/\s/g, '').toLowerCase();
    }

    evaluate() {
        if (this.string1.length !== this.string2.length) {
            throw new Error('Strings are not anagrams');
        }

        let swaps = 0;
        const counter = new Array(26).fill(0);

        for(let i = 0; i < this.string1.length; i++) {
            counter[this.string1.charCodeAt(i) - 97]++;
            counter[this.string2.charCodeAt(i) - 97]--;
        }

        for(let j = 0; j < 26; j++) {
            if(counter[j] !== 0) {
                throw new Error('Strings are not anagrams');
            }
        }

        let i = this.string1.length - 1;
        let j = i;

        while(i >= 0) {
            if(this.string1[i] !== this.string2[j]) {
                while(j >= 0 && this.string1[i] !== this.string2[j]) j--; 

                if (j >= 0) {
                    let temp = this.string2[j];
                    this.string2 = this.string2.substring(0, j) + this.string2.substring(j+1);
                    this.string2 = [this.string2.slice(0, i), temp, this.string2.slice(i)].join('');
                    swaps++;
                }  
            }

            i--;
            j = i;
        }

        return swaps;
    }
}
```
Example usage:

```js
const anagramChecker = new Anagram();
anagramChecker.setStrings('angry man', 'anagram');
console.log(anagramChecker.evaluate()); // will output 5
```
The evaluate method first checks that the strings are anagrams by comparing the frequency of each character in both strings. If they are anagrams, it then determines the number of swaps required to transform the first string into the second by checking each character from end to start. If a character is not in the correct position, it finds its correct position in the other string and swaps it. It counts each swap and returns the total after finishing the check.

Browser Helper Objects (BHO) was a mechanism used by versions of Internet Explorer to add functionality to the browser, however, they not supported in IE11 and Microsoft Edge. 

With that being said, to detect DOM modifications in IE7, you can use Mutation events. However, keep in mind that Mutation Events are deprecated. Performance-wise they are slow and lead to cross-browser inconsistencies.

The MutationEvent interface provides events which are sent to notify code of changes to the structure of a DOM tree.

Here's an example:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMSubtreeModified', function(e){
  console.log('tree changed');
}, false);
```

This event will fire up every time you change something in the DOM structure.

Nevertheless, the recommendation would be to move to more modern solution if possible, like developing a Chrome extension or Firefox add-on where there are far more resources and the development experience is ultimately better. These platforms have robust APIs for listening to DOM changes (`MutationObserver` for instance):

```javascript
// select the target node
var target = document.querySelector('#some-id');

// create an observer instance
var observer = new MutationObserver(function(mutations) {
  mutations.forEach(function(mutation) {
    console.log(mutation.type);
  });
});

// configuration of the observer:
var config = { attributes: true, childList: true, characterData: true };

// pass in the target node, as well as the observer options
