Here's a simple C program to count the occurrences of each character:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <ctype.h>

#define MAX_CHAR 256

int main() {
    FILE *file;
    int count[MAX_CHAR] = {0};
    int total_distinct = 0;
    char ch;

    file = fopen("input.txt", "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        printf("Could not open the file.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    while ((ch = fgetc(file)) != EOF) {
        if (ch != ' ') 
            count[tolower(ch)]++;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_CHAR; i++) {
        if (count[i] > 0) {
            printf("'%c' : %d\n", i, count[i]);
            total_distinct++;
        }
    }

    printf("Total distinct characters: %d\n", total_distinct);

    fclose(file);

    return 0;
}
```

The above program will read the content from the text file named `input.txt`, count the occurrences of each character, and print the result. The `tolower` function is used to ensure the count is case-insensitive, i.e., 'a' and 'A' are considered to be the same.

This code will return an error message if the file cannot be opened. If the file is successfully opened but is empty, the program will report that there are no distinct characters. The total number of distinct characters is also printed at the end.

Note: The `MAX_CHAR` is defined as 256, which is the total number of ASCII characters. You can adjust this value if you're dealing with a different charset.

