Please note that in Rust, String indices don't always correspond to the positions of grapheme clusters that a user would call a character. String slice indices are byte offsets, not character offsets, so a String with multibyte characters will have a length in bytes that is longer than the number of characters. Thus, I'm assuming that by "characters" you mean Rust's concept of characters (i.e., bytes for a UTF-8 encoded string), not the user's concept of characters (i.e., grapheme clusters).

```rust
use std::io::{self, Write};

fn main() {
    // Create a vector with the indices of the characters to print
    let indices = vec![3, 4, 5];

    print!("Enter a string: ");
    io::stdout().flush().unwrap();

    let mut input = String::new();
    io::stdin().read_line(&mut input).unwrap();

    let input_trim = input.trim();               

    for index in indices {
        if index < input_trim.len() {
            print!("{}", &input_trim.chars().nth(index).unwrap());
        }
    }

    println!();
}
```

