&lt;p&gt;
Consider the following collection of cubical dice with nonstandard distribution of pips:
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Die $A$: $1$ $4$ $4$ $4$ $4$ $4$&lt;br&gt;
Die $B$: $2$ $2$ $2$ $5$ $5$ $5$&lt;br&gt;
Die $C$: $3$ $3$ $3$ $3$ $3$ $6$&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
A game is conducted by two participants selecting a die in sequence and rolling it. The participant who rolls the highest numerical value emerges victorious.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
If the initial participant selects die $A$ and the subsequent participant selects die $B$ we obtain&lt;br&gt;
$P(\text{subsequent participant emerges victorious}) = 7/12 \gt 1/2$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
If the initial participant selects die $B$ and the subsequent participant selects die $C$ we obtain&lt;br&gt;
$P(\text{subsequent participant emerges victorious}) = 7/12 \gt 1/2$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
If the initial participant selects die $C$ and the subsequent participant selects die $A$ we obtain&lt;br&gt;
$P(\text{subsequent participant emerges victorious}) = 25/36 \gt 1/2$.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
Thus, irrespective of the die the initial participant selects, the subsequent participant can select a different die and have a probability greater than $50\%$ of emerging victorious.&lt;br&gt;
A collection of dice possessing this characteristic is termed a &lt;strong&gt;nontransitive collection of dice&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;
We aim to explore the quantity of nontransitive dice collections that exist. We will consider the following stipulations:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;There are three six-faced dice with each face possessing between $1$ and $N$ pips, inclusive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Dice with identical distribution of pips are considered equal, irrespective of the face on the die where the pips are situated.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The same pip value may be present on multiple dice; if both participants roll the identical value neither participant emerges victorious.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The collections of dice $\{A,B,C\}$, $\{B,C,A\}$ and $\{C,A,B\}$ are considered the same collection.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;p&gt;
For $N = 7$ we discover there are $9780$ such collections.&lt;br&gt;
What is the quantity for $N = 30$?
&lt;/p&gt;

