Here's a Java method that accomplishes this task. Note that a `long` can hold values from -9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807, so this will limit the maximum factorial that can be computed to the largest value that can fit in this range.

Firstly, we would need to create a hashmap for memoization purpose to store previously computed results.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;

public class Factorial {
    private HashMap<Integer, Long> memo = new HashMap<>();

    public long factorial(int n) {
       if (n < 0) {
          throw new IllegalArgumentException("n must be non-negative");
       }
       if (n <= 1) {
          return 1;
       }
       if (this.memo.containsKey(n)) {
          return this.memo.get(n);
       }
       long computed = this.factorial(n - 1) * n;
       this.memo.put(n, computed);
       return computed;
    }

    public void printFactorial(int num) {
       try {
          long result = -1 * factorial(num);
          System.out.println(result);
       } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
           System.out.println(e.getMessage());
       } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
           System.out.println("Error: The result exceeds the maximum limit");
       } catch (Exception e) {
           System.out.println("An error occurred: " + e.getMessage());
       }
    }
}
```

To use this code, you would do something like this:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Factorial factorial = new Factorial();
        factorial.printFactorial(10);  // should print -3628800
    }
}
```

This code starts by checking if `n` is less than 0, in which case it throws an exception. If `n` is 0 or 1, it returns 1 directly (since factorial of 0 & 1 is 1). If the factorial of `n` has been previously calculated and stored in the `memo` HashMap, it gets the saved value and returns it. Otherwise, it calculates the factorial recursively and stores the result in `memo` before returning it.

In the `printFactorial` method, it fetches the factorial and multiplies it with -1 to get negative equivalent and then prints it. If any type of error occurs, it catches the exception and prints an error message.

