JavaScript can be used to create a fairly accurate stopwatch. However, keep in mind that JavaScript is not designed for precise time measurements. It should be sufficient for a basic stopwatch, but for cases where a high degree of accuracy is required, you would be better off using a specialized tool or language.

Here is an example of a very simple stopwatch, created with JavaScript, that could also consider time discrepancies due to latency or server request-response time. This stopwatch will use dates to measure time, a start/pause button, and a reset button:

```javascript
// Initialize the variables
let startTime, endTime, running, duration = 0;

function start() {
    if(running) throw new Error('Stopwatch has already started.');
    running = true;
    // Record the start time
    startTime = new Date();
}

function stop() {
    if(!running) throw new Error('Stopwatch is not started.');
    running = false;
    // Record the stop time
    endTime = new Date();

    // Calculate the time difference in seconds
    const seconds = Math.abs(endTime - startTime) / 1000; 
    duration += seconds;
}

function reset() {
    // Reset all the values
    startTime = null;
    endTime = null;
    running = false;
    duration = 0;
}

function getDuration() {
    // Return the recorded time
    return duration;
}
```
This is a basic example which you might need to adjust according to your need and this doesn't consider the latency or server request-response time. Unfortunately, in JavaScript we can't be 100% accurate considering latency in each environment is pretty different.

