```java
import java.util.*;
public class Main
{
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(getPrimeFactorProduct(315));    //Output is 5*5*3*7 = 525
    }

    public static long getPrimeFactorProduct(long n){
        Set<Long> primes = getPrimeFactors(n);
        long product = 1;
        for(long p : primes){
            product *= p;
        }
        return product;
    }

    public static Set<Long> getPrimeFactors(long numbers) {
        long n = numbers;
        Set<Long> factors = new HashSet<Long>();
        for (long i = 2; i <= n / i; i++) {
            while (n % i == 0) {
                factors.add(i);
                n /= i;
            }
        }
        if (n > 1) {
            factors.add(n);
        }
        return factors;
    }
}
```
This function firstly uses the method `getPrimeFactors()` to find out all prime factors of the input number. This is done by continuously checking if the current number can be divided by the current factor without rest. If this is possible, this factor is a prime factor of the original number and is added to HashSet. This factor is then divided from the current number and this step is repeated until this is not possible anymore. Then, the factor is increased and the steps repeated, up until the point where the factor is not smaller or equal to the square root of the current number. If the current number is still larger than 1, the rest number is also a prime factor and is added to the HashSet.

After prime factors are found, the product of these factors is calculated in the method `getPrimeFactorProduct()`. We initialize the product at 1 and then go through all the stored prime factors within the HashSet. We always multiply the current prime factor with the current product and update it until all factors have been taken into account. The final product is then returned.

