To export an entire class from a DLL in C++, you can do something similar to what you mentioned in your question. Here's a basic example (Windows):

First, create the class in your DLL:

```cpp
// myclass.h
#ifdef EXPORT_DLL
#define DLL_EXPORT __declspec(dllexport)
#else
#define DLL_EXPORT __declspec(dllimport)
#endif

class DLL_EXPORT MyClass
{
public:
    MyClass();       // constructor
    ~MyClass();      // destructor
    void someMethod();
};
```

Then in the .cpp file for that class:

```cpp
// myclass.cpp
#include "myclass.h"

MyClass::MyClass() { /*...*/ }
MyClass::~MyClass() { /*...*/ }
void MyClass::someMethod() { /*...*/ }
```

Note the `DLL_EXPORT` macro, which resolves to `__declspec(dllexport)` when you're building the DLL, but to `__declspec(dllimport)` when you're building a client of the DLL. You can do this by defining `EXPORT_DLL` in your DLL build, but not in your client build.

To import the DLL, include the same "myclass.h" in your client code and call it as normal:

```cpp
#include "myclass.h"
...
MyClass m;
m.someMethod();
...
```

When you include the header file in your client code and link against the DLL's `.lib` file at compile time, the compiler knows how to call the public methods on the DLL's class.

As for dynamically loading the class at runtime, that's a bit more complex. You would probably need to have exported 'factory' functions that create instances of the class and return them, though you would need to carefully manage ownership and lifetime of those instances.

For example:

```cpp
// In your DLL:
extern "C" DLL_EXPORT IMyClass* CreateMyClassInstance()
{
    return new MyClass();
}

extern "C" DLL_EXPORT void DestroyMyClassInstance(IMyClass* p)
{
    delete p;
}
```

Then in the client code, you would dynamically load the DLL using `LoadLibrary()`, get pointers to these functions using `GetProcAddress()`, and then use those function pointers to create and destroy instances of the class.

```cpp
typedef IMyClass* (*CreateMyClassInstanceFunc)();
typedef void (*DestroyMyClassInstanceFunc)(IMyClass*);

HMODULE hDLL = LoadLibrary(...);
CreateMyClassInstanceFunc createFunc = (CreateMyClassInstanceFunc)GetProcAddress(hDLL, "CreateMyClassInstance");
DestroyMyClassInstanceFunc destroyFunc = (DestroyMyClassInstanceFunc)GetProcAddress(hDLL, "DestroyMyClassInstance");
IMyClass* p = createFunc();
// use p...
destroyFunc(p);
FreeLibrary(hDLL);
```

This might require using some kind of interface (either base class or pure virtual class) to interact with the class instances, because the client code doesn't know the size or layout of the `MyClass` objects. This is where C++ gets a bit messy when dealing with DLLs. It's simpler if you're using a language like C# or Java which has native support for dynamically loading assemblies or .jar files and using reflection to create instances, but that's not really the case in C++. It's usually easier to just link at compile time and use the classes directly, unless you have a specific need for runtime linking.

