This sounds like implementing a 3D perspective for images in a container using JavaScript. Although achieving a 3D perspective would also generally involve CSS3 and HTML, here's an example of a JavaScript function to possibly start the process:

```javascript
// Selects all the images within a specified container
let images = document.querySelectorAll('.container img');

// Function to transform the coordinates of the images
function transformImages() {
  images.forEach(img => {
    // Get image's boundingClientRect
    let rect = img.getBoundingClientRect();
    let x = rect.left + rect.width / 2;
    let y = rect.top + rect.height / 2;

    // Calculate the distance from the center of the viewport
    let dx = x - window.innerWidth / 2;
    let dy = y - window.innerHeight / 2;

    // Apply a transform to give a 3D perspective based on the distance to the center
    let depth = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
    let scale = Math.max(0, 1 - depth / 1000);
    img.style.transform = `translate3D(${-dx * scale}px, ${-dy * scale}px, ${-depth / 2}px)`;
  });
}

// Call function to transform the coordinates of the images
transformImages();

// Redraw 3D perspective when user resizes the window
window.addEventListener('resize', transformImages);
(window.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", transformImages));
```

This example moves images in a way that they seem to have depth, depending on their position relative to the center of the viewport.

Please note that this is a simplistic demonstration and isn't guaranteed to work perfectly in all scenarios. Mild motion-parallax effect is induced, yet for a true trompe l'oeil result, much more complex calculations or a 3D graphics library would be necessary.

You would also need to use CSS to add perspective to the container and set the `preserve-3d` property on the transforms. The images would need to be positioned absolutely within the container to be individually transformed as elements.

This example also does not handle updating the transform as the user scrolls or as elements are added/removed. You might need to set up additional event handlers to achieve the desired effect.

