No, referencing a field from an enclosing class does not create a circular dependency. 

A circular dependency in Java is when Class A depends on Class B and Class B also depends on Class A, making it difficult for one to function without the other. 

In your example, the inner class is dependent on the Outer class, but the Outer class does not depend on the inner class. The Outer class simply contains an instance of the inner class. The outer class can exist without the inner class but not the other way around. 

To avoid a circular dependency, you should review the organization of your classes and see if you can separate the responsibilities of each class more clearly. In your example, if you are worried about creating a dependency between the inner class and the outer class, you can pass the necessary fields from the outer class to the inner class at the time of its creation, like so:

```
public class Outer {

  private Other o;
  private Inner i;    

  public Outer() {
    o = new Other();

    i = new Inner(o);
  }

  // Inner class
  public class Inner {
    private Other other;

    public Inner (Other other) {
      this.other = other;
    }

    public void doSomething() {
      other.foo();
    }
  }
}
```

This way, the Inner class still has access to the required dependencies but those are clearly defined in the constructor of the class and do not have a direct dependency on the Outer class thus creating a clear separation of responsibilities.

