The binary search algorithm works by dividing the search space in half and repeat until the search space is reduced to zero. Here is how you can implement it in C:

```c
#include<stdio.h>

int binarySearch(int arr[], int l, int r, int x)
{
    while (l <= r) {
        int mid = l + (r - l) / 2;

        // Check if x is the middle element
        if (arr[mid] == x)
            return mid;

        // If x is greater, ignore left half
        if (arr[mid] < x)
            l = mid + 1;

        // If x is smaller, ignore right half
        else
            r = mid - 1;
    }

    // if we reach here, then element was
    // not present
    return -1;
}

int main() 
{
    int arr[] = {3, 4, 6, 9, 10}; 
    int n = sizeof(arr)/ sizeof(arr[0]); 
    int x = 9; 
    int result = binarySearch(arr, 0, n-1, x);
    if(result != -1) 
        printf("Element is present at index %d", result); 
    else
        printf("Element is not present in array");
    return 0;
}
```

Note that in the binary search algorithm, first, middle element of the list is checked. If it's equal to the desired value then function returns its index. If target value is greater than middle element then first half of the array is ignored and the search continues in the right half. In opposite case if target value is less than middle element then search continues in the left half of the array. This process continues until desired element is found or subarray size becomes zero.

