Consider two positive integers $A$ and $B$. We define a relationship between them, denoted as &lt;dfn&gt;connected&lt;/dfn&gt; (&#34;$A \leftrightarrow B$&#34;), if they satisfy either of the following conditions:&lt;br&gt;
(1) $A$ and $B$ are of the same length and differ by exactly one digit. An instance of this would be $123 \leftrightarrow 173$.&lt;br&gt;
(2) By appending a single digit to the left of $A$ (or $B$), we obtain $B$ (or $A$). For instance, $23 \leftrightarrow 223$ and $123 \leftrightarrow 23$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
We introduce the concept of a &lt;dfn&gt;$2$&#39;s relative&lt;/dfn&gt;, which is a prime number $P$ that can be linked to $2$ through a chain of connected primes, with the condition that no prime in the chain is greater than $P$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
As an illustration, $127$ is a $2$&#39;s relative. A possible chain could be:&lt;br&gt;
$2 \leftrightarrow 3 \leftrightarrow 13 \leftrightarrow 113 \leftrightarrow 103 \leftrightarrow 107 \leftrightarrow 127$&lt;br&gt;
However, $11$ and $103$ are not considered $2$&#39;s relatives.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Let's define a function $F(N)$ that represents the sum of all primes less than or equal to $N$ that do not qualify as $2$&#39;s relatives.&lt;br&gt;
It has been established that $F(10^3) = 431$ and $F(10^4) = 78728$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Your task is to compute the value of $F(10^7)$.
&lt;/p&gt;

