This challenge can be solved using sorting and data structure, specifically trie (prefix tree).

The key idea of how to solve it is:
1. As we are looking for the most compact representation, so any word that is suffix of another can be ignored. In this case, we only need the longest word because the shorter suffix will always be part of the longest word.
2. Due to this, we can sort the words' array in reverse order (`words[i] > words[i+1]`) and by its length. This ensures that if there is a word that is a suffix of another, the longest will come first.
3. After sorting, insert each word into a trie, if the word is newly inserted (it is a new suffix), we keep track of the size of this word and the hash symbol.

Here is a Python code implementing the above idea:

```python
class Trie:
    def __init__(self):
        self.root = {}
        self.end_symbol = '#'

    def insert(self, word):
        is_new_word = False
        node = self.root
        for i in range(len(word) - 1, -1, -1):
            if word[i] not in node:
                is_new_word = True
                node[word[i]] = {}
            node = node[word[i]]
        if self.end_symbol not in node:
            node[self.end_symbol] = {}
            is_new_word = True
        return is_new_word

class Solution:
    def minimumLengthEncoding(self, words):
        trie = Trie()
        words.sort(key = lambda word: [ -len(word), word])
        return sum(len(word) + 1 for word in words if trie.insert(word))
```

Using this approach, each word is checked only once, and the problem is solved in O(n log n) time complexity due to the sorting.

