Indeed, the default LocalFileSettingsProvider used by .NET settings mechanism stores user settings in a non-roaming folder, which is an issue if you want your settings to travel with the user on different machines.

As per your desire for a custom folder structure, unfortunately, there is no direct way to change the storage location with LocalFileSettingsProvider.

To overcome these, you could create a custom settings provider that inherits from LocalFileSDettingsProvider and overwrites necessary methods to customize the path of the configuration file and to make it roam. 

You may find it tricky, but here it is a sample of how this could be done:

```
internal class RoamingSettingsProvider : LocalFileSettingsProvider
{
    public override void Initialize(string name, NameValueCollection values)
    {
        base.Initialize(this.ApplicationName, values);
    }

    public override string ApplicationName
    {
        get
        {
            return Application.ProductName;
        }
        set
        {
            // Do nothing
        }      
    }

    public override SettingsPropertyValueCollection GetPropertyValues(SettingsContext context, SettingsPropertyCollection properties)
    {
        // Change the default path to roaming
        string prevPath = Application.LocalUserAppDataPath;
        Application.LocalUserAppDataPath = Application.UserAppDataPath;
        SettingsPropertyValueCollection valueCollection = base.GetPropertyValues(context, properties);
        Application.LocalUserAppDataPath = prevPath;

        return valueCollection;
    }

    public override void SetPropertyValues(SettingsContext context, SettingsPropertyValueCollection properties)
    {
        // Change the default path to roaming
        string prevPath = Application.LocalUserAppDataPath;
        Application.LocalUserAppDataPath = Application.UserAppDataPath;
        base.SetPropertyValues(context, properties);
        Application.LocalUserAppDataPath = prevPath;
    }
}
```

Then you should specify this provider to be used by settings in your Properties/Settings.settings file:

```
<SettingsFile xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/VisualStudio/2004/01/settings" CurrentProfile="(Default)" GeneratedClassNamespace="{NAMESPACE OF YOUR RoamingSettingsProvider}" GeneratedClassName="Settings">
  <Profiles />
  <Settings>
    <Setting Name="YourSetting" Type="System.Boolean" Scope="User">
      <Value Profile="(Default)">False</Value>
      <Provider Type="{NAMESPACE OF YOUR RoamingSettingsProvider}.RoamingSettingsProvider, {ASSEMBLY NAME WHERE YOUR RoamingSettingsProvider IS}" />
    </Setting>
  </Settings>
</SettingsFile>
```

Also, keep in mind that your RoamingSettingsProvider should be available in the assembly you specified for the provider. You might need to explicitly add reference to that assembly in your project.

Please note, that this code changes the file storage path to roaming for the whole application, so if you need only some of your settings to roam, you would need a more complex logic. 

It's also necessary to handle situations where you want to differentiate between various versions of your app, etc. (because the roaming setting would apply to all versions across all machines).

