It appears that the code snippet selects a random color for both the background and the text, which could result in very poor visibility if both colors roughly match. Even though the rgba format allows the selection of a random alpha value (which can provide some difference experience in the visibility), it's not necessarily enough to make sure your text will always be readable.

We could solve this by calculating the color's luminance and depending its value, deciding if the text should be white or black, thus ensuring contrast and therefore readability.

Here's one way to accomplish this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <style>
        body {
            transition: background-color 0.5s, color 0.5s; /* Just for nice color transition */
        }
    </style>
    <script>
        window.onload = function() {
            var r = Math.floor(Math.random() * 256);
            var g = Math.floor(Math.random() * 256);
            var b = Math.floor(Math.random() * 256);

            var luminosity = (0.299*r + 0.587*g + 0.114*b)/255;

            if (luminosity > 0.5) {
                document.body.style.color = 'black';  // bright colors - black font
            } else {
                document.body.style.color = 'white';  // dark colors - white font
            }

            var randomColor = 'rgb('+r+','+g+','+b+')';
            document.body.style.backgroundColor = randomColor;
        }
    </script>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Test content</h1>
    <p>This is some test content. Reloading the page should randomly change text and background colors, ensuring readability.</p>
</body>
</html>
```
This code will ensure that the text color is the appropriate color (black or white) to ensure visibility given any background color. The luminosity equation comes from the perceived brightness of colors in human vision.

Just keep in mind that `window.onload` will ensure that codes inside it will only run once the complete page is fully loaded, including all frames, objects and images. Therefore, if your webpage takes a while to load, you may see a stark white page until everything is done. In such case, consider using `document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function(){...})` which fires when the initial HTML document has been completely loaded and parsed.

