Yes, it is possible to calculate the coordinates of the 6 intersection points. Looking at the image you provided tactically, I will assume that the green circle is centered at the same point as the blue circle i.e. (cx, cy). If that is not your case, the following method would need minor adjustments. We will solve this problem based on simple trigonometric considerations. Let's discuss the P1 and P2 points, the others being symmetrical to these.

For both P1 and P2, the x-coordinate is the sum of the x-coordinate of the center and the radius of the blue circle times the cosine of a certain angle. This angle is 60 degrees for P1 and 120 degrees for P2. The y-coordinate is the sum of the y-coordinate of the center and the radius of the blue circle times the sine of the same angle.

To calculate the points of the green circle, replace the radius of the blue circle with the radius of the green circle in the formulas for x and y. The angles remain the same.

Here's code example in P5JS:

```js
let cx = 200; // x-coordinate of the center
let cy = 200; // y-coordinate of the center
let rBlue = 100; // radius of the blue circle
let rGreen = 150; // radius of the green circle

let bluePoints = [];
let greenPoints = [];

function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  angleMode(DEGREES); // Use degrees for trigonometric calculations

  // Calculate points for blue circle
  for (let angle = 60; angle <= 300; angle += 60) {
    let x = cx + rBlue * cos(angle);
    let y = cy + rBlue * sin(angle);
    bluePoints.push(createVector(x, y));
  }

  // Calculate points for green circle
  for (let angle = 60; angle <= 300; angle += 60) {
    let x = cx + rGreen * cos(angle);
    let y = cy + rGreen * sin(angle);
    greenPoints.push(createVector(x, y));
  }
}

function draw() {
  background(220);

  // Draw circles
  ellipse(cx, cy, rBlue*2);
  ellipse(cx, cy, rGreen*2);

  // Draw points
  for (let point of bluePoints) {
    fill(0);
    ellipse(point.x, point.y, 8);
  }
  for (let point of greenPoints) {
    fill(0, 255, 0);
    ellipse(point.x, point.y, 8);
  }
}
```
The "setup" function calculates the x and y coordinates for each point in both circles (bluePoints and greenPoints). In each loop, the angle decreases by 60 degrees, which corresponds to the angle between each point in an equilateral triangle. The "draw" function then displays the two circles and all points. Each point is represented by a small ellipse.

Here is the step by step procedure on how to remove a specific node from a singly linked list in Java:

```java
public class LinkedList {
    Node head; // head of list

    // Node class
    static class Node {
        int data;
        Node next;

        // Constructor to create a new node
        Node(int d) {
            data = d;
            next = null;
        }
    }

    /* Given a key, deletes the first occurrence of key in linked list */
    void deleteNode(int key)
    {
        // Store head node
        Node temp = head, prev = null;

        // If head node itself holds the key to be deleted
        if (temp != null && temp.data == key)
        {
            head = temp.next; // Changed head
            return;
        }

        // Search for the key to be deleted, keep track of the
        // previous node as we need to change temp.next
        while (temp != null && temp.data != key)
        {
            prev = temp;
            temp = temp.next;
        }  

        // If key was not present in linked list
        if (temp == null) return;

        // Unlink the node from linked list
        prev.next = temp.next;
    }

    // method to create a simple linked list with 3 nodes
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        LinkedList llist = new LinkedList();

        llist.head = new Node(1);
        Node second = new Node(2);
        Node third = new Node(3);

        llist.head.next = second; // Link first node with the second node
        second.next = third; // Link second node with the third node

        // delete the node with data 2
        llist.deleteNode(2);
    }
}
```

In this block of code, we first check if the head is null or if the head contains the key to be deleted. If so, we simply point head to the next node. If not, we traverse the list, saving the previous node until we find the node we want to delete, then we just set the next property of the preceding node to the next node of the currently selected node (skipping over the node we wish to delete). If the key does not exist in the list, the function will return without any change.

Here's a simple implementation of the Quick Sort algorithm in C++:

```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

void swap(int* a, int* b) {
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

int partition (int arr[], int low, int high) {
    int pivot = arr[high]; 
    int i = (low - 1);

    for (int j = low; j <= high-1; j++) {
        if (arr[j] < pivot) {
            i++; 
            swap(&arr[i], &arr[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(&arr[i + 1], &arr[high]);
    return (i + 1);
}

void quickSort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
        int pi = partition(arr, low, high);
        quickSort(arr, low, pi - 1);
        quickSort(arr, pi + 1, high);
    }
}

void printArray(int arr[], int size) {
    int i;
    for (i=0; i < size; i++)
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    cout << endl;
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {10, 7, 8, 9, 1, 5};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
    quickSort(arr, 0, n-1);
    cout << "Sorted array: \n";
    printArray(arr, n);
    return 0;
}
```

In this code, swap() is used to swap two elements, partition() function takes last element as pivot, places the pivot element at its correct position in the sorted array, and places all smaller elements to the left of the pivot and all greater elements to the right of the pivot.

quickSort() is the main function that implements the Quick Sort algorithm by calling itself recursively. 

