The problem stated is quite complex and should be approached step-by-step. This is because we need to implement multiple conditions involving sorting the array, shifting elements, maintaining the positions of even and odd elements and validating the odd count of elements smaller than the first in the sorted array. 

Below mentioned is a python solution that covers all the stated conditions:

```python
def move_three_balls(arr):
    if len(arr) == 0:
        return True

    swaps = [0]*len(arr)
    temp = arr.copy()
    temp.sort()
    for i in range(len(arr)):
        if arr[i] != temp[i]:
            swaps[i] = 1

    if sum(swaps) > 6:
        return False

    for i in range(0, len(arr), 2):
        if arr[i] % 2 != 0:
            for j in range(1, len(arr), 2):
               if arr[j] % 2 == 0:
                   arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]
                   break

    odd_count = len([x for x in arr if x < arr[0]])
    if odd_count % 2 == 0:
        for i in range(1, len(arr)):
            if arr[i] > arr[0]:
                arr[0], arr[i] = arr[i], arr[0]
                break

    return arr == sorted(arr)
```

This code first checks if the array can be sorted with at most three swaps (covering first condition). Then it ensures that all even elements are located at even position (covering third condition). Next, it moves a larger element to the front if the count of smaller elements is not odd (covering second condition). At last it checks if the final array is non-decreasing in order (covering fourth condition). Note that the code does these steps in an order to lower the swaps needed to meet all conditions.

Please also note that it might not be always possible to meet all these conditions which is why the code returns False in such cases. For example, if there are less than 4 elements and all are even, then third condition cannot be met.

First, ensure that the XML::Parser perl module is installed properly. If it isn't installed, you can install it using `cpan XML::Parser` command in the terminal.

Here is a simple example of how you can use the Objects style with XML::Parser. XML::Parser::Style::Objects makes every XML element an object. The objects will have a `Tag`, `Kids (children)` and `Text` and so on.

To answer your question, first let's create a simple XML file named `foo.xml`:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<root>
    <normal>This is a normal node</normal>
    <special>This is a special node</special>
</root>
```

Now, let's create the Perl script:

```perl
use XML::Parser;

package MyNode;

sub start {
    my ($self, $elem, %attr) = @_;
    print "Start of $elem\n";
}

sub end   {
    my ($self, $elem) = @_;
    print "End of $elem\n";
}

sub char {
    my ($self, $text) = @_;
    print "$text\n";
}

sub special::start {
    my ($self, $elem, %attr) = @_;
    print "Start of special $elem\n";
}

sub special::end {
    my ($self, $elem) = @_;
    print "End of special $elem\n";
}

sub special::char {
    my ($self, $text) = @_;
    print "Special: $text\n";
}

package main;

my $p = XML::Parser->new(Style => 'Objects', Pkg => 'MyNode');
my $tree = $p->parsefile('foo.xml');
```

When you execute this script, it should print:

```
Start of root
Start of normal
This is a normal node
End of normal
Start of special
Special: This is a special node
End of special
End of root
```

In this example, the Start and End method in the `special` package are called for the `special` node, while the text is processed by the `special::char` method. 

Please note that the methods `start`, `end` and `char` are reminiscent of the Stream style. Also, object construction and destruction is left for the user to implement. The XML::Parser only calls the appropriate methods.

