Consider $\omega(n)$ as a representation of the quantity of unique prime factors of a positive integer $n$.&lt;br&gt;
For instance, $\omega(1) = 0$ and  $\omega(360) = \omega(2^{3} \times 3^{2} \times 5) = 3$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Now, let's define $S(n)$ as $ \sum_{d \mid n} 2^{\omega(d)}  $.
&lt;br&gt;
As an example, $S(6) = 2^{\omega(1)}+2^{\omega(2)}+2^{\omega(3)}+2^{\omega(6)} = 2^0+2^1+2^1+2^2 = 9$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Next, let's introduce $F(n)=\sum_{i=2}^n S(i!)$.
We know that $F(10)=4821.$
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Your task is to compute $F(10\,000\,000)$. Please provide your solution modulo  $1\,000\,000\,087$.
&lt;/p&gt;

